// Disputed questions: angels and demons (ST I qq.50–64, 106–114)
DQ.push(
{id:"angels-exist",tract:"angels",level:1,note:"De fide (Lateran IV, 1215; Vatican I)",st:"ST I q.50 a.1",
 q:"Do angels exist, and can reason show that they should?",
 one:"Angels exist: the Church teaches it as part of the faith; reason cannot prove it strictly, but it can show that a universe with purely spiritual creatures is more fitting than one without.",
 plain:`<p>Faith comes first here. The Fourth Lateran Council (1215) professed that God 'from the beginning of time made at once out of nothing both orders of creatures, the spiritual and the corporeal, that is, the angelic and the earthly, and then the human creature, who as it were shares in both orders, being composed of spirit and body' (as quoted in CCC 327). Vatican I repeated this (Dei Filius ch. 1). The Catechism calls the existence of angels 'a truth of faith' (CCC 328).</p>
<p>Thomas then shows why it makes sense. God makes things to share his goodness, and the most perfect way an effect can resemble its cause is to act as the cause acts. God creates by intellect and will. So a complete universe should contain creatures that know and love, and among them some that are pure intellect, without a body: 'the perfection of the universe requires the existence of an incorporeal creature' (I q.50 a.1). Humans are spirit-in-a-body, the lowest of intellectual beings; it fits that there should be beings above us, as there are beings below us.</p>`,
 analogy:["The staircase with a missing step","Creation runs from bare matter, through plants and animals, to man, who is body and spirit. Then there is God, pure spirit without limit. A staircase that went from man straight to God would skip every step between. Angels fill that gap: finite, created spirits."],
 art:{obj:["It seems that there are no incorporeal creatures, for everything created is limited, and whatever is limited is in a place; but what is in a place is a body.","Further, Scripture's angels eat, walk and are seen. So they are bodies.","Further, the ancients thought nothing existed except bodies."],
  sc:"It is written: 'Who makest thy angels spirits' (Ps 103 [104]:4).",
  resp:"God's principal aim in creating is that creatures should resemble him, and the highest resemblance is for the effect to act as the cause acts. God produces things by intellect and will, so the perfection of the universe requires intellectual creatures. But understanding is not the act of a body, since 'every body is limited to \"here\" and \"now\"' (I q.50 a.1). Therefore the perfection of the universe requires incorporeal creatures.",
  rep:["To be limited is not the same as to be in a place by dimensions. An angel is limited by its essence, which receives existence in a finite measure, not by bulk.","The bodies in which angels appear are assumed, not their own (see the question on angelic bodies).","The ancients failed 'to make a proper distinction between sense and intellect' (I q.50 a.1)."]},
 deeper:`<p><strong>What reason can and cannot do.</strong> Thomas's argument is an argument from fittingness (convenientia), not a demonstration. Reason can show that pure created spirits are possible and that their existence suits the order of the universe; that they actually exist we know from revelation. This is why the existence of angels is a dogma of faith, not a conclusion of metaphysics.</p>
<p><strong>The scale of being.</strong> Thomas's universe is graded: in each kind of thing, higher natures touch the lowest point of the next kind up. The human soul is the lowest of intellects, so in need of a body and senses to learn; the angel is intellect without that need. 'The order of the universe' is the key phrase: angels are not decorations but a necessary part of a creation that fully reflects God.</p>
<p><strong>Why the Church insists.</strong> Denying angels makes nonsense of Scripture from Genesis to Revelation, of Christ's own words (Mt 18:10; 26:53), and of the fall of the devil, which is part of the account of sin. Liberal theology that reduced angels to literary devices was rejected by Pius XII (Humani Generis 26, rejecting doubt whether angels are personal beings).</p>`,
 authors:[["Christian B. Wagner","thomism.com: 'On the Number of Angels'; 'Demons as a Source of Theology'","Gathers the Fathers' reading of the ninety-nine sheep as the unfallen angels and argues that angelology is not peripheral: the Church's teaching on the devil and on spiritual warfare depends on it."],["Réginald Garrigou-Lagrange","The Trinity and God the Creator (commentary on ST I qq.27–119); Reality: A Synthesis of Thomistic Thought","Treats angels as the summit of creation's order and the clearest case of the real distinction between essence and existence: even pure spirits are not their own being."],["Edward Feser","Scholastic Metaphysics (2014); Immortal Souls (2024)","Explains the metaphysics angelology presupposes: act and potency, essence and existence, and why intellect cannot be a bodily power."]],
 errors:[["Angels are just metaphors for God's action.","Rejected by Lateran IV and Humani Generis; Christ speaks of them as persons."],["Angels are dead humans who 'earned their wings'.","Angels and humans are different natures; the saints become 'like the angels' (Mt 22:30) in glory, not angels."],["Angels can be prayed to by names revealed in private devotions.","The Church allows only the names in Scripture: Michael, Gabriel, Raphael (Directory on Popular Piety and the Liturgy, 2001, n. 217)."]],
 scripture:[["Ps 103 [104]:4","he makes his angels spirits"],["Mt 18:10","their angels always see the face of my Father"],["Heb 1:14","ministering spirits sent to serve"],["Col 1:16","thrones, dominations, principalities, powers"]],
 tests:[["Is the existence of angels proved by reason or held by faith?","Held by faith (Lateran IV); reason shows it is fitting, not that it is necessary."],["Why must intellect be immaterial?","Because a body is limited to 'here and now', while understanding grasps the universal, which is not tied to any here or now."]],
 read:["ST I q.50 a.1","CCC 328–336","<a href=\"#lesson-t5\">Academy T5: Creation and angels</a>"]},

{id:"angels-matter",tract:"angels",level:2,note:"Thomistic thesis (sententia communior); not defined",st:"ST I q.50 a.2",
 q:"Are angels completely without matter, or do they have a 'spiritual matter'?",
 one:"Thomas holds that an angel is a pure form with no matter at all; what makes it a creature rather than God is not matter but the distinction between its essence and its act of existing.",
 plain:`<p>Some medieval thinkers, following the Jewish philosopher Avicebron and later St Bonaventure, thought every creature must have some 'matter', even angels, because matter is what makes a thing potential and limited, and only God is pure act. Thomas disagrees. Matter in the strict sense is the potential to be a body; a spirit has none. But angels are still limited and potential in another way: their <em>essence</em> is not their <em>existence</em>. An angel is a form that receives being from God. That composition (essence + act of existing) is enough to separate every creature from God, who alone is his own being.</p>`,
 analogy:["Water poured into different cups","Existence is like water; essence is like the cup that receives it. God is not a cup at all: he is the ocean, being itself. Angels are cups of different sizes, holding existence in the measure their nature allows. You do not need clay (matter) to make a cup in this sense; the limit is the nature itself."],
 art:{obj:["Whatever is not pure act has potency; but potency is matter. So angels, which are not pure act, have matter.","Further, angels are individual and distinct; but matter is the principle of individuation.","Further, angels are limited, and form is limited only by matter."],
  sc:"Dionysius calls the angels 'incorporeal and immaterial'.",
  resp:"Avicebron held a 'universal matter of spiritual and corporeal things', but 'there cannot be one matter of spiritual and of corporeal things' (I q.50 a.2), for spiritual and corporeal forms cannot be received into the same matter. Moreover an intellectual substance knows by receiving forms without matter, universally; a thing with matter receives forms individually. Hence 'the intellectual substance is altogether immaterial'. Yet the angel is not pure act: in it there is a composition of form and existence (esse), as of potency and act.",
  rep:["Not every potency is matter. In angels the potency is the essence itself, in relation to the existence it receives.","Angels are individual by their form: each is its own species (see the next question).","A form is limited not only by matter but by being received; the angel's form is a finite participation in being."]},
 deeper:`<p><strong>Why this matters.</strong> The debate is a test case for the <em>real distinction</em> between essence and existence, the centrepiece of Thomas's metaphysics (De ente et essentia ch. 4). If angels need matter to be creatures, essence-existence composition is not doing the work; if they do not, it is. Thomas's position makes the distinction between God and creatures turn on being itself: 'God alone is his own being; in every other thing, the thing is one, its being another'.</p>
<p><strong>Two kinds of composition.</strong> Bodies have two compositions: matter and form; and essence (the matter-form whole) and existence. Angels have only the second. God has neither. This yields a three-level metaphysics: material substances, separate substances, and subsistent Being itself.</p>`,
 schools:`<p><strong>Franciscan school.</strong> Bonaventure and many early Franciscans held 'universal hylomorphism': angels have spiritual matter. Scotus rejected spiritual matter but also denied Thomas's real distinction between essence and existence, holding instead a 'formal' distinction. Suárez likewise denied the real distinction. The Thomists (Cajetan, John of St Thomas, Garrigou-Lagrange) defend it as the key to Thomas. The 24 Thomistic Theses approved in 1914 include the real distinction (thesis 3) and the angels' immateriality.</p>`,
 authors:[["Réginald Garrigou-Lagrange","Reality, chs on the real distinction; the 24 Thomistic Theses","Calls the real distinction between essence and existence 'the fundamental truth of Christian philosophy', which alone explains why angels are creatures without matter."],["Edward Feser","Scholastic Metaphysics ch. 4 (essence and existence)","Sets out the arguments for the real distinction in modern terms and answers analytic objections."],["Christian B. Wagner","Annotated Thomist: 'Whether God is the Same as his Essence or Nature'; 'Whether God is Contained in a Genus'","Works through the texts on God's simplicity that form the other side of the same doctrine: only in God are essence and existence identical."]],
 errors:[["Spirit is just very fine matter.","A spirit has no parts outside parts; 'fine matter' is still matter."],["If angels have no matter they are infinite like God.","Their essence limits their being; only God is unlimited being."]],
 tests:[["What composition is in every creature, including angels?","Essence and existence (potency and act)."],["Who held that angels have 'spiritual matter'?","Avicebron and Bonaventure (universal hylomorphism)."]],
 read:["ST I q.50 a.2","Thomas, De ente et essentia ch. 4","<a href=\"#lesson-f4\">Academy F4: essence and existence</a>"]},

{id:"angels-species",tract:"angels",level:3,note:"Thomistic opinion; disputed by Scotists",st:"ST I q.50 a.4",
 q:"Is every angel its own species?",
 one:"Yes, for Thomas: two things of the same species differ only by matter, and angels have no matter, so each angel differs from every other as a lion differs from an oak, not as one lion from another.",
 plain:`<p>Why are there many dogs? Because the same dog-nature is received in different chunks of matter. Matter is what lets one form be 'copied'. Take matter away and the form cannot be copied: there could be only one of it. Since angels have no matter, there cannot be two angels of the same kind. Each angel is a unique species, the only member of its kind. Thomas: 'it is impossible for two angels to be of one species; just as it would be impossible for there to be several whitenesses apart, or several humanities' (I q.50 a.4).</p>
<p>So the angelic world is not a crowd of identical beings but a vast hierarchy in which every member is a distinct way of reflecting God. Michael differs from Gabriel more than a human differs from a horse.</p>`,
 analogy:["The perfect colour","There is one 'redness' in itself; many red things exist only because redness is in many surfaces. If redness existed on its own, with no surfaces, there could be only one. Each angel is like such a colour existing on its own."],
 art:{obj:["Things alike in the noblest respect are alike in species; all angels share rational nature, the noblest. So they are one species.","Further, more and less do not change species; angels differ only by more and less perfection.","Further, the human soul and the angel are both intellectual; so souls too would each be a species, which is false."],
  sc:"In things of one species there is no 'first' and 'second'; but among angels there are first, middle and last (Dionysius).",
  resp:"Things that agree in species but differ in number agree in form and are distinguished materially. Angels are not composed of matter and form; therefore 'it is impossible for two angels to be of one species' (I q.50 a.4). Even if they had matter, matter would distinguish them by diversity of powers, which also differs in species.",
  rep:["Rational is not their ultimate specific difference; it is the genus. The angels differ by the degree of intellect.","More and less from different form-degrees do make different species, as numbers differ.","The human soul is the form of a body; souls are multiplied by their relation to different bodies and remain one species."]},
 deeper:`<p><strong>The principle of individuation.</strong> For Thomas, what individuates material things is 'matter signed with quantity' (materia signata quantitate). This is why the same humanity can exist in billions of people. Angels, having no matter, are individuated by their very form. The consequence is striking: there are as many angelic species as angels.</p>
<p><strong>What about separated souls?</strong> Souls after death have no matter, yet many souls are one species. Thomas answers that each soul keeps its individuality from its relation to the body it formed and will form again at the resurrection. A soul is 'made for' its body; an angel is not.</p>
<p><strong>Consequences.</strong> No two angels have the same nature, so each has its own natural knowledge and its own natural perfection. The angelic orders (seraphim, cherubim and the rest) are groups of many species, not species themselves.</p>`,
 schools:`<p>The Paris condemnations of 1277 included a proposition that God cannot make several intelligences of one species without matter (prop. 81 in Mandonnet's numbering), which some took to touch Thomas. Scotus denied the thesis, holding that individuation comes from a positive 'thisness' (haecceitas) and that God could make many angels of one species. The Thomists hold Thomas's view as a direct consequence of form being individuated by matter. The Church has not settled it.</p>`,
 authors:[["Edward Feser","Scholastic Metaphysics, on individuation","Defends matter as principle of individuation for material substances, which is the premise of Thomas's argument here."],["Garrigou-Lagrange","The Trinity and God the Creator, on q.50","Defends the Thomist thesis against Scotus and Suárez as following necessarily from the immateriality of angels."]],
 errors:[["All angels are basically the same kind of being with different jobs.","For Thomas each is a different nature; the 'jobs' follow from the nature."]],
 tests:[["What individuates material things, for Thomas?","Matter marked by quantity (materia signata)."],["Why are souls not each a species?","Because each is the form of a body and keeps its relation to that body."]],
 read:["ST I q.50 a.4","Thomas, De ente et essentia ch. 4"]},

{id:"angels-bodies",tract:"angels",level:2,note:"Theological conclusion (common teaching)",st:"ST I q.51 aa.1–3",
 q:"How can an angel appear in a body and still be purely spiritual?",
 one:"An angel never has a body as its own: it forms a visible likeness of a body by God's power and moves it the way a puppeteer moves a figure, using it as a sign of its spiritual powers, while remaining pure spirit.",
 plain:`<p>Scripture shows angels being seen by many people at once: three visitors eat with Abraham (Gen 18), angels are seen by the men of Sodom (Gen 19), Raphael travels with Tobias (Tobit 5–12). These were not only visions in someone's imagination, because many saw them. So the angels must have used real, visible bodies. But angels are pure spirits. How?</p>
<p>Thomas distinguishes two ways of being united to a body. A <strong>soul</strong> is united to its body as its <em>form</em>: it makes that body alive and human; together they are one being. An <strong>angel</strong> is united to an assumed body only as its <em>mover</em>: 'The body assumed is united to the angel not as its form, nor merely as its mover, but as its mover represented by the assumed movable body' (I q.51 a.2 ad 2). The body is not alive, does not sense, and is not part of the angel. The angel shapes it, by God's power, out of the elements (Thomas follows the science of his day and says out of condensed air), and moves it so that its eyes, hands and voice <em>represent</em> the angel's knowledge, power and message.</p>
<p>So the angel does not become bodily. The body is a kind of living icon the angel uses; the angel stays wholly spiritual.</p>`,
 analogy:["The puppeteer and the marionette","A skilled puppeteer can make a marionette walk, bow and 'speak' so convincingly that children think it alive. The puppet is not alive and is not the puppeteer; the puppeteer is not made of wood. The puppet's movements express the puppeteer's intentions. An assumed angelic body is like this, but fashioned and moved with a perfection no human art can match. (Unlike a puppeteer, the angel is not 'inside' or 'above' the body by contact of size: it is present by the application of its power.)"],
 art:{obj:["Angels need no body, since they know without senses. So they never assume bodies.","Further, if an angel assumed a body, it would be united to it either as form or as mover; not as form, since then the angel would be a soul; not as mover, since then it would not need to 'assume' it.","Further, angels' bodies would be made of earth, water, fire or air; but none of these can take a lasting visible shape.","Further, the angels in Scripture eat (Gen 18:8); but eating belongs to living bodies. So either they had living bodies, or Scripture is false."],
  sc:"Augustine says that angels appeared to Abraham under the form of men (De Civ. Dei XVI.29); and the angel who appeared to Tobias was seen by all.",
  resp:"Some said angels never assume bodies and that all apparitions were in imagination. 'But this is contrary to the intent of Scripture' (I q.51 a.2), which shows angels seen by many at once: by Abraham and his household, by Lot and the men of Sodom, by all present with Tobias. Such things are seen by bodily vision, and only a body can be seen bodily. Since angels are not bodies and have no bodies naturally united to them, 'it follows that they sometimes assume bodies'.",
  rep:["'Angels need an assumed body, not for themselves, but on our account; that by conversing familiarly with men they may give evidence of that intellectual companionship which men expect to have with them in the life to come' (I q.51 a.2 ad 1). Thomas adds that the Old Testament apparitions were a figure of the Word who would take a real body.","The assumed body is united to the angel 'as its mover represented by the assumed movable body': the angel fashions sensible features to represent its intelligible properties, as Scripture uses sensible images to represent spiritual truths.","Thomas, following the physics of his time, says angels condense air into shape by God's power. The principle does not depend on this physics: the angel produces a perceptible body by acting on matter, which it can move locally.","They do not truly eat. The food 'was neither changed into the assumed body', so 'it was not a true eating, but figurative of spiritual eating'; Raphael says, 'I seemed indeed to eat and to drink; but I use an invisible meat and drink' (Tob 12:19; I q.51 a.3 ad 5)."]},
 deeper:`<p><strong>What the assumed body can and cannot do.</strong> In the next article Thomas asks whether angels exercise functions of life in assumed bodies. His answer: only in what life-functions share with non-living motion. An angel can produce sounds like a voice and move limbs as in walking, because sound and motion can be produced without life. But it cannot truly sense, digest, or speak in the strict sense, because 'nothing can have a function of life except what has life' (I q.51 a.3). The eyes of an assumed body do not see; they signify the angel's knowledge: 'by the eye the power of the angel's knowledge is pointed out' (I q.51 a.3 ad 2).</p>
<p><strong>Is this deception?</strong> No. Thomas compares it to Scripture's use of images: it is 'in no wise contrary to truth for intelligible things to be set forth in Scripture under sensible figures' (I q.51 a.3 ad 1). The angel does not claim to be a man; the appearance is a sign. The angel uses a likeness of a man rather than a real man, because a real man would point to man, not to the angel.</p>
<p><strong>Contrast with the Incarnation.</strong> This is the decisive difference between angelic apparitions and Christ. The angel's body is a sign it moves; Christ's humanity is truly his own nature, united to the Word in one person (the hypostatic union). Christ truly ate after the resurrection (Lk 24:43): Thomas notes 'Christ had a body of such a true nature that food could be changed into it; hence it was a true eating' (I q.51 a.3 ad 5). The heresy of Docetism, that Christ only seemed to have a body, makes Christ an angel-like apparition. The Church rejects it absolutely (1 Jn 4:2–3).</p>
<p><strong>Contrast with the human soul.</strong> A human soul is naturally the form of a body: it needs the body to sense and to learn, and without it is incomplete. An angel is complete without any body: 'to have a body united to it is not of the nature of an intellectual substance, as such' (I q.51 a.1). This is why death is unnatural for humans but bodilessness is natural for angels.</p>
<p><strong>Where is the angel when it appears?</strong> Spirits are not in place by bulk but 'by application of the angelic power' to a place (I q.52 a.1). The angel is where it acts; it is in the assumed body as a mover is in what it moves, and moves 'accidentally' when that body moves (I q.51 a.3 ad 3).</p>`,
 schools:`<p>Some theologians (e.g. some later Scotists and Suárez) allowed that angels might use already existing bodies, or bodies of other elements, rather than condensed air. Modern Thomists note that Thomas's physics of 'condensed air' is dated but that his metaphysical point is unaffected: whatever matter the angel uses, it is related to it as mover and sign-maker, never as form. Some modern authors suggest many apparitions could also be 'imaginative' visions caused in the beholder; Thomas grants that some are, but insists that those seen by many are bodily.</p>`,
 authors:[["Christian B. Wagner","christianbwagner.com: 'Can Demons Work True Miracles ad mentem Divi Thomae?'; thomism.com: 'On the Number of Angels'","Explains, from Thomas, what angelic power over bodies can and cannot achieve: local motion and appearance, not true miracles or life; the same principles govern apparitions."],["Garrigou-Lagrange","The Trinity and God the Creator, on q.51","Stresses that assumed bodies show the condescension of God, who adapts spiritual realities to our senses, and prefigure the Incarnation."],["Edward Feser","Immortal Souls (2024)","Sets out the hylomorphic account of the human soul as the form of the body, which is the necessary contrast: the angel is never a form of matter."]],
 errors:[["Angels have 'light bodies' or ethereal bodies of their own.","Some Fathers thought so (e.g. Origen, some Platonists); Thomas and the later tradition reject it as against their spirituality (I q.51 a.1)."],["When an angel appears, it becomes temporarily human.","No union of nature takes place; only Christ became man."],["Genesis 6 shows angels having children with women.","Thomas, with Augustine, reads 'sons of God' as the line of Seth (I q.51 a.3 ad 6). Spirits cannot generate, since generation is a life-function."]],
 scripture:[["Gen 18–19","the three visitors; angels at Sodom, seen by many"],["Tob 12:19","'I seemed indeed to eat and to drink'"],["Lk 24:39–43","the risen Christ: 'a spirit hath not flesh and bones', and he truly eats"],["Heb 13:2","some have entertained angels unawares"]],
 tests:[["How is an assumed body united to the angel?","As to its mover, represented by the movable body; not as form."],["Did the angels truly eat with Abraham?","No: the food was not changed into their substance; it was 'figurative of spiritual eating'."],["Why does this matter for Christology?","It shows the difference between an apparition (a sign) and the Incarnation (a true human nature)."]],
 read:["ST I q.51 aa.1–3; q.52 a.1","Augustine, City of God XVI.29","<a href=\"#dq-angels-place\">Where is an angel?</a>"]},

{id:"angels-place",tract:"angels",level:2,note:"Theological conclusion",st:"ST I qq.52–53",
 q:"Where is an angel, and how does it move?",
 one:"An angel is 'in' a place only where it acts, by applying its power there; it is never everywhere, never in two places at once, and it can pass from place to place without crossing the space between.",
 plain:`<p>Bodies are in a place by contact: their surfaces touch the surfaces around them. Angels have no surfaces. So 'an angel is said to be in a corporeal place by application of the angelic power in any manner whatever to any place' (I q.52 a.1). It is where it acts, whole wherever it is.</p>
<p>Because its power is finite, it acts on one determined place at a time: 'he is not everywhere, nor in several places, but in only one place' (I q.52 a.2). Only God, whose power is infinite, is everywhere. Thomas also holds that two angels cannot be in exactly the same place, since 'it is impossible for two complete causes to be the causes immediately of one and the same thing' (I q.52 a.3).</p>
<p>As for motion, an angel can move continuously through intervening places if it wills, or apply its power first here and then there, 'jumping' without passing through the middle. Its change of place still takes a moment of angelic time, not zero time.</p>`,
 analogy:["Attention","Think of where your attention is. It is not spread across the room like paint; it is wherever you direct it. You can switch it from the window to the door without 'travelling' over the floor. An angel's presence is something like an act of attention with power: it is wherever it acts."],
 art:{obj:["To be in a place belongs to bodies; angels are not bodies; therefore they are in no place.","Further, if angels are in place, they are in several places at once, since one angel moves the whole heaven.","Further, many angels can be in one place, as many demons in one possessed man ('My name is Legion', Mk 5:9)."],
  sc:"The Gloss says that 'every creature is circumscribed by place'.",
  resp:"An angel is in a place, but differently from a body. A body is in place 'according to the contact of dimensive quantity'; 'but there is no such quantity in the angels, for theirs is a virtual one' (I q.52 a.1). An angel is in place by the application of its power, and 'not everywhere, nor in several places, but in only one place' (I q.52 a.2), because its power is finite.",
  rep:["Angels are in place not as contained but as containing: they act on the place rather than being enclosed.","What an angel acts on as one is 'one place' for it, however large.","The many demons in the Gerasene were not in the same place as one complete cause; they afflicted the man, each acting in its own way."]},
 deeper:`<p><strong>Definitive presence.</strong> Scholastics distinguish three ways of being present: <em>circumscriptive</em> (bodies, measured by their place), <em>definitive</em> (spirits and souls: here and not elsewhere, but not measured) and <em>repletive</em> (God, everywhere by essence, presence and power, I q.8 a.3). The Eucharist introduces a fourth, sacramental presence, which is unique (III q.76 a.5).</p>
<p><strong>Angelic motion and time.</strong> Thomas denies that angelic motion is strictly instantaneous in the sense of taking no time at all; it takes place in angelic time, measured by the succession of the angel's acts. Angels exist in aeviternity, not time, but their operations succeed one another (see the question on angelic time).</p>
<p><strong>Practical consequence.</strong> Demons and angels are not omnipresent. Satan cannot tempt everyone at once; most temptation comes from 'the world, the flesh and the devil' together, and much from the first two.</p>`,
 errors:[["The devil is everywhere and knows everything.","Only God is omnipresent and omniscient; the devil is a finite creature."]],
 tests:[["How is an angel in a place?","By applying its power to it (definitive presence), not by contact of size."],["Can an angel be in two places at once?","No; its power is finite and applied to one place at a time."]],
 read:["ST I q.52 aa.1–3; q.53"]},

{id:"angels-know",tract:"angels",level:2,note:"Theological conclusions; some points defined (only God knows hearts)",st:"ST I qq.54–58",
 q:"How do angels know, and what can they not know?",
 one:"Angels know by ideas infused at their creation, intuitively and without reasoning step by step; they know natures and their effects perfectly, but they cannot know with certainty future free choices or the secret thoughts of hearts, which belong to God alone.",
 plain:`<p>We learn by abstracting ideas from sense experience and by reasoning from one point to another. Angels do neither. 'The species whereby the angels understand are not drawn from things, but are connatural to them' (I q.55 a.2): God gave them their ideas with their nature. And they do not reason discursively; they see conclusions in their principles at a glance (I q.58 a.3). Higher angels know more through fewer, more universal ideas, the way a great teacher grasps a whole field from one principle that a student has to work out piece by piece (I q.55 a.3).</p>
<p>Limits: an angel knows future events in their causes, so it can predict what follows necessarily or usually, and much better than we can, but it cannot know free future choices with certainty (I q.57 a.3). Nor can it read the secrets of our hearts: 'it is proper to God to read the secrets of hearts' (I q.57 a.4), as Jeremiah says: 'who can know it? I am the Lord, who search the heart' (Jer 17:9–10). Angels can, however, infer our thoughts from bodily signs, as a perceptive person reads a face, and far better.</p>`,
 analogy:["The chess grandmaster and the pupil","A beginner works out moves one at a time. A grandmaster sees the whole position and its consequences at a glance. Angels are like infinitely better grandmasters of reality. Yet even the best grandmaster cannot know for certain what move a free opponent will choose; he can only predict."],
 art:{obj:["Angels know all things, since they are 'full of eyes' (Ezek 1:18). So they know future events and our thoughts.","Further, the devil tempts each person to the sin he is most prone to; so he knows our thoughts.","Further, angels learn by experience, since the angels 'desire to look into' the Gospel (1 Pet 1:12)."],
  sc:"What is proper to God does not belong to angels; 'it is proper to God to read the secrets of hearts' (I q.57 a.4).",
  resp:"Angels know future things in their causes: what proceeds necessarily, with certainty; what proceeds usually, by conjecture; and in this 'by so much the more than it does in us, as they understand the causes of things both more universally and more perfectly' (I q.57 a.3). But free future acts and the will's secret inclinations are known only to God, who alone is present to all time and moves the will from within.",
  rep:["Their knowledge is vast but finite; 'full of eyes' signifies its breadth, not omniscience.","The devil infers our dispositions from our behaviour, temperament and imagination, which he can observe and influence; he does not see into the will.","Angels can receive new knowledge by revelation from God and illumination from higher angels; the mysteries of grace exceed their natural knowledge."]},
 deeper:`<p><strong>Morning and evening knowledge.</strong> Following Augustine, Thomas distinguishes the angels' 'morning knowledge' (knowing creatures in the Word, in God) from their 'evening knowledge' (knowing creatures in their own natures) (I q.58 a.6). Augustine even reads the six days of Genesis in these terms.</p>
<p><strong>Natural and supernatural knowledge.</strong> By nature angels know God only as reflected in their own essence, an image of him. To see God's essence requires the light of glory, a gift of grace (I q.12 a.5; q.56 a.3). This is why angels too needed grace to reach beatitude, and why some fell.</p>
<p><strong>No error in their natural knowledge.</strong> Since they do not reason discursively, angels cannot be mistaken about natures they understand. Error enters only when the will turns away from the supernatural order and judges about it by natural lights alone, which is how the demons err about matters of grace (I q.58 a.5).</p>`,
 authors:[["Christian B. Wagner","christianbwagner.com: 'How Do the Saints Hear Us?'","Uses the same Thomistic principles about created intellects seeing in God to explain how the saints know our prayers: not by natural powers but by what God shows them in the beatific vision."],["Garrigou-Lagrange","Reality; The Trinity and God the Creator","Uses angelic knowledge to illustrate the hierarchy of intellects and the principle that higher minds know by fewer, more universal ideas."]],
 errors:[["The devil can read my mind.","He cannot see the will or the intellect directly; he guesses from signs. This is a comfort against scrupulosity and fear."],["Fortune-tellers with spirit help can know the future.","Free future acts are known only by God; spirits can only conjecture (and demons deceive). Divination is forbidden (CCC 2116)."]],
 scripture:[["Jer 17:9–10","God alone searches the heart"],["1 Kgs 8:39","'thou only knowest the heart of all the children of men'"],["Mk 13:32","the angels do not know the day or the hour"],["1 Pet 1:12","angels desire to look into the Gospel"]],
 tests:[["Where do angels' ideas come from?","They are infused (connatural) at creation, not abstracted from things."],["Can an angel know my secret thoughts with certainty?","No; it can only infer them from signs."]],
 read:["ST I qq.54–58","CCC 2115–2117 (divination)"]},

{id:"angels-grace",tract:"angels",level:3,note:"Created in grace: more probable opinion (Thomas); trial of angels: common teaching",st:"ST I q.62",
 q:"Were the angels created in grace, and how were they tested?",
 one:"Thomas holds it 'more probable' that God created the angels in sanctifying grace; each was then tested, and by a single free act of charity the good angels merited beatitude immediately, while the others refused it.",
 plain:`<p>Angels, like us, were made for a supernatural end: to see God face to face. That end is beyond any creature's natural powers, so it needs grace. Thomas thinks it 'more probable, and more in keeping with the sayings of holy men, that they were created in sanctifying grace' (I q.62 a.3), as Augustine taught. They were not created already in glory, since then none could have sinned.</p>
<p>Because angels act all at once rather than gradually, their trial was short: 'The angel was beatified instantly after the first act of charity, whereby he merited beatitude' (I q.62 a.5). Grace perfects nature according to its mode, and angels reach their natural perfection all at once, not step by step like us.</p>`,
 analogy:["The single decisive answer","A human life is like a long conversation in which we can change our minds many times. An angel's decision is like a single answer given with total clarity and full knowledge: there is nothing more to learn that would make it think again."],
 art:{obj:["Grace comes after nature; so angels were first created in nature, then given grace.","Further, if created in grace they could not have sinned.","Further, merit needs time; so angels could not merit beatitude by one act."],
  sc:"Augustine: God was 'at the same time fashioning their nature and bestowing grace' on them (De Civ. Dei XII.9).",
  resp:"Although opinions conflict, 'it seems more probable, and more in keeping with the sayings of holy men, that they were created in sanctifying grace' (I q.62 a.3). Grace stands to beatitude as a seed to its effect, and God gave all things their 'seeds' at their first making. Further, 'the angel was beatified instantly after the first act of charity, whereby he merited beatitude' (I q.62 a.5), because grace perfects nature according to the nature's mode.",
  rep:["Grace is prior to beatitude, not necessarily later in time than nature.","Grace, unlike glory, does not confirm the will; it can be lost.","Merit requires a free act, not a long time; the angel's single act was fully free and fully informed."]},
 deeper:`<p><strong>The structure of the angelic trial.</strong> Thomas holds there were at least three moments: creation (in grace), the act of choice (merit or sin), and the reception of glory or damnation. The 'instants' are moments of angelic duration, not of our time.</p>
<p><strong>Degrees.</strong> Grace and glory were given to angels in proportion to their natural gifts: higher angels received more (I q.62 a.6). This is not unfair: it follows from their more perfect natures turning more wholly to God.</p>
<p><strong>The lesson for us.</strong> The angels show what the human pilgrimage is for: the free acceptance of God's gift. We get a lifetime; they had one act. Death fixes the human will as the first choice fixed theirs (see 'Why is the fall of the angels irrevocable?').</p>`,
 schools:`<p>Peter Lombard and some early scholastics held that the angels were created in a purely natural state and received grace later. Thomas leans toward creation in grace. Scotus discusses the question differently. The Church has not defined it; the manuals generally treat the angels' elevation to grace and their testing as common teaching.</p>`,
 tests:[["Why could angels merit beatitude by one act?","Because grace perfects nature according to its mode, and angels act with total, immediate clarity."]],
 read:["ST I q.62 aa.1–6"]},

{id:"angels-fall",tract:"angels",level:2,note:"Fall by free sin: de fide (Lateran IV); eternity of their punishment: de fide",st:"ST I qq.63–64",
 q:"What was the sin of the angels, and why can it never be forgiven?",
 one:"The devil's sin was pride: he wanted to be like God by his own power, seeking happiness from his own nature instead of receiving it as God's gift; and because an angel chooses with total, unchanging clarity, its will stays fixed in that choice, as a human will is fixed at death.",
 plain:`<p>The Fourth Lateran Council taught: 'The devil and the other demons were indeed created naturally good by God, but they became evil by their own doing' (CCC 391). Evil is not a rival god; it is a good creature's misuse of freedom.</p>
<p><strong>What sin?</strong> Angels have no body, so they cannot sin by sensual desires. They can sin only by pride or envy (I q.63 a.2). Thomas asks whether the devil desired 'to be as God', and answers yes, but not by equality, which an angel knows is impossible. He desired God-likeness wrongly: 'by desiring, as his last end of beatitude, something which he could attain by the virtue of his own nature, turning his appetite away from supernatural beatitude, which is attained by God's grace' (I q.63 a.3). In short: 'I will be happy on my own terms.' Envy of man followed.</p>
<p><strong>Why irrevocable?</strong> Not because the sin was too big for God's mercy. Thomas follows Damascene: 'death is to men, what the fall is to the angels' (I q.64 a.2). A human mind moves from one consideration to another and can reconsider; 'the angel's will adheres fixedly and immovably' (I q.64 a.2), because it apprehends with complete clarity all at once. There is nothing further it could learn that would make it choose differently. Its decision is its final self-definition, as death fixes ours.</p>`,
 analogy:["The marriage vow said with perfect knowledge","Imagine making a vow knowing absolutely everything relevant, with no fatigue, emotion, illusion or pressure. Such a choice would not be revisited: there would be no new reason to revisit it. The angels' choice was like that."],
 art:{obj:["Every sin can be forgiven, since God's mercy is infinite. So the demons' sin can be forgiven.","Further, free will can always turn to its opposite; demons have free will; so they can repent.","Further, Origen held that all spirits will return to God."],
  sc:"Christ says the wicked go 'into everlasting fire, which was prepared for the devil and his angels' (Mt 25:41).",
  resp:"Origen's view 'deprives angels and saints of true beatitude, because everlasting stability is of the very nature of true beatitude' and contradicts Scripture; 'according to Catholic Faith, it must be held firmly both that the will of the good angels is confirmed in good, and that the will of the demons is obstinate in evil' (I q.64 a.2). The cause is not the gravity of the sin but the angel's mode of knowing: it 'apprehends immovably', so its will 'adheres fixedly and immovably'.",
  rep:["Mercy is offered to those who can turn; the demons will not turn, so they do not receive it. Their damnation is the eternal confirmation of their own choice.","The demons remain free in other acts, but not in their final orientation to their last end, which is fixed.","Origen's opinion on universal restoration was condemned (Synod of Constantinople, 543; cf. II Constantinople, 553)."]},
 deeper:`<p><strong>When did they fall?</strong> Not in the very first instant of creation (that would make God the author of their evil), but immediately after it (I q.63 aa.5–6). Isaiah 14:12 ('How art thou fallen… O Lucifer') and Ezekiel 28:13 are applied by the Fathers, in their spiritual sense, to the devil.</p>
<p><strong>Who fell?</strong> Thomas thinks the highest of the sinning angels was probably the highest of all angels (I q.63 a.7), and that 'more angels stood firm than sinned' (I q.63 a.9), since sin is against natural inclination.</p>
<p><strong>Why no redemption for angels?</strong> Hebrews 2:16: Christ 'nowhere taketh hold of the angels: but of the seed of Abraham he taketh hold'. Thomas gives the reason above: the angelic will is fixed. Redemption presupposes a will still able to turn.</p>
<p><strong>The demons' state now.</strong> They keep their natural knowledge, but their intellect is darkened about the things of grace, and they suffer the pain of loss. They are allowed to tempt humans until the end, within limits God sets, for our trial and merit.</p>`,
 schools:`<p>Scotus held that the first sin of the angels was a disordered love of their own excellence (a form of pride) rather than a desire to attain beatitude by nature. Suárez discussed whether the devil's sin concerned the Incarnation: the tradition (including Suárez, and the Spanish Jesuits) records an opinion that Lucifer refused to adore the future incarnate Word. Thomas does not hold this, but it is a pious and widely held view, not condemned.</p>`,
 authors:[["Christian B. Wagner","christianbwagner.com, Annotated Thomist on grace and merit; thomism.com: 'Demons as a Source of Theology'","Applies Thomas on the fixity of the will after the final choice to human death and judgment, and defends the eternity of hell against universalist readings."],["Garrigou-Lagrange","Life Everlasting (1947); The Trinity and God the Creator","Uses the angelic fall to explain why the soul's will is fixed at death: the separated soul, like the angel, knows without the shifting of discursive reason."],["Edward Feser","Blog essays on hell and the fixity of the will; Immortal Souls","Defends the Thomist explanation that the will is fixed at death because the separated soul knows in an angelic mode, making eternal punishment intelligible."]],
 errors:[["Satan is an evil god equal to God.","Dualism (Manicheism) is condemned: the devil is a creature, good by nature, evil by choice (Lateran IV)."],["In the end even the devil will be saved.","Origenist apokatastasis was condemned in 543/553; Christ speaks of 'everlasting fire' (Mt 25:41)."]],
 scripture:[["Isa 14:12","'How art thou fallen from heaven, O Lucifer'"],["Lk 10:18","'I saw Satan like lightning falling from heaven'"],["2 Pet 2:4; Jude 6","angels who sinned and kept not their principality"],["Rev 12:7–9","Michael and his angels fought against the dragon"],["Jn 8:44","the devil 'stood not in the truth'"]],
 tests:[["What was the devil's sin?","Pride: desiring God-likeness by his own power, rejecting supernatural beatitude as a gift."],["Why can't demons repent?","Because an angel's will adheres 'fixedly and immovably' to what it chooses, as a human will is fixed at death."]],
 read:["ST I qq.63–64","CCC 391–395"]},

{id:"angels-demons",tract:"angels",level:2,note:"Common teaching; Church discipline on exorcism",st:"ST I qq.110–111, 114",
 q:"What can demons really do, and what can they not do?",
 one:"Demons can tempt, deceive, act on our imagination and senses, move bodies and, rarely, possess a person's body; they cannot work true miracles, create, read our hearts, or force the will, which God alone moves from within.",
 plain:`<p>The Church takes the devil seriously (CCC 391–395, 2851–2854) without making him a rival to God. Thomas sets out precise limits:</p>
<ul><li><strong>No true miracles.</strong> 'A miracle properly so called is when something is done outside the order of nature'; 'God alone can work miracles' (I q.110 a.4). Demons can do marvels that exceed <em>our</em> powers and knowledge (I q.114 a.4), but always within created nature.</li>
<li><strong>No direct power over the will.</strong> 'The will can be changed in two ways': from within, which 'God alone' can do, and from without, by presenting something as good (I q.111 a.2). Angels and demons can only persuade.</li>
<li><strong>Power over imagination and senses.</strong> 'Both a good and a bad angel by their own natural power can move the human imagination' (I q.111 a.3): images, impressions, dreams. This is the ordinary channel of temptation.</li>
<li><strong>Temptation</strong> comes from the devil, the world and the flesh; not every temptation is directly diabolical (I q.114 a.3).</li></ul>`,
 analogy:["The salesman and the buyer","A salesman can show you products, flatter you, create a mood and pressure you, but he cannot reach inside you and make you choose. The decision is yours. The devil is a superhumanly skilled salesman; God alone can touch the will itself."],
 art:{obj:["Demons work wonders, like Pharaoh's magicians; so they work miracles.","Further, the devil 'entered into' Judas (Jn 13:27); so he moves the will.","Further, people say 'the devil made me do it'."],
  sc:"'God alone can work miracles' (I q.110 a.4); and 'God alone can thus change the will' from within (I q.111 a.2).",
  resp:"For a miracle 'it be against the order of the whole created nature. But God alone can do this' (I q.110 a.4). And the will is moved from within only by its Creator. Demons can move bodies locally, and through this can affect the imagination and senses, and so can propose evil under the appearance of good; but the consent remains the person's own.",
  rep:["The magicians' wonders were real natural effects produced by demonic skill, or illusions, not miracles strictly; they failed when God's power exceeded nature (Ex 8:18–19).","The devil 'entered' Judas by suggestion and by possessing his consent, which Judas freely gave.","The devil can incite but not compel; our responsibility remains, though passions and pressures can reduce it."]},
 deeper:`<p><strong>Possession, obsession, oppression.</strong> Traditional theology distinguishes ordinary temptation; <em>obsession</em> (external harassment); and <em>possession</em>, where the demon controls the body (never the soul's free will). The Church's Rite of Exorcism (1614, revised 1999) lists signs: speaking unknown languages, knowing hidden things, strength beyond nature, aversion to the sacred. Before solemn exorcism, illness must be excluded (CCC 1673; see P1 lesson 2).</p>
<p><strong>Genesis 6:1–4.</strong> Could demons father children? Thomas reports Augustine on 'incubi' and rejects the reading that angels married women before the Flood: 'by the sons of God are to be understood the sons of Seth, who were good; while by the daughters of men the Scripture designates those who sprang from the race of Cain' (I q.51 a.3 ad 6). Since angels have no life-functions, they cannot generate.</p>
<p><strong>Discernment.</strong> Because demons can imitate signs and stir imagination, the Church judges claimed apparitions and charisms by their fruits, conformity to faith and the sanity of those involved. Wagner's essay on non-Catholic miracles applies these principles: a true miracle, which only God works, cannot confirm a falsehood.</p>`,
 authors:[["Christian B. Wagner","christianbwagner.com: 'Can Demons Work True Miracles ad mentem Divi Thomae?'; thomism.com: 'On Non-Catholic Miracles'; 'How Miracles Refute Protestantism'","Argues from I q.110 a.4 that true miracles are God's seal and cannot confirm error, while demonic marvels can deceive; so miracles are a real criterion of the true Church."],["Garrigou-Lagrange","The Three Ages of the Interior Life","Treats diabolical temptation in the spiritual life, the discernment of spirits, and the devil's tactics with beginners and proficients."]],
 errors:[["Every bad thought is the devil.","Most temptation comes from concupiscence and the world (Jas 1:14); blaming the devil can avoid responsibility."],["The devil is a myth for evil.","Paul VI (General Audience, 15 Nov 1972) and CCC 391–395 affirm a personal devil."],["Mental illness is usually demonic.","CCC 1673 requires excluding illness first; see the psychology track."]],
 scripture:[["Ex 7–8","Pharaoh's magicians and their limits"],["Job 1–2","Satan acts only within God's permission"],["Mk 5:1–20","the Gerasene demoniac"],["Jas 1:13–14","each is tempted by his own concupiscence"],["1 Pet 5:8–9","resist him, steadfast in faith"]],
 tests:[["Can a demon change my will?","No; only God moves the will from within. A demon can only persuade."],["What is a miracle strictly?","An effect beyond the order of the whole created nature, which God alone can produce."]],
 read:["ST I qq.110–111, 114","CCC 391–395, 1673, 2850–2854"]},

{id:"angels-guardian",tract:"angels",level:1,note:"Guardian angels exist: sententia certa; each human has one: sententia communis",st:"ST I q.113",
 q:"Does every person have a guardian angel, and what does it do?",
 one:"Yes: Thomas teaches that 'each man has an angel guardian appointed to him', from birth, for as long as he is a pilgrim on earth; the angel enlightens, protects and moves him toward good, but cannot override his freedom.",
 plain:`<p>Jesus says of the little ones that 'their angels in heaven always see the face of my Father' (Mt 18:10). The Church celebrates the Guardian Angels on 2 October and teaches that 'from its beginning until death, human life is surrounded by their watchful care and intercession' (CCC 336).</p>
<p>Thomas explains it as part of providence: 'Each man has an angel guardian appointed to him' (I q.113 a.2). Life is a journey on a dangerous road, and 'as guardians are appointed for men who have to pass by an unsafe road, so an angel guardian is assigned to each man as long as he is a wayfarer' (I q.113 a.4). Every person has one, Christian or not, because the guardianship belongs to man as a rational being, so it begins at birth, not at baptism (I q.113 a.5). The guardian acts on our imagination and understanding, proposing good, and protects us from dangers, but it cannot move our will.</p>`,
 analogy:["The mountain guide","A guide on a dangerous mountain does not carry you up; you walk. But he knows the route, warns you of crevasses, steadies you, and if you fall behind, waits. He cannot make you follow."],
 art:{obj:["Angels guard nations and churches, not individuals.","Further, if each had an angel, the wicked would not perish.","Further, Christ needed no guardian; so the angel is not for everyone."],
  sc:"'Their angels in heaven always see the face of my Father' (Mt 18:10).",
  resp:"The guardianship of angels belongs to the execution of providence, which for corruptible things that are perishable as individuals cares only for the species, but for incorruptible individuals, like humans, cares for each one. 'Each man has an angel guardian appointed to him' (I q.113 a.2), from birth, for the whole of his earthly journey.",
  rep:["Higher angels guard communities and nations (Dan 10:13, 20–21); lower angels guard individuals.","The angel does not force; we can resist good inspirations, and God permits it.","Christ, as head and comprehensor, did not need guarding, but angels ministered to him (Mt 4:11)."]},
 deeper:`<p><strong>What the guardian does.</strong> Enlightens the mind by strengthening and ordering images and ideas (I q.111 a.1), restrains demons, protects from physical and spiritual harm as God permits, carries our prayers to God (Tob 12:12; Rev 8:3–4), and at death accompanies the soul (Lk 16:22).</p>
<p><strong>Does it grieve?</strong> Thomas: the angels do not grieve over those they guard who are lost, since they will what God's justice wills (I q.113 a.7).</p>
<p><strong>In heaven</strong> the guardian no longer guards but 'reigns with' the saved; in hell, Thomas adds, there is a demon to punish.</p>`,
 errors:[["Guardian angels are our loved ones who have died.","Different natures; the dead do not become angels."],["Only baptised Christians have guardian angels.","Thomas (with Jerome) holds that the guardian is appointed at birth."]],
 scripture:[["Mt 18:10","their angels see the Father's face"],["Ps 90 [91]:11","he hath given his angels charge over thee"],["Acts 12:15","'It is his angel'"],["Ex 23:20","I will send my angel before thee"]],
 tests:[["When does the guardian angel's care begin?","At birth (I q.113 a.5)."],["Can my guardian angel make me do good?","No, it can enlighten and protect; the will is moved from within only by God."]],
 read:["ST I q.113","CCC 336"]},

{id:"angels-orders",tract:"angels",level:2,note:"Nine choirs: traditional (Dionysius, Gregory); not defined",st:"ST I qq.106–108",
 q:"How are the angels ordered, and how do they 'speak' to one another?",
 one:"Following Dionysius and Gregory, Thomas arranges the angels in three hierarchies of three orders (seraphim, cherubim, thrones; dominations, virtues, powers; principalities, archangels, angels); higher angels 'illuminate' lower ones by passing on truth, and they 'speak' simply by willing that another know a thought.",
 plain:`<p>Scripture names nine kinds of heavenly beings in different places (Isa 6; Gen 3:24; Col 1:16; Eph 1:21; Rom 8:38; 1 Thess 4:16; Jude 9). Pseudo-Dionysius (c. 500) arranged them in three 'hierarchies' of three 'orders', and Gregory the Great adopted a slightly different arrangement. Thomas notes they differ only on the place of Virtues and Principalities (I q.108 a.6).</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table class="t"><thead><tr><th>Hierarchy</th><th>Orders</th><th>Contemplates</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>First</td><td>Seraphim, Cherubim, Thrones</td><td>God's goodness and the reasons of things in God himself</td></tr>
<tr><td>Second</td><td>Dominations, Virtues, Powers</td><td>The universal ordering of providence</td></tr>
<tr><td>Third</td><td>Principalities, Archangels, Angels</td><td>The execution of providence in particular things, nations and persons</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p><strong>Illumination.</strong> 'Intellectual light is nothing else than a manifestation of truth' (I q.106 a.1). A higher angel strengthens a lower one's intellect and presents truth in a form it can grasp, as a teacher breaks down a principle. <strong>Speech.</strong> Angels have no voices; one angel speaks to another by willing to make its thought known (I q.107 a.1). There is no barrier except the will to share.</p>`,
 analogy:["A university faculty","The most senior scholars grasp a field's first principles; mid-level faculty organise curricula; tutors work one-to-one with students. Knowledge flows downward by teaching, and everyone serves the same truth."],
 deeper:`<p><strong>One hierarchy of all rational creatures.</strong> Thomas says there is ultimately 'one hierarchy, not only of all the angels, but also of all rational creatures, who can be participators of sacred things' (I q.108 a.1), so men in glory are taken into the angelic orders according to their merit (I q.108 a.8). The Church is the earthly part of this hierarchy.</p>
<p><strong>Archangels and names.</strong> 'Archangel' is both an order and a title for angels sent on great missions. Only Michael, Gabriel and Raphael are named in the canonical Scriptures.</p>`,
 schools:`<p>The ninefold scheme is traditional and was universally received in the Middle Ages, but it is not a dogma. Some modern theologians question the authority of Pseudo-Dionysius once his writings were shown to date from c. 500 (not the 1st century), but the scriptural names remain and the scheme continues to be used in the liturgy (e.g. the Preface: 'with Angels and Archangels, with Thrones and Dominions').</p>`,
 tests:[["Which order is highest?","Seraphim."],["How does one angel 'speak' to another?","By willing that its thought be known to the other."]],
 read:["ST I qq.106–108","Pseudo-Dionysius, The Celestial Hierarchy"]},

{id:"angels-time",tract:"angels",level:3,note:"Theological conclusion",st:"ST I q.10 aa.5–6",
 q:"Do angels live in time? What is aeviternity?",
 one:"Angels exist not in time (which measures change) nor in eternity (which belongs to God alone), but in aeviternity: an unchanging existence that nonetheless has a beginning and admits a succession of acts.",
 plain:`<p>Thomas defines eternity as 'the simultaneously-whole and perfect possession of interminable life' (Boethius; I q.10 a.1). Only God has it. Time is the measure of change, of before and after. Angels do not change in their substance, but they do have successive thoughts and choices. So their duration is a third thing: 'Aeviternity differs from time, and from eternity, as the mean between them both' (I q.10 a.5).</p>
<p>Thomas adds that the difference is not simply that eternity has no beginning and aeviternity has one; even if angels had always existed, aeviternity would still differ from eternity, because only in God is existence wholly without potency and succession.</p>`,
 analogy:["The mountain and the river","A river flows (time). A mountain stands unchanged for ages, though weather passes over it (aeviternity). The sky in which both exist is not itself measured by either (eternity)."],
 deeper:`<p><strong>Why it matters.</strong> Aeviternity explains how angels can make an unrepeatable choice, how the separated soul exists between death and resurrection (souls too are aeviternal in their being), and how the saints in glory share God's eternity by participation while remaining creatures. Wagner's Annotated Thomist covers the whole of q.10, including the two articles on aeviternity.</p>`,
 authors:[["Christian B. Wagner","christianbwagner.com, Annotated Thomist: 'Whether Aeviternity Differs from Time?'; 'Whether Eternity Differs from Time?'; 'Whether this is a Good Definition of Eternity'","A line-by-line annotated walk through ST I q.10, explaining the Boethian definition and the three measures of duration."]],
 tests:[["What are the three measures of duration?","Eternity (God), aeviternity (angels, souls, heavenly beings), time (changing things)."]],
 read:["ST I q.10 aa.1–6"]},

{id:"angels-number",tract:"angels",level:1,note:"Opinion; Scripture indicates very many",st:"ST I q.50 a.3",
 q:"How many angels are there?",
 one:"Scripture and Thomas say there is an immense number, 'far beyond all material multitude'; the Fathers read the ninety-nine sheep left in the hills as the angels, compared with fallen humanity, the one lost sheep.",
 plain:`<p>Daniel sees 'thousands of thousands… and ten thousand times a hundred thousand' (Dan 7:10); Jesus could ask for 'more than twelve legions of angels' (Mt 26:53). Thomas reasons that since God's aim is the perfection of the universe, and the more perfect things are made in greater abundance, 'the angels, even inasmuch as they are immaterial substances, exist in exceeding great number, far beyond all material multitude' (I q.50 a.3). Since each angel is its own species, this also means a staggering number of different natures.</p>
<p>Christian Wagner gathers the patristic reading of the parable of the lost sheep (Lk 15:3–7; Mt 18:12–13): the ninety-nine are the unfallen angels, the one lost sheep is the human race, which the Good Shepherd leaves heaven to find. Hilary, Cyril of Jerusalem, Gregory of Nyssa, Ambrose, Cyril of Alexandria and Gregory the Great read it this way; Thomas's Catena on Matthew 18 transmits it.</p>`,
 analogy:["The galaxy and the planet","Our whole human story, from the first man to the last, may be like one planet compared with a galaxy of spiritual creatures. Yet the Shepherd came for this one."],
 deeper:`<p>The point is not arithmetic but proportion and mercy. Humanity is small in the scale of creation, yet God became man, not angel (Heb 2:16). Gregory the Great draws the conclusion that the number of the elect will complete the heavenly city diminished by the fall.</p>`,
 authors:[["Christian B. Wagner","thomism.com: 'On the Number of Angels'","A 'quote mine' of Fathers and theologians (Petavius, Cornelius a Lapide, Hilary, the Cyrils, Gregory of Nyssa, Ambrose, Gregory the Great, Theophylact, Anselm, the Glossa) reading the ninety-nine sheep as the angels; Bareille notes no Father fixes a number."]],
 scripture:[["Dan 7:10","thousands of thousands ministered to him"],["Mt 26:53","more than twelve legions of angels"],["Heb 12:22","an innumerable company of angels"],["Lk 15:3–7","the lost sheep"]],
 tests:[["Why does Thomas think angels are very numerous?","Because God intends the perfection of the universe, and higher things are made in greater abundance."]],
 read:["ST I q.50 a.3"]}
);

// Disputed questions: God, one in nature (ST I qq.2–26, 105)
DQ.push(
{id:"god-simple",tract:"god",level:2,note:"De fide (Lateran IV: 'a simple substance or nature altogether'; Vatican I)",st:"ST I q.3",
 q:"What does it mean that God is 'simple', and how can a simple God have many attributes?",
 one:"Simplicity means God has no parts or composition of any kind (no body, no matter and form, no essence distinct from existence, no substance and accidents); his justice, mercy and wisdom are not pieces of God but one infinite perfection that we name in many ways because we know it through many creatures.",
 plain:`<p>Everything we know is composed: bodies of parts, living things of matter and form, people of a nature plus qualities they can gain or lose. Composition always implies dependence: the whole depends on its parts and on whatever put them together. God is the first cause of everything, so he cannot depend on anything. Hence Thomas concludes that God is 'nowise composite, but is altogether simple' (I q.3 a.7).</p>
<p>Then how can God be just, merciful, wise and powerful? Christian Wagner's explanation is clear: real composition is 'the real union of really distinct things', like Lego blocks clicked together. God's attributes are not blocks clicked together. All the perfections that creatures have in a divided and limited way, God has in one undivided way, 'under the sole and simple form of the Deity'. We need many names because our minds know God through many created effects; but what each name points to in God is the one divine essence.</p>`,
 analogy:["White light and a prism","White light passed through a prism appears as seven colours. The colours are real, and each tells you something true about the light, but in the light itself they are not seven separate things glued together. Creation is the prism; God's justice, mercy and wisdom are the 'colours' we see; in God they are one."],
 art:{obj:["Whatever has several attributes is composite; God is wise, just, powerful. So God is composite.","Further, if God's justice is his mercy, then God punishes by mercy and pardons by justice, which is absurd.","Further, the Trinity is three persons; so God is not simple."],
  sc:"Augustine: God 'is truly and absolutely simple' (De Trin. VI.6–7).",
  resp:"There is in God no composition of quantitative parts, of matter and form, of nature and supposit, of essence and existence, of genus and difference, or of subject and accident. 'Therefore, it is clear that God is nowise composite, but is altogether simple' (I q.3 a.7). Every composite is posterior to and dependent on its parts, and has a cause that unites them; but God is the first being and uncaused.",
  rep:["The many names signify one simple reality under different aspects (rationes), because 'all perfections existing in creatures divided and multiplied, pre-exist in God unitedly' (I q.13 a.5). The distinction is of reason with a foundation in the thing, not a real distinction.","Justice and mercy are the same in God, but their effects differ in creatures: mercy is God's goodness as relieving misery, justice the same goodness as giving each its due.","The divine persons are really distinct only by their relations of origin, which are not parts of the essence: each person is the whole divine essence (see the Trinity tract)."]},
 deeper:`<p><strong>Why it is not abstract.</strong> Simplicity is a negative name: it tells us what God is not (composed), not a positive picture. It protects God's aseity (existing from himself), immutability, eternity and infinity. If God had parts, something would be prior to God.</p>
<p><strong>Essence and existence.</strong> The deepest point is the identity of God's essence and existence: 'God is not only His own essence… but also His own existence' (I q.3 a.4). In every creature what it is differs from that it is; in God, what he is <em>is</em> to be. Hence the name 'He who is' (Ex 3:14) is the most proper name of God (I q.13 a.11).</p>
<p><strong>Church teaching.</strong> Lateran IV (1215) calls God 'one supreme reality, incomprehensible and ineffable… a simple substance or nature altogether'; Vatican I (Dei Filius ch. 1) calls him 'a unique, completely simple and immutable spiritual substance'. Wagner points to the condemnation of propositions denying any distinction in God (in Denzinger, the Eckhart propositions), which shows that simplicity does not mean the absence of all distinction: the persons are really distinct.</p>`,
 schools:`<p><strong>Thomists</strong> hold that the distinction between divine attributes is a distinction of reason with a foundation in God (rationis ratiocinatae). <strong>Scotus</strong> held a 'formal distinction' (distinctio formalis a parte rei) between attributes, less than real but more than of reason. <strong>Palamite Orthodoxy</strong> holds a real distinction between God's essence and his uncreated energies; Catholic Thomists argue this introduces composition (see 'East, West and the Reformation'). Some modern analytic philosophers (Plantinga, Mullins) reject simplicity; Feser and others defend it.</p>`,
 authors:[["Christian B. Wagner","thomism.com: 'Why Divine Simplicity is Obvious'; 'Divine Simplicity and the Multiplicity…'; Annotated Thomist on ST I q.3","Simplicity is dogma and follows readily from reason once 'composition' is defined: the real union of really distinct things. God has distinctions (the persons) but no composition."],["Edward Feser","Five Proofs of the Existence of God (2017) ch. 6–7; blog series on classical theism","Shows that each of his five proofs arrives at a God who must be simple, and answers the 'modal collapse' and 'attribute identity' objections of analytic philosophers."],["Réginald Garrigou-Lagrange","God: His Existence and His Nature (1914; Eng. 1934); The One God","Treats simplicity as following from God as Ipsum Esse Subsistens, the 'self-subsistent Being itself', the metaphysical summit of Thomism."]],
 errors:[["Simplicity means God is impersonal or static.","God is supremely living, knowing and loving; simplicity means he has these perfections in fullness, not by parts."],["The attributes are merely our labels with nothing real behind them.","They truly signify God's essence, though imperfectly (I q.13 a.2)."]],
 scripture:[["Ex 3:14","I AM WHO AM"],["Jas 1:17","with whom there is no change"],["1 Jn 4:8","God is love: not 'has' love"]],
 tests:[["Define composition.","The real union of really distinct parts."],["Why are the persons not 'parts' of God?","Each person is the whole divine essence; they are distinguished only by relations of origin."]],
 read:["ST I q.3 aa.1–8","<a href=\"#dq-controversy-energies\">Essence and energies</a>"]},

{id:"god-analogy",tract:"god",level:2,note:"Theological conclusion; Lateran IV on dissimilarity",st:"ST I q.13 aa.2–6",
 q:"How can human words truly describe God?",
 one:"Words like 'good' and 'wise' are said of God and creatures neither in exactly the same sense (univocally) nor in unrelated senses (equivocally) but by analogy: they name perfections that exist in God first and supremely, and in creatures by participation.",
 plain:`<p>If 'wise' meant the same in 'God is wise' and 'Socrates is wise', God would be a bigger Socrates. If it meant something totally different, we would know nothing about God. Thomas takes the middle way: 'Univocal predication is impossible between God and creatures' (I q.13 a.5), yet we are not left in total darkness. Perfections that come from God as cause exist in him in a higher way, so the name is used analogically, 'according to proportion' (I q.13 a.5).</p>
<p>When we say 'God is wise', the <em>thing signified</em> (wisdom) is truly in God, more truly than in us. But the <em>mode of signifying</em> is ours: we picture wisdom as a quality added to a person. In God it is not added; it is his essence. So the word 'leaves the thing signified as incomprehended, and as exceeding the signification of the name' (I q.13 a.5).</p>`,
 analogy:["'Healthy'","We call a man healthy, medicine healthy and complexion healthy. Not in the same sense, nor unrelated senses: medicine causes health, complexion shows it, the man has it. 'Good' said of creatures and God is like this, except that God is goodness itself and creatures are good by sharing in it."],
 art:{obj:["Names are imposed from creatures; so they signify creaturely modes and cannot apply to God.","Further, God is infinitely distant from creatures; so no common name applies.","Further, if names are analogical they are vague and tell us nothing."],
  sc:"Paul: 'the invisible things of him… are clearly seen, being understood by the things that are made' (Rom 1:20).",
  resp:"No name is predicated univocally of God and creatures, because perfections divided in creatures pre-exist united in God; nor purely equivocally, for then nothing could be known or demonstrated of God from creatures, contrary to Romans 1:20. Hence names are said of God and creatures 'in an analogous sense, i.e. according to proportion' (I q.13 a.5): according to the order of creature to God as to its principle and cause, in whom all perfections pre-exist excellently.",
  rep:["The names are imposed from creatures (order of knowing), but the perfections are in God first (order of being): 'these names are applied primarily to God rather than to creatures' as to what they signify (I q.13 a.6).","Infinite distance excludes univocity, not analogy based on causality.","Analogy gives true but inadequate knowledge: we know that God is wise and what wisdom is, but not how it exists in God."]},
 deeper:`<p><strong>The three ways.</strong> We name God by causality (God is the cause of wisdom), negation (God's wisdom is not limited, not acquired, not a quality) and eminence (God is wisdom itself, beyond any concept). See Academy F6.</p>
<p><strong>Lateran IV's rule.</strong> 'Between Creator and creature no similarity can be expressed without implying a greater dissimilarity' (Lateran IV, cited CCC 43). Analogy does not tame God; it lets us speak truly while confessing that he exceeds our speech.</p>
<p><strong>Metaphor versus proper names.</strong> 'Rock', 'lion', 'angry' are metaphors: they include a creaturely mode in their meaning. 'Being', 'good', 'living', 'wise' are proper names: their meaning does not include limitation (I q.13 a.3).</p>`,
 schools:`<p>Cajetan (De nominum analogia, 1498) made the 'analogy of proper proportionality' central; others (Suárez, many modern Thomists) stress the 'analogy of attribution'. Scotus argued that a univocal concept of being is necessary for natural theology; Thomists reject it. Karl Barth called the analogia entis 'the invention of Antichrist'; Erich Przywara and later Catholic theologians defended it.</p>`,
 authors:[["Christian B. Wagner","Annotated Thomist on ST I q.13 (all articles, July 2023)","Article-by-article commentary on the divine names, including 'Whether what is said of God and of creatures is univocally predicated', 'Whether this name He Who Is is the most proper name of God'."],["Edward Feser","Five Proofs ch. 6; Scholastic Metaphysics ch. 5","Defends analogy against the charge that it makes theology empty, showing how causal dependence grounds real knowledge of God."],["Garrigou-Lagrange","God: His Existence and His Nature, vol. 2","A classic treatment of analogical knowledge of God and the danger of both agnosticism and anthropomorphism."]],
 errors:[["We can know nothing about God (agnosticism).","Contradicts Rom 1:20 and Vatican I."],["God is just like us but bigger (anthropomorphism).","Ignores the 'greater dissimilarity'."]],
 tests:[["Why is 'rock' a metaphor for God but 'good' a proper name?","'Rock' includes material limitation in its meaning; 'good' does not."]],
 read:["ST I q.13","CCC 39–43","<a href=\"#lesson-f6\">Academy F6</a>"]},

{id:"god-immutable",tract:"god",level:3,note:"De fide (God is immutable: Lateran IV, Vatican I)",st:"ST I q.9; q.13 a.7; q.19 a.7",
 q:"If God cannot change, how can he love us, answer prayer, become angry or relent?",
 one:"God's knowledge and will are eternal and unchanging, but they are directed to things that change; the change is in creatures, not in God, and his 'relations' to us (Creator, Lord, Father) are real in us and relations of reason in him.",
 plain:`<p>God is pure act: he has no unrealised potential, so nothing can be added to him. He cannot change for better (he is perfect) or for worse. Then how does Scripture say God 'repented' (Gen 6:6) or 'relented' (Jonah 3:10)?</p>
<p>Thomas answers with a distinction. God's will does not change, but he can will a change: from eternity he wills that a sinner be threatened and, if he repents, spared. When the sinner changes, the effect changes; God's will was the same throughout. Biblical language of repenting and anger is anthropomorphic: it describes God's unchanging will in terms of the effects we experience. 'The will of God is entirely unchangeable', but 'to change the will is one thing; to will that certain things should be changed is another' (I q.19 a.7).</p>
<p>What about prayer? We do not pray to change God's mind but to receive what God from eternity willed to give through our prayer: prayer is one of the causes God's providence uses (II-II q.83 a.2; see Academy T9a).</p>`,
 analogy:["The lighthouse","A ship passing a lighthouse first approaches its beam, then sails into its light, then away. To the sailors the light seems to come and go, warm and then cold. The lighthouse has not moved; the ship has. Our relationship to God changes as we turn toward or away from him; God's love does not."],
 art:{obj:["Love involves being affected by the beloved; so if God loves, he changes.","Further, God became Creator when the world began; so he gained a new relation.","Further, Scripture says God repented."],
  sc:"'I am the Lord, and I change not' (Mal 3:6).",
  resp:"God is pure act without potentiality, the first being, infinite; whatever changes acquires something new and has potency. Therefore God is altogether immutable. Names like 'Lord' and 'Creator' which imply relation to creatures are said of God in time, not because of a change in him but because of a change in creatures: 'in God there is no real relation to creatures, but a relation only in idea, inasmuch as creatures are referred to Him' (I q.13 a.7).",
  rep:["Divine love is not a passion; it is the will willing good to another. God's love does not respond to our goodness; it causes it: 'the love of God infuses and creates goodness' (I q.20 a.2).","The relation is real in the creature, which depends on God; 'Creator' is said of God from the time creatures begin, without change in him, as a column becomes 'on the right' when a man moves (I q.13 a.7).","Anthropomorphic language expresses the effect God works: punishment looks like anger, mercy after repentance looks like relenting."]},
 deeper:`<p><strong>The 'mixed relation'.</strong> Thomas's example: a column is 'on the right' of an animal only because the animal stands in a certain position; the relation is real in the animal and only in idea in the column. Likewise creatures really depend on God, but God does not depend on creatures. This guards God's freedom and our total dependence on him.</p>
<p><strong>Does this make God indifferent?</strong> No: God loves more than any creature loves, but without need. Our love seeks a good we lack; God's love gives a good we lack. The Incarnation lets the impassible God suffer in his human nature (see Christology): 'the impassible one suffered' is said of the Word because of the communication of idioms.</p>`,
 schools:`<p>Process theology (Whitehead, Hartshorne) and 'open theism' (Pinnock, Boyd) deny divine immutability to make God responsive. Some recent Catholic writers have sympathised with a 'suffering God' (Moltmann's influence). Thomists, with Feser and Wagner, answer that a God who changes would not be the God of the five ways, and that responsiveness is better secured by eternal providence that includes our free acts and prayers.</p>`,
 authors:[["Christian B. Wagner","thomism.com: 'On the Divine Freedom and Immutability'; 'On Divine Freedom and the Thomists' (on Marín-Sola's Concordia Tomista); Annotated Thomist on ST I q.9 and q.19","Works through how God's freedom and immutability are compatible, drawing on the Thomist commentators and Fr Francisco Marín-Sola."],["Edward Feser","Five Proofs ch. 6; blog posts on classical theism vs theistic personalism","Distinguishes 'classical theism' from 'theistic personalism' (God as a very great person who changes) and argues the latter is incoherent."],["Garrigou-Lagrange","The One God (commentary on I qq.2–26)","Defends immutability and the relation of reason as essential to divine transcendence."]],
 errors:[["God needs us.","God creates out of generosity, not need."],["Prayer changes God's mind.","Prayer is a cause God eternally wills to use; it changes us and the world."]],
 scripture:[["Mal 3:6","I change not"],["Jas 1:17","no change, nor shadow of alteration"],["Num 23:19","God is not as a man, that he should repent"],["Gen 6:6; Jonah 3:10","anthropomorphic 'repenting'"]],
 tests:[["How can 'Creator' be said of God in time without God changing?","The relation is real in creatures and of reason in God; the change is in creatures."],["Why pray if God does not change?","To receive what God eternally willed to give through our prayers."]],
 read:["ST I q.9; q.13 a.7; q.19 a.7; q.20","<a href=\"#lesson-t9a\">Academy T9a: why pray</a>"]},

{id:"god-free",tract:"god",level:3,note:"De fide (God created freely: Vatican I, Dei Filius can. 1.5)",st:"ST I q.19 aa.3, 10",
 q:"Did God have to create? How can a necessary, unchanging God be free?",
 one:"God necessarily wills his own goodness, but he does not necessarily will creatures, since his goodness is complete without them; so creation is absolutely free, though, once willed, it is unchangeably willed.",
 plain:`<p>Thomas distinguishes two kinds of necessity. Something is <em>absolutely</em> necessary when its opposite is impossible (a triangle has three sides). Something is necessary <em>by supposition</em> when it follows from something else being the case (if Socrates is sitting, he is sitting). God wills his own goodness absolutely necessarily. But creatures add nothing to his goodness: 'since the goodness of God is perfect, and can exist without other things inasmuch as no perfection can accrue to Him from them, it follows that His willing things apart from Himself is not absolutely necessary' (I q.19 a.3). It is necessary only by supposition: given that he wills it, his will does not change.</p>
<p>So God did not have to create. Vatican I defined that God created 'by an absolutely free decision' (Dei Filius ch. 1) and anathematised those who say he created necessarily.</p>`,
 analogy:["The artist who needs nothing","A rich, happy artist paints a picture purely to give it away. He did not need to paint; his happiness was complete. Having freely decided to paint, he paints. The picture depends on him; he does not depend on the picture."],
 art:{obj:["Whatever God wills he wills from eternity; what is eternal is necessary; so he wills creatures necessarily.","Further, God wills creatures because he wills his goodness; but he wills his goodness necessarily; so he wills creatures necessarily.","Further, a simple, unchanging God cannot have chosen otherwise, since he would then be different."],
  sc:"Paul: God 'worketh all things according to the counsel of his will' (Eph 1:11); what is done by counsel is not necessary.",
  resp:"God wills his own goodness by absolute necessity, as our will necessarily wills happiness. But other things he wills as ordered to his goodness, and we do not necessarily will means without which the end can be had. Since God's goodness can exist without creatures, his willing them 'is not absolutely necessary. Yet it can be necessary by supposition' (I q.19 a.3).",
  rep:["'From the fact that God wills from eternity whatever He wills, it does not follow that He wills it necessarily; except by supposition' (I q.19 a.3 ad 1).","'Although God necessarily wills His own goodness, He does not necessarily will things willed on account of His goodness' (I q.19 a.3 ad 2).","God would be no different intrinsically if he had not created; the difference would be entirely on the side of creatures. This is the 'modal collapse' objection, answered below."]},
 deeper:`<p><strong>The 'modal collapse' objection.</strong> Analytic critics argue: if God is identical with his will to create, and God exists necessarily, then creation exists necessarily. Thomists reply that God's act of will is necessary as an act, but its <em>terminus</em> (that it is directed to this world) adds nothing real to God; the contingency lies wholly in the creature. God's eternal act would be the same act whether or not it terminated in creatures, since its whole object and motive is his goodness. Feser and others have developed this reply at length.</p>
<p><strong>Free choice in God.</strong> Thomas affirms that God has free choice (liberum arbitrium) with respect to what he does not necessarily will (I q.19 a.10), without the imperfection of hesitation or possible sin.</p>`,
 schools:`<p>The 'modal collapse' debate (R.T. Mullins, Ryan Mullins; replies by Feser, Brian Davies, Christopher Tomaszewski and others) is lively. Wagner draws on Marín-Sola, a 20th-century Dominican whose unpublished Concordia Tomista treats divine knowledge, will and predestination, and who is known for a less rigid account of God's decrees than the classical Bañezian school.</p>`,
 authors:[["Christian B. Wagner","thomism.com: 'On Divine Freedom and the Thomists'; 'On the Divine Freedom and Immutability'; Annotated Thomist: 'Whether Whatever God Wills He Wills Necessarily?', 'Whether God has Free Will?'","Presents the Thomist commentators and Marín-Sola on the constitutive principle of the freedom of the divine act."],["Edward Feser","Blog posts on modal collapse and divine freedom; Five Proofs ch. 7","Argues that divine simplicity does not entail modal collapse because God's free act has an extrinsic terminus."]],
 errors:[["God created because he was lonely.","The Trinity is perfect communion; creation adds nothing to God's happiness."],["God had to create the best possible world.","There is no best possible world, since God could always make something better; he freely chose this order (I q.25 a.6)."]],
 tests:[["Distinguish absolute and suppositional necessity.","Absolute: the opposite is impossible. Suppositional: necessary given something else (e.g. given God willed it)."]],
 read:["ST I q.19 aa.3, 10; q.25 aa.5–6","Vatican I, Dei Filius ch. 1 and can. 1.5"]},

{id:"god-foreknow",tract:"god",level:2,note:"De fide (God's infallible foreknowledge; human freedom)",st:"ST I q.14 a.13",
 q:"If God knows everything I will do, am I really free?",
 one:"God does not 'foresee' the future from inside time; from eternity he sees all times as present, so his knowledge of my free choice no more forces it than your seeing someone sitting forces them to sit.",
 plain:`<p>The worry: if God knew yesterday that I will lie tomorrow, I cannot do otherwise. Thomas answers with eternity. God's knowledge is measured by eternity, which is 'simultaneously whole' and 'comprises all time'. So 'all things that are in time are present to God from eternity… because His glance is carried from eternity over all things as they are in their presentiality' (I q.14 a.13). God does not know my choice before I make it, as a prediction; he knows it in his eternal now, as present.</p>
<p>Knowing something as present does not cause it to be necessary. If you see Socrates sitting, he must be sitting while you see him (necessity by supposition), but your seeing does not make him sit. Likewise God's knowledge leaves my choice free: it is necessary that what God knows will happen, but not necessary that it happen by necessity.</p>`,
 analogy:["The watcher on the hill","Travellers walk along a winding road, each seeing only the bend in front. Someone on a high hill sees the whole road at once: who is coming, who has passed, who is at the bend. His view does not make anyone walk; they walk freely. God's eternity is a hill above all of time."],
 art:{obj:["What God knows must happen, since his knowledge cannot be wrong; so what he knows of my choices happens necessarily.","Further, a true conditional with a necessary antecedent has a necessary consequent: 'If God knew this will happen, it will happen'; the antecedent is past and so necessary; so the consequent is necessary.","Further, knowledge of the future is only of what is determined in its causes; free acts are not; so God cannot know them."],
  sc:"'All things are naked and open to his eyes' (Heb 4:13).",
  resp:"Contingent things can be considered in their causes, as future, or in themselves, as actual. God knows contingents 'not successively, as they are in their own being, as we do but simultaneously', because 'His knowledge is measured by eternity', which 'comprises all time'; so things in time 'are present to God from eternity' (I q.14 a.13).",
  rep:["What is known by God is necessary as known (by supposition), not absolutely in itself.","The consequent must be taken as it is in God's knowledge, i.e. as present; as present it is necessary by supposition but contingent in its causes.","God knows free acts not only in their causes but in themselves, in his eternity."]},
 deeper:`<p><strong>The deeper question: how does God know?</strong> Thomas's eternity answer explains why foreknowledge does not destroy freedom. But God's knowledge is also the cause of things (I q.14 a.8). So does God know my free act because he causes it? Here the schools divide (see 'premotion'). The Thomists (Báñez) hold that God knows free acts in his eternal decrees, which move the will infallibly but freely. The Molinists say God knows what each free creature would do in any circumstances ('middle knowledge', scientia media) and then decrees accordingly.</p>`,
 schools:`<p>Boethius (Consolation of Philosophy V) first gave the eternity solution, which Thomas adopts. Ockham and later 'Ockhamists' posit 'soft facts' about the past. Molina (1588) introduced middle knowledge. The Congregatio de Auxiliis (1597–1607) ended with Paul V permitting both Thomists and Molinists to teach their views and forbidding them to call each other heretics.</p>`,
 authors:[["Christian B. Wagner","Annotated Thomist: 'Whether the Knowledge of God is of Future Contingent Things?'; 'Whether God Knows Singular Things?'","A commentary on q.14 a.13 using the classical Thomist commentators."],["Garrigou-Lagrange","Providence (1932); Predestination (1936)","Defends the Thomist account in which God knows future free acts in his decrees, against Molinist middle knowledge."]],
 errors:[["God's knowledge causes events by fatalistic necessity.","Freedom is preserved; God wills free acts to be free."],["God doesn't know the future (open theism).","Contradicts Scripture's prophecy and Vatican I's teaching that all things are 'naked and open' to God."]],
 tests:[["Why does God's knowledge not take away freedom?","Because he knows from eternity, where all times are present, and knowing something as present does not cause it necessarily."]],
 read:["ST I q.14 a.13","Boethius, Consolation of Philosophy V"]},

{id:"god-evil",tract:"god",level:2,note:"De fide (God is not the author of sin: Trent, Session 6, can. 6)",st:"ST I q.19 a.9; q.22 a.2; q.49",
 q:"Does God will evil?",
 one:"God never wills the evil of sin in any way; he wills to permit it, and that permission is good because he can draw greater good from it; the evils of nature and of just punishment he wills indirectly, as a consequence of goods he wills.",
 plain:`<p>Evil is not a thing but a lack: the absence of a good that should be there (blindness, injustice). So God, who causes all being, does not cause evil as such. Thomas distinguishes:</p>
<ul><li><strong>Evil of fault (sin)</strong>: God does not will it in any way. 'God therefore neither wills evil to be done, nor wills it not to be done, but wills to permit evil to be done; and this is a good' (I q.19 a.9).</li>
<li><strong>Evil of nature</strong> (a lion killing a deer, decay): God wills it indirectly, as the consequence of willing a good order of natures that act and interact.</li>
<li><strong>Evil of penalty</strong> (just punishment): God wills it as the good of justice.</li></ul>
<p>Why permit sin? Augustine: God 'would not allow any evil to exist in His works, unless His omnipotence and goodness were such as to bring good even out of evil' (quoted at I q.2 a.3 ad 1). The supreme example is the Cross.</p>`,
 analogy:["The free orchestra","A conductor could make a machine that plays perfectly. Instead he trains free musicians, knowing some will play wrong notes, and he writes the score so that even the wrong notes are woven into a final harmony greater than the machine could produce. He does not want the wrong notes; he permits them for the sake of free music."],
 art:{obj:["Whatever happens, God wills, since his will is always fulfilled; evil happens; so God wills evil.","Further, God could prevent every sin; not preventing is willing.","Further, God hardened Pharaoh's heart; so God causes sin."],
  sc:"Augustine: 'God is not the author of evil, because he is not the cause of tending to non-being' (83 Questions, q.21).",
  resp:"Evil, being the privation of good, cannot be sought for itself, only for a good joined to it. God wills no good more than his goodness, so he never wills the evil of fault, which is opposed to it. He wills evils of nature and of punishment by willing the goods to which they are attached. 'God therefore neither wills evil to be done, nor wills it not to be done, but wills to permit evil to be done; and this is a good' (I q.19 a.9).",
  rep:["God's will is always fulfilled, either by his antecedent will (that all be saved) being followed by his consequent will (that the unrepentant be justly punished), or by the good he draws from evil.","God is not obliged to prevent every evil; he respects the order of free creatures and draws greater goods.","God 'hardens' by withholding grace that the sinner refuses; the sin is the sinner's (I-II q.79 a.3)."]},
 deeper:`<p><strong>Antecedent and consequent will.</strong> Following Damascene, Thomas distinguishes God's antecedent will (God wills all men to be saved, considered absolutely) from his consequent will (considering all circumstances, including a man's final impenitence, God justly wills his punishment) (I q.19 a.6 ad 1). This explains 1 Tim 2:4 alongside the reality of hell.</p>
<p><strong>Thomist subtlety.</strong> The classical Thomists speak of God's 'permissive decree': God eternally decides not to give the efficacious grace that would infallibly prevent a sin, while giving sufficient grace. The defect comes first from the creature, who 'fails first'. Marín-Sola and Maritain proposed that the creature can 'nihilate' (impede) God's movement, making the creature the first cause of the defect more explicitly; this is the debate over 'impedible' motions.</p>`,
 authors:[["Christian B. Wagner","Annotated Thomist: 'Whether God Wills Evils?'; 'Whether the Will of God is Always Fulfilled?'","Close readings of I q.19 aa.6, 9."],["Garrigou-Lagrange","Providence; Predestination","Explains the permissive decree and why God is not the cause of sin even though nothing escapes his providence."],["Edward Feser","Five Proofs ch. 9 (objections); blog posts on the problem of evil","Argues that privation theory and classical theism dissolve the logical problem of evil, since God is not a moral agent among others."]],
 errors:[["God causes sin to show his justice (double predestination to sin).","Condemned at Trent, Session 6, can. 6, and at Orange (529)."],["Evil is a force or substance.","Manicheism: evil is privation."]],
 tests:[["What three kinds of evil does Thomas distinguish, and how does God relate to each?","Fault (permits only), nature (wills indirectly), penalty (wills as justice)."]],
 read:["ST I q.19 aa.6, 9; q.48–49","CCC 309–314"]},

{id:"god-predestination",tract:"god",level:3,note:"Predestination: de fide; its manner (ante/post praevisa merita): free opinion",st:"ST I q.23",
 q:"What is predestination, and is it Calvinism?",
 one:"Predestination is God's eternal plan to bring certain rational creatures to eternal life by grace; for Thomas it is gratuitous, not caused by foreseen merits, yet it works through our free acts, and reprobation is only God's permission of final sin and just punishment, never a decree causing sin, which is why Thomism is not Calvinism.",
 plain:`<p>Predestination is part of providence: the plan by which God leads rational creatures to the supernatural end they cannot reach by nature (I q.23 a.1). It is certain: those God predestines are saved (Rom 8:29–30). Its cause is God's goodness, not our foreseen merits: 'Wherefore, that which flows from free-will is also of predestination' (I q.23 a.5), since our good acts are themselves effects of grace. Considering the effects in particular, merit is a reason for glory; but the whole effect (grace, merit, glory) together has no cause in us.</p>
<p><strong>Reprobation</strong> is different. 'As men are ordained to eternal life through the providence of God, it likewise is part of that providence to permit some to fall away from that end; this is called reprobation' (I q.23 a.3). God does not cause the sin; the sinner's free fault is the cause of damnation. As Thomas puts it, 'The reason for the predestination of some, and reprobation of others, must be sought for in the goodness of God' (I q.23 a.5 ad 3), manifested as mercy in some and justice in others.</p>`,
 analogy:["The rescue","Many climbers are lost on a mountain by their own fault. A rescuer saves some, who could not save themselves, and they owe everything to him. Those not rescued are lost by their own fault, not by the rescuer pushing them. The rescued cannot boast; the lost cannot blame the rescuer. (Every analogy fails somewhere: God offers sufficient help to all.)"],
 art:{obj:["If predestination is not caused by foreseen merits, God is a respecter of persons.","Further, if reprobation is certain, the reprobate cannot be saved and is punished unjustly.","Further, predestination makes prayer and effort pointless."],
  sc:"'He chose us in him before the foundation of the world… predestinated us unto the adoption of children… according to the purpose of his will' (Eph 1:4–5).",
  resp:"No one has said that merit causes the act of predestination in God. The question is whether its effect has a cause in us. Origen (merits in a former life), the Pelagians (we begin, God completes) and those who made foreseen use of grace the reason are all rejected. 'It is impossible that the whole of the effect of predestination in general should have any cause as coming from us; because whatsoever is in man disposing him towards salvation, is all included under the effect of predestination; even the preparation for grace' (I q.23 a.5).",
  rep:["Respect of persons is giving what is owed unequally; grace is not owed, so God is just to all and merciful to many.","Reprobation includes permitting sin and willing to punish it; the reprobate are damned for their own fault and could have done otherwise in the sense that they had sufficient grace and freely refused.","'Predestination can be furthered by the prayers of the saints' (I q.23 a.8): God predestines the means with the end, including our prayers and efforts."]},
 deeper:`<p><strong>Positive and negative reprobation.</strong> The Thomists distinguish negative reprobation (God's not electing some to glory, ante praevisa demerita) from positive reprobation (God's decree to punish, which presupposes foreseen sin). Calvin taught that God positively decrees both salvation and damnation, and that grace cannot be resisted or lost; Thomists teach that sufficient grace is given to all adults, that justification can be lost by mortal sin, and that no one is damned except for personal sin.</p>
<p><strong>Can I know I am predestined?</strong> Not with the certainty of faith without special revelation (Trent, Session 6, ch. 12 and can. 15–16). The Church gives 'signs' of predestination (perseverance in prayer, love of the poor, devotion to Our Lady), which give hope, not certainty.</p>`,
 schools:`<p><strong>Thomists (Báñez, Billuart, Gonet, Garrigou-Lagrange):</strong> predestination to glory is ante praevisa merita (prior to foreseen merits); grace is efficacious of itself. <strong>Molinists and Congruists (Molina, Suárez, Bellarmine):</strong> Suárez and Bellarmine also held election ante praevisa merita, but explained efficacy by middle knowledge; Molina himself held post praevisa merita. <strong>Marín-Sola</strong> and Maritain proposed modifications allowing 'impedible' divine motions. The Church permits these schools (Paul V, 1607). Calvinism is excluded by Trent.</p>`,
 authors:[["Christian B. Wagner","thomism.com: 'On Calvinism and Thomism'; 'On Thomism, Calvinism and the Freedom…'; Annotated Thomist on all eight articles of I q.23 and 'The Book of Life' (q.24)","Argues that charges of 'crypto-Calvinism' against Thomists are unfounded and that the real question is how the divine decree relates to free will, reporting the common Thomist answer from Billuart and Gonet."],["Garrigou-Lagrange","Predestination (1936)","The classic modern defence of the Thomist doctrine: the 'principle of predilection' (no one would be better than another unless more loved by God) and the reconciliation of God's universal saving will with gratuitous election."]],
 errors:[["God decrees that some will sin so that he can damn them.","Calvinist double predestination is rejected by Trent (Session 6, can. 17) and the Council of Orange."],["I can be sure I am saved.","Trent, Session 6, ch. 12."],["Predestination makes my choices irrelevant.","God predestines through my free acts; 'that which flows from free-will is also of predestination'."]],
 scripture:[["Rom 8:28–30","whom he predestinated, them he also called"],["Rom 9:10–24","Jacob and Esau; the potter"],["Eph 1:3–11","chosen in him before the foundation of the world"],["1 Tim 2:4","God will have all men to be saved"],["2 Pet 1:10","make your calling and election sure by good works"]],
 tests:[["Is predestination caused by foreseen merits, for Thomas?","Not the whole effect; merits are effects of predestination and can be reasons for glory only within the whole effect."],["How does reprobation differ from predestination?","Predestination causes grace and glory; reprobation only permits sin and wills just punishment for it."]],
 read:["ST I q.23 aa.1–8; q.24","Trent, Session 6","<a href=\"#dq-god-premotion\">Physical premotion</a>"]},

{id:"god-premotion",tract:"god",level:3,note:"God's universal causality: de fide; how it works (premotion vs middle knowledge): free school opinion",st:"ST I q.105 aa.4–5; I-II q.109 a.1",
 q:"If God moves my will, how is my act still free and mine?",
 one:"God moves the will from within as the cause of its very power and inclination, so his motion does not compete with my freedom but makes it exist; Thomists call this 'physical premotion', and the Church allows them and the Molinists to dispute exactly how it works.",
 plain:`<p>Nothing moves from potency to act without a cause in act, and God is the first cause of every act, including my free choice (I q.105 a.5). But does God's moving my will make it unfree? Thomas answers no: 'God, while moving the will, does not force it, because He gives the will its own natural inclination' (I q.105 a.4 ad 1). Force is from outside against an inclination; God moves from inside, as the source of the inclination.</p>
<p>He adds: 'to be moved from within is not repugnant to being moved by another' (I q.105 a.4 ad 2). God's causality and mine are not two causes competing on the same level, as two men pushing a boat. They are on different levels: God causes my free act <em>as free</em>. A free act is completely mine and completely God's, under different aspects.</p>`,
 analogy:["The author and the character","An author makes a character make free choices within the story; the character's choices are wholly the character's (they come from its personality and reasons) and wholly the author's (nothing in the story exists unless the author writes it). The author is not a rival character pushing the other around. (The analogy limps: we are real, not fictional.)"],
 art:{obj:["If God moves the will infallibly, it cannot do otherwise; so it is not free.","Further, a voluntary act is from an interior principle; but God is exterior.","Further, 'homicide is not ascribed to the stone, but to the thrower'; so if God moves the will, our acts are imputed to God, not to us."],
  sc:"'It is God who worketh in you, both to will and to accomplish' (Phil 2:13).",
  resp:"The will is moved by its object, the good, and by Him who creates the power of willing. It can be moved by good as object, 'but by God alone sufficiently and efficaciously', since its capacity extends to the universal good, which God alone is; and to incline toward the universal good belongs to the First Mover (I q.105 a.4).",
  rep:["God moves each thing according to its nature: necessary causes necessarily, free causes freely. The will moved by God could do otherwise in the sense of having the power to the opposite (sensus divisus), though it will not in fact (sensus compositus).","'To be moved from within is not repugnant to being moved by another' (I q.105 a.4 ad 2).","'Since its being moved by another does not prevent its being moved from within itself… it does not thereby forfeit the motive for merit or demerit' (I q.105 a.4 ad 3)."]},
 deeper:`<p><strong>The De Auxiliis controversy.</strong> Domingo Báñez (Dominican) taught that God gives 'physical premotion' (praemotio physica), a real motion that applies the will to act and infallibly determines which act, without destroying freedom; for sin, God moves to the physical entity of the act but permits the defect. Luis de Molina (Jesuit, Concordia, 1588) answered that God gives 'simultaneous concurrence' and knows by 'middle knowledge' what each will freely do, then decrees the circumstances. After ten years of papal hearings (1597–1607), Paul V declared neither view heretical, ordered both sides to stop accusing the other, and reserved judgment.</p>
<p><strong>Efficacious and sufficient grace.</strong> For Thomists, sufficient grace gives the real power to do good; efficacious grace gives the actual doing. Garrigou-Lagrange's image: sufficient grace is like the blossom, efficacious grace the fruit; resisting sufficient grace deserves the withholding of efficacious grace.</p>`,
 schools:`<p>See the deeper section. In the 20th century, Marín-Sola, Maritain and Bernard Lonergan (on Thomas's own view in Grace and Freedom) questioned whether Thomas himself held Báñezian premotion. Garrigou-Lagrange defended it vigorously. Wagner draws on Billuart, Gonet and Marín-Sola in his essays.</p>`,
 authors:[["Garrigou-Lagrange","Predestination; Grace (1947); God: His Existence and His Nature","The leading 20th-century defender of physical premotion as the only way to keep God first cause without denying freedom."],["Christian B. Wagner","thomism.com: 'On Calvinism and Thomism'; 'On Divine Freedom and the Thomists'","Explains the Thomist school and why premotion is not determinism."],["Edward Feser","Five Proofs; Scholastic Metaphysics on causation; blog posts on concurrentism","Defends 'concurrentism' (God's causal activity in all creaturely acts) against occasionalism and mere conservation, without settling the De Auxiliis dispute."]],
 errors:[["Either God does it or I do it.","Primary and secondary causes are not on the same level; both wholly cause the act under different aspects."],["Thomism is Calvinism.","Thomists hold sufficient grace for all, resistibility, losability of grace and damnation only for personal sin."]],
 scripture:[["Phil 2:13","God works in you to will and to do"],["Prov 21:1","the heart of the king is in the hand of the Lord"],["Isa 26:12","thou hast wrought all our works for us"],["Sir 15:14","God left man in the hand of his own counsel"]],
 tests:[["Why does God's motion not force the will?","Because it gives the will its own inclination from within; force acts against inclination from without."],["What did Paul V decide in 1607?","Both Thomists and Molinists may teach their views; neither may call the other heretical."]],
 read:["ST I q.105 aa.4–5; I-II q.109 a.1; q.10 a.4","<a href=\"#dq-grace-efficacious\">Sufficient and efficacious grace</a>"]},

{id:"god-vision",tract:"god",level:2,note:"De fide (Benedict XII, Benedictus Deus, 1336; Vienne 1312 on the light of glory)",st:"ST I q.12",
 q:"Can a creature see God's essence, and how?",
 one:"Yes: the blessed see God's essence directly, not through any image, because God himself unites with their intellect, which is strengthened by a created 'light of glory'; they see God wholly but not totally, and some more perfectly than others.",
 plain:`<p>God is supremely knowable in himself, being pure act, but he exceeds every created intellect as the sun exceeds a bat's eyes (I q.12 a.1). Some concluded no creature could ever see God. Thomas says this 'is not tenable', since our final happiness consists in the highest act of our highest power; if we could never see God, either we would never be happy or our happiness would be in something other than God, 'which is opposed to faith'.</p>
<p>How? No created image could represent the infinite essence, so God's essence itself becomes the 'form' by which the intellect understands, and the intellect is raised by 'the light of glory strengthening the intellect to see God… \"In Thy light we shall see light\"' (I q.12 a.5, citing Ps 35 [36]:10). The blessed see God, but do not <em>comprehend</em> him, that is, know him as fully as he is knowable (I q.12 a.7). Those with more charity receive more of the light of glory and see more perfectly (I q.12 a.6).</p>`,
 analogy:["Many cups in one ocean","Cups of different sizes dipped into the ocean are each completely full, but none contains the ocean. The blessed are each completely filled with the vision of God, though none 'contains' God, and larger cups (greater charity) hold more."],
 art:{obj:["God is infinite; a finite intellect cannot see the infinite.","Further, we know through images; there is no image of God's essence.","Further, 'No man hath seen God at any time' (Jn 1:18)."],
  sc:"'We shall see him as he is' (1 Jn 3:2).",
  resp:"Since beatitude consists in the operation of the highest power, if the created intellect could never see God it would never attain beatitude or would find it in something else besides God, 'which is opposed to faith' (I q.12 a.1). Also, natural desire to know the cause of what we see would be in vain. Therefore the blessed see God's essence.",
  rep:["They see the infinite, but not infinitely: wholly, not totally (totum sed non totaliter).","God's essence itself takes the place of an image, uniting to the intellect as the intelligible form; the intellect needs the light of glory to receive it.","John 1:18 refers to comprehension and to sight in this life; in glory we see but do not comprehend."]},
 deeper:`<p><strong>Benedictus Deus (1336).</strong> Pope Benedict XII defined that the souls of the saints, after death (and purification if needed), 'see the divine essence with an intuitive vision, and even face to face, without the mediation of any creature by way of object of vision'. This settled the controversy caused by John XXII's private opinion that souls see God only after the Last Judgment.</p>
<p><strong>The natural desire to see God.</strong> Thomas argues from the intellect's natural desire to know causes (I q.12 a.1; SCG III.50–57). How this desire relates to the gratuity of grace sparked the 20th-century 'nature and grace' debate: Henri de Lubac (Surnaturel, 1946) argued that man has an innate desire for the beatific vision; Garrigou-Lagrange and the classical Thomists (and later Lawrence Feingold, Steven Long, Feser) defended a 'pure nature' account in which the desire is elicited and conditional. Pius XII's Humani Generis 26 warned against destroying 'the gratuity of the supernatural order'.</p>`,
 authors:[["Christian B. Wagner","Annotated Thomist on ST I q.12 (all articles, May–June 2023); 'How Do the Saints Hear Us?'","Line-by-line commentary on the beatific vision; applies the doctrine that the blessed see all things in God that pertain to them, which explains how saints know our prayers."],["Garrigou-Lagrange","Life Everlasting; Beatitude (1951); The Three Ages of the Interior Life","Treats the beatific vision as the goal that orders the whole spiritual life, and defends the gratuity of the supernatural against the nouvelle théologie."],["Edward Feser","Blog posts on the natural desire to see God and 'pure nature'; Aquinas (2009)","Defends the classical Thomist account of pure nature and the elicited natural desire against de Lubac's view."]],
 errors:[["We will see God with bodily eyes.","God is spirit; the vision is intellectual (I q.12 a.3), though the risen body will see Christ's glorified humanity."],["The blessed know everything God knows.","They see God but not all he can do; they know in God what pertains to them (I q.12 a.8)."]],
 scripture:[["1 Jn 3:2","we shall see him as he is"],["1 Cor 13:12","then face to face"],["Ps 35 [36]:10","in thy light we shall see light"],["Mt 5:8","the clean of heart shall see God"]],
 tests:[["What is the light of glory?","A created disposition strengthening the intellect so that God's essence can unite with it as its intelligible form."],["Do the blessed comprehend God?","No: they see him wholly but not totally."]],
 read:["ST I q.12 aa.1–13","Benedict XII, Benedictus Deus (1336)","<a href=\"#lesson-t15\">Academy T15</a>"]}
);

// Disputed questions: the Trinity (ST I qq.27–43)
DQ.push(
{id:"trin-logic",tract:"trinity",level:2,note:"De fide (Nicaea 325; Constantinople 381; Lateran IV; Florence)",st:"ST I q.28 a.3; q.29 a.4",
 q:"Isn't 'one God in three persons' a contradiction?",
 one:"No: a contradiction affirms and denies the same thing in the same respect, but God is one in nature and three in persons; and the persons are distinguished only by opposed relations, which are each really identical with the one essence but not with one another.",
 plain:`<p>'Father, Son and Holy Spirit are each God; there is one God; the Father is not the Son.' The sceptic says: if A = God and B = God, then A = B. Thomas answers with a distinction from Aristotle: 'whatever things are identified with the same thing are identified with each other, if the identity be real and logical' (I q.28 a.3 ad 1), that is, identical both in reality and in definition. Paternity and filiation are each really the same as the divine essence, but 'in their own proper idea and definitions import opposite respects. Hence they are distinguished from each other.'</p>
<p>Ordinary example: the road from Athens to Thebes is the same road as the road from Thebes to Athens, yet 'uphill' and 'downhill' are not the same. Being a father and being a son are opposite relations: nothing can be its own father. So a relation, unlike a quality, can multiply persons without multiplying the substance.</p>`,
 analogy:["The one road","One road, two directions: travelling from A to B and from B to A are the same tarmac, yet they are opposed as directions. In God the 'tarmac' is the one infinite essence; the opposed relations of origin are Father, Son and Spirit. (All analogies of the Trinity limp; this one illustrates only the logic of relation.)"],
 art:{obj:["Things identical with one and the same thing are identical with each other; Father and Son are each identical with the essence; so they are identical with each other.","Further, if there are three persons, there are three of something; three of 'God' would be three gods.","Further, a relation is an accident; in God there are no accidents; so there are no real relations, and no real distinction."],
  sc:"The Athanasian Creed: 'the Father is God, the Son is God, the Holy Spirit is God; and yet they are not three Gods, but one God'.",
  resp:"Relation means regard of one to another according to relative opposition. Since there are real relations in God, there is real opposition, and 'the very nature of relative opposition includes distinction. Hence, there must be real distinction in God, not, indeed, according to that which is absolute—namely, essence, wherein there is supreme unity and simplicity—but according to that which is relative' (I q.28 a.3).",
  rep:["The principle holds only 'if the identity be real and logical'; paternity and filiation are really the same as the essence but opposed in their proper notions (I q.28 a.3 ad 1).","'Three persons' counts subsisting relations, not natures; there is one 'what' (God) and three 'whos'.","In God relation is not an accident: 'relation really existing in God is really the same as His essence and only differs in its mode of intelligibility' (I q.28 a.2)."]},
 deeper:`<p><strong>Why relation is unique among categories.</strong> Every accident has two aspects: its being-in-a-subject and its proper nature. For quantity and quality the proper nature itself refers to the subject. But relation's proper nature is 'toward another'. So in God the being of a relation is the divine essence (no accident), while its 'toward-another' remains, and can distinguish. Gilbert de la Porrée, who made relations 'externally affixed', erred and retracted at Reims (1148) (I q.28 a.2).</p>
<p><strong>Florence's rule.</strong> The Council of Florence (Cantate Domino, 1442): in God 'everything is one where there is no opposition of relation'. Hence the persons share every perfection except the relations that distinguish them.</p>
<p><strong>Modern analytic debates.</strong> 'Relative identity' theories (Peter Geach, Peter van Inwagen), 'social trinitarianism' (three centres of consciousness, e.g. Swinburne, Plantinga) and 'Latin trinitarianism' (Leftow) all try to state the doctrine without contradiction. Thomists warn that social trinitarianism risks tritheism, since three minds and wills would be three Gods; in God there is one intellect and one will, possessed by each person according to its relation.</p>`,
 authors:[["Christian B. Wagner","thomism.com and christianbwagner.com: 'The Logical Problem of the Trinity: Brief Considerations'; 'Person is Relation'; 'A Thomistic Guide to the Trinity'; Annotated Thomist on ST I qq.27–39","A systematic, article-by-article commentary on the whole treatise on the Trinity, showing that the Thomist account of subsisting relations answers the logical problem."],["Edward Feser","Blog posts on the Trinity and analytic philosophy; Five Proofs ch. 7","Argues that classical theism's simplicity and the Thomist account of relation make the Trinity consistent, whereas social trinitarianism compromises monotheism."],["Garrigou-Lagrange","The Trinity and God the Creator (1952)","A detailed commentary on ST I qq.27–43, using the relations and the axiom of Florence."]],
 errors:[["Modalism (Sabellius): one person wearing three masks.","Denies real distinction; condemned."],["Tritheism or social trinitarianism with three wills.","Denies the one essence, intellect and will."],["Subordinationism (Arius): the Son is a lesser God.","Condemned at Nicaea (homoousios)."]],
 scripture:[["Mt 28:19","in the name (singular) of the Father, and of the Son, and of the Holy Ghost"],["Jn 10:30","I and the Father are one"],["Jn 14:16–17","another Paraclete"],["2 Cor 13:13","the grace of the Lord Jesus Christ, the charity of God, the communication of the Holy Ghost"]],
 tests:[["When does 'identical with the same thing, identical with each other' hold?","When the identity is both real and logical (in definition)."],["What distinguishes the persons?","Only opposed relations of origin."]],
 read:["ST I q.28; q.29 a.4","<a href=\"#lesson-t4\">Academy T4</a>"]},

{id:"trin-processions",tract:"trinity",level:2,note:"De fide (the Son is begotten, the Spirit proceeds); the psychological analogy: common teaching",st:"ST I q.27",
 q:"Why are there exactly two processions in God, and why is only one called 'generation'?",
 one:"Procession in God can only be by acts that remain within the agent, and in a spiritual nature these are knowing and loving: the Word proceeds by knowing and is 'begotten' because knowledge produces a likeness; Love proceeds by willing, as an impulse, not a likeness, so it is 'spiration', not generation.",
 plain:`<p>Some actions go out of the agent (heating, building); some stay inside (thinking, loving). God cannot have anything proceed by an outward action without making a creature. But inner acts produce something that stays within: when I understand, I form an inner 'word' (concept); when I love, the beloved is present in me as an impulse toward it. In God these inner products are not accidents but subsistent persons.</p>
<p>'In a nature which is intellectual… these actions are two, the acts of intelligence and of will… It follows that no other procession is possible in God but the procession of the Word, and of Love' (I q.27 a.5). The first is generation because 'the procession of the intellect is by way of similitude, and is called generation, because every generator begets its own like; whereas the procession of the will is not by way of similitude, but rather by way of impulse and movement towards an object' (I q.27 a.4). Hence the Son is begotten; the Holy Spirit 'proceeds… as spirit'.</p>`,
 analogy:["The mind's word and the heart's love","When you know yourself truly, you form an inner image of yourself; when you love that good you know, a movement of love arises. Your mind, its word and its love are one soul, yet distinct in their relations. Augustine and Thomas use this 'psychological analogy' as the best created image of the Trinity: in God the Word and Love are not acts of a person but persons."],
 art:{obj:["God's power is infinite; so there should be infinitely many processions.","Further, goodness is diffusive; so there should be a procession of goodness distinct from love.","Further, if love proceeds from the will, it too is like the lover; so it should be called generation."],
  sc:"The Creed: the Son is 'begotten', the Holy Spirit 'proceeds'.",
  resp:"Processions in God can be only by actions remaining in the agent; in an intellectual nature these are understanding and willing. 'No other procession is possible in God but the procession of the Word, and of Love' (I q.27 a.5).",
  rep:["Power is the principle of action on another; it points to creation, not to processions.","Goodness belongs to the essence; its diffusion within God is through the processions of Word and Love.","The will does not produce a likeness but an inclination; hence love proceeds 'as spirit', a 'vital movement and impulse' (I q.27 a.4)."]},
 deeper:`<p><strong>Not a proof.</strong> Thomas insists the Trinity cannot be demonstrated by reason (I q.32 a.1); the psychological analogy shows its fittingness once revealed. Wagner's Annotated Thomist covers this in 'Whether the Trinity of the divine persons can be known by natural reason'.</p>
<p><strong>The order.</strong> Love presupposes knowledge (nothing is loved unless known), so the Spirit proceeds from the Father and the Word. This is the root of the Western argument for the Filioque.</p>`,
 tests:[["Why only two processions?","Because the only immanent acts of a spiritual nature are knowing and willing."],["Why is the Spirit not 'begotten'?","Because the will's act is an inclination, not a likeness."]],
 read:["ST I q.27 aa.1–5; q.32 a.1"]},

{id:"trin-person",tract:"trinity",level:3,note:"De fide that there are three persons; the definition as subsisting relation: common Thomist teaching",st:"ST I q.29; q.30; q.32 a.3",
 q:"What exactly is a divine person? (Relations, persons, notions)",
 one:"A divine person is a subsisting relation: since in God relation is the essence itself, which subsists, 'the divine paternity is God the Father'; there are four real relations, three persons and five notions.",
 plain:`<p>Boethius defined a person as 'an individual substance of a rational nature'. Applied to God, Thomas explains: 'person' means what is distinct in a nature. In man, what distinguishes is this flesh and these bones; in God, 'distinction… is only by relation of origin… while relation in God is not as an accident in a subject, but is the divine essence itself; and so it is subsistent… Therefore a divine person signifies a relation as subsisting' (I q.29 a.4).</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table class="t"><thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Number</th><th>What they are</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Processions</td><td>2</td><td>Generation (of the Word), spiration (of Love)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Real relations</td><td>4</td><td>Paternity, filiation, active spiration, passive spiration (procession)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Persons</td><td>3</td><td>Paternity (Father), filiation (Son), procession (Holy Spirit); active spiration belongs to Father and Son together, so is not a fourth person</td></tr>
<tr><td>Notions</td><td>5</td><td>Innascibility (unbegottenness), paternity, filiation, common spiration, procession (I q.32 a.3)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>`,
 analogy:["Being a son","You are not a son in addition to being human, as if sonship were a coat; you are a human who is a son. In God, there is nothing 'under' the sonship: the Son simply is the divine essence as begotten. That is what 'subsisting relation' means."],
 art:{obj:["'Person' signifies substance; so in God it signifies the essence, not a relation.","Further, a relation is weaker than a substance; persons are the most perfect; so persons are not relations.","Further, there are four relations; so there should be four persons."],
  sc:"Augustine: 'every word that belongs to the persons signifies relation' (De Trin. VII).",
  resp:"'Person' in general signifies what is distinct in a nature. In God distinction is only by relation of origin, and relation in God is the subsisting essence. 'Therefore, as the Godhead is God so the divine paternity is God the Father, Who is a divine person. Therefore a divine person signifies a relation as subsisting' (I q.29 a.4).",
  rep:["It signifies relation directly and essence indirectly, as subsisting.","Relation in God is not an accident but is the essence, as perfect as the essence.","Active spiration is not opposed to paternity or filiation, so it belongs to two persons and constitutes no third; only opposed relations distinguish."]},
 deeper:`<p><strong>Innascibility.</strong> The Father is known by being from no one (innascibility), a negative notion, not a relation: hence five notions but four relations.</p>
<p><strong>Why not two Sons?</strong> There is one Word because God knows everything in one act; one Love because he loves everything in one act. Hence exactly one Son and one Spirit (I q.30 a.2).</p>
<p><strong>Hypostasis, subsistence, essence.</strong> Thomas distinguishes the terms (I q.29 a.2): essence (ousia) is what a thing is; hypostasis or person is the subsisting individual. The Greek 'three hypostases, one ousia' and the Latin 'three persons, one substance' (essence) say the same.</p>`,
 authors:[["Christian B. Wagner","Annotated Thomist: 'Whether Boethius' Definition of a Person is Unfitting'; 'Whether this Word Person Signifies Relation'; 'Whether there are Notions in God'; 'Whether there are Five Notions'; 'Whether in God there are only Four Real Relations'; thomism.com 'Person is Relation'","A full commentary on ST I qq.28–32, including the technical vocabulary that confuses most readers."],["Garrigou-Lagrange","The Trinity and God the Creator","Explains why the person is constituted by the relation (the Thomist view) rather than by origin (the opinion of some theologians)."]],
 errors:[["Person = separate consciousness.","Modern psychological 'person' is not the classical concept; one divine intellect is shared."],["The Father is 'more God' as source.","He is source without priority of time or perfection."]],
 tests:[["How many real relations, persons and notions are there?","Four relations, three persons, five notions."],["Why is active spiration not a person?","Because it is not opposed to paternity or filiation; it is shared by Father and Son."]],
 read:["ST I qq.28–32"]},

{id:"trin-names",tract:"trinity",level:2,note:"Common teaching",st:"ST I qq.34–38",
 q:"Why is the Son called 'Word' and 'Image', and the Spirit 'Love' and 'Gift'?",
 one:"Each name expresses a person's relation of origin: the Son proceeds by way of intellect, so he is the Word and the perfect Image of the Father; the Spirit proceeds by way of will, so he is Love in person, and since love is the first gift, he is the Gift.",
 plain:`<p><strong>Word.</strong> A word in the mind is what the intellect conceives; it proceeds from the knower and expresses what is known. The Son proceeds by way of intellect, so 'Word' is his proper personal name (I q.34 a.2; Jn 1:1).</p>
<p><strong>Image.</strong> An image proceeds from another and is like it in species. The Son, proceeding as a likeness of the same nature, is the perfect Image (Col 1:15). Man is 'to' the image, an imperfect copy; the Son is the Image (I q.35).</p>
<p><strong>Love.</strong> 'If taken personally it is the proper name of the Holy Ghost; as Word is the proper name of the Son' (I q.37 a.1). We have fewer words for the will's products than for the intellect's, so we use circumlocution.</p>
<p><strong>Gift.</strong> 'A gift is properly an unreturnable giving… the reason of donation being gratuitous is love… love has the nature of a first gift' (I q.38 a.2). The Holy Spirit is the Gift in person, given to us at baptism (Rom 5:5).</p>`,
 analogy:["The thought and the welcome","When you understand a friend you form an inner word of who they are. When you love them, an inward welcome arises, which is the first gift you give before any present. In God the Word is a person, and so is the welcome: the Spirit is the 'kiss' of Father and Son (Bernard)."],
 deeper:`<p><strong>The Father and Son love each other by the Holy Spirit.</strong> Thomas explains this famous phrase (I q.37 a.2): not that the Spirit is the cause of their love, but that the Spirit is the Love proceeding from their mutual love, as we say a tree flowers with flowers.</p>
<p><strong>Missions.</strong> The Son and Spirit are 'sent' into the world without leaving the Father (I q.43): the visible missions (Incarnation; the dove and tongues of fire) and invisible missions (grace in the soul). In grace the Trinity dwells in us as in a temple (Jn 14:23).</p>`,
 authors:[["Christian B. Wagner","Annotated Thomist: 'Whether Word is the Son's Proper Name'; 'Whether the Name of Image is Proper to the Son'; 'Whether Love is the Proper Name of the Holy Spirit'; 'Whether Gift is the Proper Name of the Holy Spirit'; 'Whether the Father and the Son Love Each Other by the Holy Spirit'","Article-by-article commentary on ST I qq.34–38."]],
 tests:[["Why is 'Gift' proper to the Spirit?","Because love is the first gift, and the Spirit is Love in person."]],
 read:["ST I qq.34–38; q.43"]},

{id:"trin-filioque",tract:"trinity",level:3,note:"De fide for Catholics (Lyons II 1274; Florence 1439)",st:"ST I q.36 aa.2–3",
 q:"Why does the Spirit proceed from the Father and the Son (Filioque)?",
 one:"Because the persons are distinguished only by opposed relations, the Spirit could not be distinct from the Son unless related to him by origin; so he proceeds from Father and Son as from one principle, which the Greek formula 'from the Father through the Son' also expresses.",
 plain:`<p>Thomas's argument: 'if He were not from Him, He could in no wise be personally distinguished from Him' (I q.36 a.2). The Father has two relations, to Son and Spirit, but they are not opposed, so he is one person. If Son and Spirit were each related only to the Father, there would be nothing opposing them to each other, and they would be one person. So the Son and Spirit must be related by an opposed relation of origin: either the Son from the Spirit (no one holds this) or the Spirit from the Son.</p>
<p>'From the Father through the Son' says the same: 'the Holy Ghost proceeds from the Father immediately, as from Him, and mediately, as from the Son; and thus He is said to proceed from the Father through the Son' (I q.36 a.3 ad 1). The Father remains the source without source; the Son receives from the Father the power to spirate.</p>`,
 analogy:["Three people and two relationships","If two siblings each relate only to a parent, nothing in that alone distinguishes them from each other by origin. In God, where only origin distinguishes, the Spirit's being 'from' the Son is what makes him not the Son."],
 art:{obj:["Jn 15:26 says the Spirit 'proceedeth from the Father', not from the Son.","Further, the Creed of Constantinople did not include the Filioque, and Ephesus forbade adding to the Creed.","Further, if the Spirit proceeds from the Son, there are two principles of the Spirit."],
  sc:"The Spirit is called 'the Spirit of his Son' (Gal 4:6) and 'the Spirit of Christ' (Rom 8:9); and Christ says, 'he shall receive of mine' (Jn 16:14).",
  resp:"Persons are distinguished only by relations, and relations distinguish only as opposed. The Son and Spirit must therefore be related by opposed relations, which can only be relations of origin. Hence 'it must be said that the Holy Ghost is from the Son' (I q.36 a.2).",
  rep:["What belongs to the Father belongs to the Son except being Father (Jn 16:15); 'proceeds from the Father' does not exclude the Son, as 'no one knows the Father but the Son' does not exclude the Spirit.","Ephesus forbade a contrary creed, not the explanation of the same faith; Rome has authority to explain the Creed.","Father and Son are one principle of the Spirit because they share one spirative power: 'the same spirative power belongs to the Father and to the Son' (I q.36 a.3 ad 2)."]},
 deeper:`<p><strong>History.</strong> The Filioque was added in Spain (Toledo III, 589, against Arianism), spread through Frankish lands, and was accepted at Rome in the liturgy in 1014. Photius (867) and later Michael Cerularius (1054) made it a central charge. Lyons II (1274) and Florence (Laetentur Caeli, 1439) defined that the Spirit proceeds 'from the Father and the Son as from one principle and by one spiration', and accepted that the Greek Fathers' 'through the Son' means the same. Florence also declared that the Latins' insertion was 'lawful and reasonable'.</p>
<p><strong>Today.</strong> Eastern Catholic Churches may recite the Creed without the Filioque. The Pontifical Council for Promoting Christian Unity's 1995 clarification ('The Greek and Latin Traditions regarding the Procession of the Holy Spirit') distinguishes the Greek ekporeuesthai (procession from the sole source) from the Latin procedere.</p>`,
 schools:`<p>Orthodox theologians since Photius argue that the Father alone is the cause of the Spirit's existence, and that Thomas's argument from relations is a rationalist innovation. Some (Gregory of Cyprus, Gregory Palamas) accept an eternal 'manifestation' of the Spirit through the Son. Wagner has debated these points publicly (e.g. with the Orthodox apologist 'Ubi Petrus', reviewed by Jay Dyer) and wrote on St Isidore and the Filioque.</p>`,
 authors:[["Christian B. Wagner","thomism.com: 'St. Isidore and the Filioque'; Annotated Thomist: 'Whether the Holy Spirit Proceeds from the Son', 'Whether the Holy Spirit Proceeds from the Father through the Son', 'Whether the Father and the Son are One Principle of the Holy Spirit'; public debates on the Filioque","Defends the Filioque from Thomas and the Fathers, and challenges Orthodox historical claims."],["Garrigou-Lagrange","The Trinity and God the Creator, on q.36","Defends Thomas's argument that without the Filioque the Son and Spirit would not be distinct."]],
 errors:[["The Filioque makes two sources of the Spirit.","Florence: one principle, one spiration."],["The Father is not the source of the Son's spirative power.","The Son has it from the Father."]],
 scripture:[["Jn 15:26","proceedeth from the Father"],["Jn 16:13–15","he shall receive of mine; all the Father hath is mine"],["Gal 4:6","the Spirit of his Son"],["Rom 8:9","the Spirit of Christ"],["Jn 20:22","he breathed on them"]],
 tests:[["What is Thomas's main argument for the Filioque?","Without an opposed relation of origin, the Son and Spirit would not be distinct."],["How does Florence reconcile 'and the Son' with 'through the Son'?","Both mean the Spirit proceeds from the Father, and from the Son as receiving from the Father, as from one principle."]],
 read:["ST I q.36","Council of Florence, Laetentur Caeli (1439)","<a href=\"#apologetics\">Apologetics: John 15:26</a>"]},

{id:"trin-appropriation",tract:"trinity",level:2,note:"Common teaching; Florence (opera ad extra indivisa)",st:"ST I q.39 aa.7–8; q.45 a.6",
 q:"If all three persons act together, why do we call the Father 'Creator' and the Spirit 'Sanctifier'?",
 one:"All God's works outside himself are one work of the three persons; we 'appropriate' certain works and attributes to one person because they resemble that person's property, to help us know the persons; only the Incarnation belongs to the Son alone, and the indwelling is proper in a special way to all three.",
 plain:`<p>Florence teaches that the Father, Son and Spirit are 'not three principles of creation but one principle'. So why does the Creed call the Father 'Creator'? Thomas: 'For the manifestation of our faith it is fitting that the essential attributes should be appropriated to the persons' (I q.39 a.7). Power fits the Father (the unbegotten source), wisdom the Son (the Word), goodness and love the Spirit. Creation is appropriated to the Father, redemption to the Son, sanctification to the Spirit, though each is the work of all three.</p>
<p>Thomas even gives Hilary's beautiful triad: eternity to the Father, species (beauty) to the Son, 'use' (enjoyment) to the Spirit. Beauty fits the Son because it requires integrity, proportion and clarity, and the Son is the perfect, proportioned Image and the 'brightness' of the Father (I q.39 a.8).</p>`,
 analogy:["A family's gifts","A family gives a gift together, but the card says 'from Mum' because she chose it. The gift is from all of them; the attribution highlights something true about one."],
 deeper:`<p><strong>Not mere words.</strong> Appropriation has a real foundation: the attribute appropriated resembles the person's proper relation. But it is not proper: the Son is not wiser than the Father.</p>
<p><strong>The Incarnation.</strong> The act of uniting a human nature to the Word is the work of all three; but the term of the union, the person who became man, is the Son alone. Only the Son is incarnate.</p>
<p><strong>Indwelling.</strong> In grace the whole Trinity dwells in the soul (Jn 14:23). Some theologians (Petavius, Scheeben) argued for a proper, non-appropriated relation of the soul to the Holy Spirit; most Thomists hold the indwelling is common to the three but attributed to the Spirit. Pius XII (Mystici Corporis 78–80) left the question open.</p>`,
 authors:[["Christian B. Wagner","Annotated Thomist: 'Whether the Essential Names should be Appropriated to the Persons'; 'Whether the Essential Attributes are Appropriated to the Persons in a Fitting Manner by the Holy Doctors?'","Commentary on ST I q.39 aa.7–8, including Hilary's and Augustine's triads."]],
 tests:[["Is creation the work of the Father alone?","No: it is one work of the three, appropriated to the Father."],["Which work belongs to one person alone?","The Incarnation, as to its term: only the Son became man."]],
 read:["ST I q.39 aa.7–8; q.43; q.45 a.6"]}
);

// Disputed questions: the human soul (ST I qq.75–102, 118)
DQ.push(
{id:"soul-subsist",tract:"man",level:2,note:"Immortality of the soul: de fide (Lateran V, 1513)",st:"ST I q.75 aa.2, 6",
 q:"How can the soul be the form of a body and still survive death?",
 one:"Because the human soul has an activity that does not use a bodily organ (understanding universals), it has its own act of existing which it shares with the body; when the body dies, the soul keeps that existence, unlike the souls of animals, which exist only as forms of matter.",
 plain:`<p>Animals and plants have souls (the principle of life), but their souls do nothing except through bodily organs: seeing through eyes, digesting through stomachs. So when the body corrupts, the animal soul ceases. The human soul is different. 'The principle of intellectual operation which we call the soul, is a principle both incorporeal and subsistent' (I q.75 a.2). Thomas's argument: the intellect can know the natures of all bodies; but whatever knows things cannot have any of them in its own nature, 'because that which is in it naturally would impede the knowledge of anything else', as a fever-coated tongue tastes everything as bitter. So the intellect is not a body, nor does it understand through a bodily organ.</p>
<p>What acts by itself exists by itself. So the human soul has its own existence (esse), which it communicates to the body. At death the body loses that existence; the soul does not, because a thing that exists per se can be corrupted only per se, and a pure form cannot be separated from itself (I q.75 a.6).</p>`,
 analogy:["The musician and the instrument","A pianist plays through a piano; if the piano is destroyed, the playing stops but the pianist remains. Thomas's view is subtler than this Platonic picture, because the soul is not a separate thing using a body; it is the form that makes the body a living human. But the point holds for the intellect: its act is not the act of an organ, so the soul can outlast the organism."],
 art:{obj:["The soul is the form of the body; forms perish when their composites do; so the soul perishes.","Further, the soul cannot understand without phantasms, which need a body; so after death it cannot act, and what cannot act does not exist.","Further, 'the death of man and of beasts is one' (Eccl 3:19)."],
  sc:"Wisdom 3:1: 'The souls of the just are in the hand of God'; and Christ: 'fear ye not them that kill the body, and are not able to kill the soul' (Mt 10:28).",
  resp:"Whatever has existence per se can be generated or corrupted only per se. The souls of brutes are not self-subsistent, so they perish with their bodies; but the human soul is subsistent, having an operation of its own, so it cannot be corrupted by the corruption of the body; nor can it be corrupted per se, since it is a form, and 'it is impossible for a form to be separated from itself' (I q.75 a.6).",
  rep:["Material forms perish with the composite; the human soul is a subsistent form.","It understands differently after death (see 'What is the separated soul like?').","Solomon speaks in the person of the foolish, or about the body only."]},
 deeper:`<p><strong>Lateran V (1513).</strong> Against Pomponazzi's view that philosophy proves the soul mortal, the council defined that the soul is immortal and 'multiplied according to the multitude of bodies', and condemned those who say the intellectual soul is mortal or one for all men (Averroism).</p>
<p><strong>The modern argument.</strong> Feser's Immortal Souls (2024) develops the argument from the immateriality of the intellect (conceptual thought is determinate, universal and abstract; no material process is) into a full treatise, engaging cognitive science, and then argues from immateriality to incorruptibility.</p>`,
 authors:[["Edward Feser","Immortal Souls: A Treatise on Human Nature (2024); Philosophy of Mind (2007); 'Kripke, Ross and the immaterial aspects of thought' (Neo-Scholastic Essays)","Develops James Ross's argument that formal thought is determinate while physical processes are indeterminate, so intellect is immaterial, and then derives the soul's survival."],["Garrigou-Lagrange","Life Everlasting and the Immensity of the Soul (1947)","Draws out the soul's spiritual capacity for the infinite as a sign of its immortality and destiny."]],
 errors:[["Thomism is Cartesian dualism.","For Thomas the soul is the form of the body, not a separate thinking thing; the person is the union."],["The soul is naturally a person without the body.","The separated soul is not a complete person (see next question)."]],
 tests:[["Why does the animal soul perish but not the human soul?","The animal soul has no operation without an organ, so no existence of its own; the human soul has intellectual operation of its own."]],
 read:["ST I q.75","Lateran V, Apostolici Regiminis (1513)","<a href=\"#lesson-p6\">Psychology P2: brain and soul</a>"]},

{id:"soul-unity",tract:"man",level:3,note:"Soul is 'form of the body per se and essentially': de fide (Vienne 1312); unicity of substantial form: Thomist thesis",st:"ST I q.76 aa.1, 3–4",
 q:"Does a human being have one soul or several (vegetative, sensitive, rational)?",
 one:"One: the intellectual soul is the single substantial form of the human being and does all that plant and animal souls do; otherwise a human would be several things, not one.",
 plain:`<p>Plato placed different souls in different organs. Some medieval thinkers held a 'plurality of forms': a form of corporeity, a vegetative soul, a sensitive soul and a rational soul layered on top. Thomas argued for one form. If man lived by one soul, sensed by another and understood by another, he would not be one thing but several, as an aggregate; but 'that whereby primarily anything acts is a form of the thing' (I q.76 a.1), and it is one man who senses and understands. So the intellectual soul contains the powers of the lower souls virtually, the way a pentagon contains a square and a triangle.</p>
<p>The Council of Vienne (1312) defined that the rational soul is 'truly, per se and essentially the form of the human body'. It did not explicitly define the unicity of form, but Thomists see it as the best expression of that dogma.</p>`,
 analogy:["The pentagon and the triangle","A pentagon contains everything a triangle and a square have (angles, sides) and more. It is not a triangle with a square stacked on top. The human soul is like the higher figure: one form that does all a plant soul and an animal soul do, and more."],
 deeper:`<p><strong>Consequences.</strong> At death there is not a 'leftover' form of the body: the corpse is no longer a human body in the strict sense, only remains; while alive, the one soul is whole in each part of the body (I q.76 a.8). This mattered in the 13th century for the question of Christ's body in the tomb: Thomists answered that it remained united to the Word, though no longer informed by the soul (III q.50 a.2).</p>
<p><strong>For psychology.</strong> Because there is one form, mind and body are not two things interacting: every emotion has a bodily side and every thought uses the brain's images. This is why Thomism fits embodied cognitive science better than Cartesianism (P2 lesson 2).</p>`,
 schools:`<p>Franciscans (e.g. John Peckham, Richard of Middleton) and later Scotists defended a plurality of forms, at least a 'form of corporeity'. Thomas's unicity thesis was attacked at Oxford (1277, 1284). Today Thomists (Feser, Eleonore Stump, David Oderberg) defend it as the key to human unity.</p>`,
 authors:[["Edward Feser","Immortal Souls; Aristotle's Revenge (2019)","Defends hylomorphic unity against both dualism and materialism, and the unicity of substantial form."]],
 tests:[["What did Vienne define?","The rational soul is truly, per se and essentially the form of the body."]],
 read:["ST I q.76","Council of Vienne (1312)"]},

{id:"soul-origin",tract:"man",level:2,note:"Each soul created immediately by God: de fide (Lateran V; Humani Generis 36; CCC 366); traducianism condemned",st:"ST I q.90 a.2; q.118 a.2",
 q:"Where does each human soul come from? Is traducianism a heresy?",
 one:"Each spiritual soul is created directly by God and infused into the body; it cannot be passed on by the parents, because no bodily power can produce something immaterial, and Thomas calls the contrary view 'heretical'.",
 plain:`<p>Parents give their child a body; they cannot give a spiritual soul. 'It is impossible for an active power existing in matter to extend its action to the production of an immaterial effect' (I q.118 a.2). And since the soul subsists, 'to be and to be made are proper to it', so it can come to be only by creation. Thomas concludes: 'It is therefore heretical to say that the intellectual soul is transmitted with the semen' (I q.118 a.2), because that would make the soul non-subsistent and perishable.</p>
<p>The opposite view, traducianism (Tertullian; many Lutherans), held that the soul comes from the parents' souls like a cutting from a plant. Some Fathers (Augustine) hesitated, partly because it seemed to explain the transmission of original sin. The Church settled it: Pius XII taught that 'the Catholic faith obliges us to hold that souls are immediately created by God' (Humani Generis 36), and the Catechism repeats it (CCC 366).</p>`,
 analogy:["Parents and the spark","Parents prepare the lamp, oil and wick: the body. The flame of a spiritual soul cannot be lit from their own flame without diminishing it, and it is of another order. God lights each lamp directly."],
 deeper:`<p><strong>Original sin without traducianism.</strong> If the soul is created directly, how is original sin inherited? Thomas: original sin is transmitted with human nature through generation; the soul is created pure as to personal guilt but, united to a nature descended from Adam, it lacks original justice, which is the privation in which original sin consists (I-II q.81 a.1; q.83 a.1).</p>
<p><strong>A recent debate.</strong> In October 2026 Christian Wagner answered the Reformed pastor Joel Webbon, who had defended traducianism, arguing that it is not a legitimate opinion for Catholics but a heresy, as Thomas says.</p>`,
 authors:[["Christian B. Wagner","thomism.com: 'A Response to Joel Webbon: Yes, Traducianism is Heresy' (Oct 2026)","Defends from Thomas and the magisterium that traducianism contradicts the faith on the soul's spirituality and creation."]],
 errors:[["Parents make the soul.","They cooperate with God in procreation; God creates the soul."],["Creationism of the soul means evolution is false.","Humani Generis permits research on the body's origin, while affirming the immediate creation of souls."]],
 scripture:[["Eccl 12:7","the spirit returns to God who gave it"],["Zech 12:1","who formeth the spirit of man in him"],["Heb 12:9","the Father of spirits"]],
 tests:[["Why can't parents produce a soul?","A material power cannot produce an immaterial effect; a subsistent soul can come to be only by creation."]],
 read:["ST I q.90; q.118","CCC 366","Pius XII, Humani Generis 36"]},

{id:"soul-separated",tract:"man",level:3,note:"Theological conclusion",st:"ST I q.89; q.29 a.1 ad 5",
 q:"What is the soul like between death and resurrection? Is it still 'me'?",
 one:"The separated soul knows and loves, by ideas infused by God rather than by images from the senses, but it is not a complete human person, because it is naturally a part of man; hence the resurrection of the body is not an optional extra.",
 plain:`<p>In this life we understand by turning to sense images (phantasms). After death the soul has no senses. Can it understand? Thomas answers that the soul's mode of knowing follows its mode of existing: separated, it knows 'by turning to simply intelligible objects, as is proper to other separate substances'. But this is not its natural state: 'to be separated from the body is not in accordance with its nature' (I q.89 a.1). It knows, in a less natural and (for its own nature) less clear way, by ideas God infuses.</p>
<p>Is the separated soul a person? No: 'The soul is a part of the human species; and so, although it may exist in a separate state, yet since it ever retains its nature of unibility', 'neither the definition nor the name of person belongs to it' (I q.29 a.1 ad 5). In his commentary on 1 Corinthians 15 Thomas puts it strongly: 'my soul is not I'. So the hope of the Creed is not only souls in heaven but 'the resurrection of the body'.</p>`,
 analogy:["The torn photograph","Half of a photograph of you is still a picture of you, and precious; but it is not the whole picture, and it longs to be joined to the other half. The separated soul is truly the person's soul, with memory, knowledge and love, but awaits its body."],
 deeper:`<p><strong>Why we pray to the saints.</strong> Separated souls in glory see God and know in him what pertains to them, including our prayers (Wagner, 'How Do the Saints Hear Us?'). They are not 'asleep' (against 'soul sleep').</p>
<p><strong>Ghosts?</strong> Thomas allows that God may permit the souls of the dead to appear to the living for their instruction (I q.89 a.8 ad 2; Suppl. q.69 a.3), but denies that the dead naturally know or wander among the affairs of the living.</p>`,
 authors:[["Edward Feser","Immortal Souls, final part","Explains why the separated soul is incomplete, why the resurrection is fitting, and how the soul knows without phantasms."],["Christian B. Wagner","christianbwagner.com: 'How Do the Saints Hear Us?'","The saints know our prayers by seeing God, not by natural powers."],["Garrigou-Lagrange","Life Everlasting (1947)","Treats the state of the separated soul, its knowledge and the fixity of its will."]],
 errors:[["Soul sleep until the resurrection.","Contrary to Benedictus Deus (1336) and Lk 23:43."],["The real me is the soul; the body is a shell.","Thomas: the person is body and soul."]],
 scripture:[["Lk 16:19–31","the rich man and Lazarus"],["Lk 23:43","this day with me in paradise"],["2 Cor 5:1–8","absent from the body, present to the Lord"],["Rev 6:9–11","the souls under the altar"]],
 tests:[["How does the separated soul understand?","By intelligible species infused by God, not by phantasms."],["Is it a person?","No, it is a part of the human species that retains its unibility."]],
 read:["ST I q.89; q.29 a.1 ad 5","Thomas, Super I Cor. 15, lect. 2"]},

{id:"soul-embryo",tract:"man",level:3,note:"Respect from conception: Church teaching (Donum Vitae 1987; Evangelium Vitae 60); moment of ensoulment: not defined",st:"ST I q.118 a.2 ad 2; Summa Contra Gentiles II.89",
 q:"When does the human soul begin? Didn't Thomas hold 'delayed ensoulment'?",
 one:"Following Aristotle's biology, Thomas thought the rational soul came some weeks after conception; the Church has not defined the moment, but teaches that the embryo must be treated as a person from conception, and modern embryology removes the biological basis for Thomas's delay.",
 plain:`<p>Thomas believed, with the science of his day, that the embryo first lives with a vegetative soul, then a sensitive soul, and receives the rational soul when the body is sufficiently formed (I q.118 a.2 ad 2). This is 'delayed hominization'. It rested on the view that the male seed shapes inert menstrual blood into a body over weeks.</p>
<p>Modern embryology shows that from fertilisation the zygote is a new, self-organising human organism with its own genome, directing its own development. Many Thomists (e.g. Benedict Ashley, Patrick Lee, Robert George) argue that Thomas's own principles (the soul is the form of an organised body) now point to ensoulment at conception, since the organism is organised from the start. The Church teaches: 'The human being is to be respected and treated as a person from the moment of conception' (Donum Vitae I.1; CCC 2270), and Evangelium Vitae 60 says that 'the mere probability that a human person is involved would suffice to justify an absolutely clear prohibition' of killing the embryo. Abortion was condemned at every stage even when delayed ensoulment was common.</p>`,
 analogy:["The acorn","An acorn is not a 'potential oak' that becomes an oak later; it is a young oak, with everything it needs to grow. The embryo is not a potential human but a human at an early stage."],
 deeper:`<p><strong>Why this matters for a psychologist.</strong> Debates on abortion, IVF, embryo research and prenatal diagnosis often cite Thomas against the Church. The answer is that his conclusion depended on a false biology, while his principle (the rational soul is the form of a body organised for human life) supports respect from conception.</p>`,
 errors:[["Thomas would have accepted early abortion.","He condemned abortion; delayed ensoulment affected the classification of the sin, not its gravity as a grave evil (cf. In IV Sent. d.31)."]],
 tests:[["What does Donum Vitae teach?","The human being is to be respected and treated as a person from conception."]],
 read:["Donum Vitae (1987) I.1","Evangelium Vitae 60","CCC 2270–2275"]}
);

// Disputed questions: grace and justification (ST I-II qq.109–114)
DQ.push(
{id:"grace-need",tract:"grace",level:2,note:"De fide (Orange 529; Trent Session 6)",st:"ST I-II q.109",
 q:"What can a human being do without grace?",
 one:"Without grace fallen man can know some truths and do some particular natural goods, but he cannot love God above all, keep the whole moral law for long, avoid all mortal sin, prepare himself for grace, merit eternal life, or persevere: for all these he needs God's help.",
 plain:`<p>Thomas distinguishes human nature in its integrity (Adam before the fall) and in its fallen state. In both, every act needs God as first mover. But in the fallen state, 'man falls short of what he could do by his nature'; yet 'because human nature is not altogether corrupted by sin, so as to be shorn of every natural good', fallen nature 'can, by virtue of its natural endowments, work some particular good, as to build dwellings, plant vineyards, and the like' (I-II q.109 a.2). He is like a sick man who can make some movements, but not walk as a healthy man.</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table class="t"><thead><tr><th>Question in q.109</th><th>Without grace?</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Know any truth (a.1)</td><td>Yes, natural truths, with God's general motion; not supernatural truths</td></tr>
<tr><td>Will or do any good (a.2)</td><td>Some particular natural goods only</td></tr>
<tr><td>Love God above all (a.3)</td><td>Not in fallen nature</td></tr>
<tr><td>Keep all the commandments (a.4)</td><td>Not as to substance for long, and never as to the mode of charity</td></tr>
<tr><td>Merit eternal life (a.5)</td><td>No</td></tr>
<tr><td>Prepare himself for grace (a.6)</td><td>No: God must move him inwardly</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rise from sin (a.7)</td><td>No</td></tr>
<tr><td>Avoid sin (a.8)</td><td>Not all mortal sin for long</td></tr>
<tr><td>Persevere (a.10)</td><td>Not even with habitual grace, without a further special help</td></tr></tbody></table></div>`,
 analogy:["The sick man","A sick man can still move, eat a little and talk, but cannot run a race; medicine restores health, and even then he needs help to stay well. Fallen nature is wounded, not dead; grace heals it (gratia sanans) and raises it (gratia elevans)."],
 deeper:`<p><strong>Two errors.</strong> Pelagianism: man can be saved by his own powers, grace only makes it easier (condemned at Carthage 418, Orange 529). Semi-Pelagianism: the beginning of faith (initium fidei) comes from us, God completes it (condemned at Orange). At the other extreme, Luther, Baius and Jansenius held that fallen man can do nothing but sin, so every act of unbelievers is sin (condemned: Pius V 1567 against Baius; Innocent X 1653 against Jansenius). Thomas holds the middle: real wounds, real but limited natural goodness.</p>
<p><strong>Preparation for grace.</strong> Even the first stirrings toward God require that God 'moves the soul inwardly or inspires the good wish' (I-II q.109 a.6). This is actual grace. 'Convert us, O Lord, to thee, and we shall be converted' (Lam 5:21).</p>`,
 authors:[["Christian B. Wagner","Annotated Thomist on I-II q.109: 'Whether Without Grace Man can Know any Truth', '…Will or Do Any Good', '…Love God above All', '…Fulfil the Commandments', '…Merit Everlasting Life', '…Avoid Sin', '…Prepare Himself for Grace'","A complete commentary on q.109, the most important single question for the Catholic-Protestant debate on grace."],["Garrigou-Lagrange","Grace (1947): commentary on I-II qq.109–114","The standard Thomist manual on grace, with the commentators' debates."]],
 errors:[["Pelagianism: be good and you will be saved.","Grace is necessary for every salutary act."],["Total depravity: every act of an unbeliever is sin.","Condemned in Baius (prop. 25) and Jansenius."]],
 tests:[["Can fallen man do any good without grace?","Some particular natural goods, not salutary acts."],["What heresy says the beginning of faith is from us?","Semi-Pelagianism (condemned at Orange 529)."]],
 read:["ST I-II q.109","Council of Orange (529)","<a href=\"#lesson-t6\">Academy T6</a>"]},

{id:"grace-what",tract:"grace",level:2,note:"Sanctifying grace an inherent quality: theologically certain (Trent Session 6 ch. 7, can. 11)",st:"ST I-II q.110",
 q:"What is sanctifying grace? Is it something in me, or only God's favour?",
 one:"Sanctifying grace is a created quality God infuses into the essence of the soul, a real participation in the divine nature that makes us children of God; it is the effect of God's uncreated love, not a substitute for it.",
 plain:`<p>'Grace' can mean favour ('in the king's good graces'), a free gift, or gratitude. When God loves someone, his love does not find goodness in them; it causes it: 'there is understood to be an effect of God's gratuitous will in whoever is said to have God's grace' (I-II q.110 a.2). So grace is not only God thinking well of us; it changes us.</p>
<p>It changes us in two ways: by movements (actual graces, God moving us to know or will) and by a 'habitual gift… infused by God into the soul'. Since God 'provides for natural creatures… forms and powers' to act in harmony with their ends, 'it is not fitting that God should provide less for those He loves, that they may acquire supernatural good' (I-II q.110 a.2). Habitual grace is that new nature: a quality in the very essence of the soul (a.4) from which flow the infused virtues in the powers, as powers flow from the soul. It is a participation in the divine nature (2 Pet 1:4).</p>`,
 analogy:["Iron in the fire","Iron placed in fire glows, burns and gives light, not because it has become fire, but because it shares the fire's properties. The soul in grace shares God's life, knows and loves as God does, without ceasing to be a creature."],
 deeper:`<p><strong>Created and uncreated grace.</strong> Uncreated grace is God himself, dwelling in the soul; created grace is the effect in the soul that makes the indwelling possible as a new relation of knowledge and love. Some Orthodox critics (following Palamas) object that 'created grace' makes grace a mere creature. Thomists reply that created grace is the created term of God's uncreated self-gift, not a substitute for it (see 'East, West and the Reformation').</p>
<p><strong>Trent.</strong> Trent teaches that justifying grace is 'poured forth in the hearts' and 'inheres' in them (Session 6, can. 11), against the view that justification is only the external imputation of Christ's justice.</p>`,
 authors:[["Christian B. Wagner","thomism.com: 'Does Created Grace Prove Eastern…'; 'How Does Sacramental Grace Differ…'; Annotated Thomist: 'Whether Grace is in the Essence of the Soul as in a Subject or in One of the Powers'; 'Whether Grace is the Same as Virtue'","Explains the Thomist doctrine of habitual grace as a quality in the soul's essence and answers Orthodox objections to 'created grace'."],["Garrigou-Lagrange","Grace; The Three Ages of the Interior Life ch. 1–5","Sanctifying grace as 'the seed of glory' (semen gloriae): the same life that will flower in the beatific vision."]],
 errors:[["Grace is only God's attitude to me, nothing in me.","Trent, Session 6, can. 11."],["Grace makes me divine by nature.","It is participation, not identity."]],
 scripture:[["2 Pet 1:4","partakers of the divine nature"],["Rom 5:5","the charity of God is poured forth in our hearts"],["1 Jn 3:1–2","we are now the sons of God"],["Jn 14:23","we will come to him, and make our abode with him"]],
 tests:[["Where does sanctifying grace reside?","In the essence of the soul, from which the infused virtues flow into the powers."]],
 read:["ST I-II q.110","CCC 1996–2005"]},

{id:"grace-kinds",tract:"grace",level:2,note:"Common teaching",st:"ST I-II q.111",
 q:"What are the kinds of grace (sanctifying, actual, operating, cooperating, charismatic)?",
 one:"Grace that makes us pleasing to God (sanctifying) differs from graces given for others' benefit (charisms); and God's help is 'operating' when he alone moves us and 'cooperating' when we move with him; the same grace is 'prevenient' as it goes before and 'subsequent' as it follows.",
 plain:`<div class="tablewrap"><table class="t"><thead><tr><th>Division</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Gratia gratum faciens / gratis data</td><td>Grace making the receiver pleasing to God / gifts given for the good of others</td><td>Sanctifying grace / prophecy, healing, tongues (1 Cor 12)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Habitual / actual</td><td>A permanent quality / a passing movement</td><td>The state of grace / a sudden inspiration to pray</td></tr>
<tr><td>Operating / cooperating</td><td>God alone moves / we move as moved by God</td><td>Conversion of the will's first act / carrying out the good resolution</td></tr>
<tr><td>Prevenient / subsequent</td><td>The same grace as it precedes or follows an effect</td><td>Healing the soul / then willing good, doing it, persevering, reaching glory</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sufficient / efficacious</td><td>Gives the real power / gives the act itself</td><td>See next question</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>Thomas on operating and cooperating: 'in that effect in which our mind is moved and does not move, but in which God is the sole mover, the operation is attributed to God… \"operating grace.\" But in that effect in which our mind both moves and is moved… \"cooperating grace\"' (I-II q.111 a.2).</p>
<p>Sanctifying grace is far nobler than any charism, since it unites the person himself to God; charisms only dispose others (I-II q.111 a.5; 1 Cor 13: 'if I have not charity, I am nothing').</p>`,
 analogy:["The child learning to walk","A parent first lifts the child to its feet (operating grace), then walks holding its hand as the child moves its own legs (cooperating grace). Both are the parent's help; in the second the child truly walks."],
 authors:[["Christian B. Wagner","Annotated Thomist: 'Whether Grace is Fittingly Divided into Sanctifying Grace and Gratuitous Grace'; '…into Operating and Cooperating Grace'; '…into Prevenient and Subsequent Grace'; 'Whether Gratuitous Grace is Rightly Divided by the Apostle'; 'Whether Gratuitous Grace is Nobler than Sanctifying Grace'","Commentary on each article of I-II q.111."]],
 errors:[["Charisms prove holiness.","Charisms can be given to sinners (Mt 7:22–23); holiness is charity."]],
 tests:[["Which is nobler, sanctifying grace or a charism?","Sanctifying grace, which unites the person to God."]],
 read:["ST I-II q.111","CCC 2000–2005"]},

{id:"grace-efficacious",tract:"grace",level:3,note:"Existence of sufficient and efficacious grace: theologically certain; their explanation: school dispute",st:"ST I-II q.109–112; I q.19 a.6",
 q:"Why does grace work in one person and not another? Sufficient and efficacious grace",
 one:"God gives everyone sufficient grace, a real power to do good; efficacious grace is grace that actually produces the good act; Thomists say its efficacy comes from God's own motion, Molinists from God's foreknowledge of our free consent, and the Church allows both.",
 plain:`<p>Two truths must be held together. First, God wills all to be saved (1 Tim 2:4) and gives each person grace truly sufficient to avoid sin and be saved; if anyone is lost, it is his own fault. Second, when we actually do good, the doing is itself God's gift: 'what hast thou that thou hast not received?' (1 Cor 4:7). So there is grace that gives the power (sufficient) and grace that gives the act (efficacious).</p>
<p>Why does one person consent and another not? For Thomists, the difference in the end is God's love (the 'principle of predilection': no one would be better than another unless more loved by God), while the refusal is entirely the creature's fault. For Molinists, God foresees, by middle knowledge, how each would respond to each grace, and gives grace 'congruous' to those he will save.</p>`,
 analogy:["Blossom and fruit","Garrigou-Lagrange: sufficient grace is offered to us 'as the blossom', efficacious grace 'as the fruit'; if we resist the blossom, we deserve to be deprived of the fruit. The fault of the resistance is ours; the fruit, when it comes, is God's gift."],
 deeper:`<p><strong>The Thomist reasoning.</strong> Resisting grace is a defect, and defects need no positive cause beyond the creature's deficient will. Doing good is a perfection, and every perfection comes ultimately from God. Therefore good acts are attributed to God first, evil acts to the creature first. Thomists add that God's 'permissive decree' (not to give efficacious grace) follows the creature's resistance to sufficient grace in the order of nature.</p>
<p><strong>The Church's position.</strong> Trent (Session 6, can. 4) condemns the view that free will moved by God cannot dissent if it wishes; this rules out irresistible grace in the Calvinist sense. The 1607 decision of Paul V left Thomists and Molinists free. Benedict XIV (1748) confirmed that the Thomist, Augustinian and Molinist schools may all be taught.</p>`,
 schools:`<p><strong>Thomists (Báñez, Lemos, Alvarez, Billuart, Garrigou-Lagrange):</strong> efficacious grace is intrinsically efficacious by physical premotion. <strong>Molinists (Molina) and Congruists (Suárez, Bellarmine):</strong> efficacy is extrinsic, from God's middle knowledge of free responses. <strong>Augustinians (Noris, Berti):</strong> efficacy from 'victorious delight'. <strong>Marín-Sola:</strong> sufficient graces are 'impedible' motions that bear fruit unless impeded. All are permitted.</p>`,
 authors:[["Garrigou-Lagrange","Grace (1947); Predestination; The Three Ages of the Interior Life","The classic Thomist exposition, with the image of blossom and fruit and the 'principle of predilection'."],["Christian B. Wagner","thomism.com: 'On Limbo and Sufficient Grace'; 'On Calvinism and Thomism'","Applies the doctrine of sufficient grace to limbo and to the comparison with Calvinism."]],
 errors:[["Irresistible grace (Calvin).","Trent, Session 6, can. 4."],["God gives grace only to the elect.","God gives sufficient grace to all (Innocent X against Jansenius, prop. 5: 'Christ died only for the predestined' condemned)."]],
 tests:[["What does Trent say against irresistible grace?","That the will moved by God can dissent if it wishes (Session 6, can. 4)."],["Who may teach intrinsic efficacy and who extrinsic?","Thomists and Molinists alike (Paul V, 1607)."]],
 read:["Trent, Session 6","Garrigou-Lagrange, Grace, ch. on efficacious grace"]},

{id:"grace-justification",tract:"grace",level:3,note:"De fide (Trent Session 6)",st:"ST I-II q.113",
 q:"What happens when a sinner is justified? Imputation or infusion?",
 one:"Justification is not only the forgiveness of sins but the inner renewal of the person by sanctifying grace and charity; its one formal cause is 'the justice of God, not that by which he himself is just, but that by which he makes us just'; Christ's merits are truly credited to us, but they make us inwardly just, not merely declared just.",
 plain:`<p>Thomas lists four things in the justification of a sinner: the infusion of grace; the movement of free will toward God by faith; the movement of free will against sin; and the remission of guilt (I-II q.113 aa.6, 8). These happen in an instant (a.7), though preparation may take time.</p>
<p>Trent's decree (1547) gives the Catholic summary: justification 'is not remission of sins merely, but also the sanctification and renewal of the inward man, through the voluntary reception of the grace and of the gifts'. Its causes: final (God's glory and eternal life), efficient (God's mercy), meritorious (Christ's Passion), instrumental (baptism), and 'the single formal cause is the justice of God, not that by which he himself is just, but that by which he makes us just' (Session 6, ch. 7).</p>
<p>Thomas calls this God's greatest work in one sense: 'the justification of the ungodly, which terminates at the eternal good of a share in the Godhead, is greater than the creation of heaven and earth' (I-II q.113 a.9).</p>`,
 analogy:["Pardon versus healing","A judge can pardon a criminal while leaving him unchanged; a doctor heals a patient. Luther's justification looks like a pardon covering a sinner who remains inwardly unchanged ('snow over a dunghill' is a later image). Catholic justification is both pardon and healing: God forgives by making the sinner just."],
 deeper:`<p><strong>Imputation: the Catholic 'yes, but'.</strong> Catholics do hold that Christ's merits are imputed, credited to us; Trent says the justified are made just 'by the merits of Christ communicated to them'. What Catholics reject is that imputation is the formal cause of justification, as though we remained inwardly unjust and were only declared just. Thomas Stapleton (16th c.) argued this against the Reformers; Wagner has written that Catholics believe in imputation, but not as the Reformers meant it.</p>
<p><strong>Faith, hope and charity.</strong> Faith is 'the beginning, foundation and root of all justification' (Trent ch. 8), but faith justifies as living faith, formed by charity (Gal 5:6; Jas 2:24).</p>
<p><strong>Increase and loss.</strong> Justification can grow by good works (Trent ch. 10) and be lost by any mortal sin, not only unbelief (ch. 15), and regained by penance (ch. 14).</p>`,
 schools:`<p>Within Catholic theology, Augustinian and Thomist accounts both affirm inherent grace. The 1999 Joint Declaration with the Lutheran World Federation found 'consensus in basic truths' while listing remaining differences (e.g. 'simul iustus et peccator', the role of concupiscence). Wagner's essays on Augustinianism and justification and on the formal cause engage these debates.</p>`,
 authors:[["Christian B. Wagner","thomism.com: 'On Justification and Its Formal Cause'; 'On Justification and Imputation'; 'Yes, Catholics Believe in Imputation—but…'; 'Augustinianism and Justification'; 'Thomism on Justification and Imputation: Stapleton against the Protestants'; Annotated Thomist on I-II q.113","Explains Trent's single formal cause, how Catholics can speak of imputation, and why the Reformers' forensic justification fails."],["Garrigou-Lagrange","Grace, on q.113","Commentary on the four elements of justification and its instantaneous character."]],
 errors:[["Justification is only a legal declaration (forensic).","Trent, Session 6, ch. 7 and can. 11."],["Once justified, always saved.","Trent, Session 6, ch. 15, can. 23."]],
 scripture:[["Rom 3:21–26","justified freely by his grace"],["Rom 5:5","charity poured forth"],["Titus 3:5–7","the laver of regeneration and renovation"],["Jas 2:24","by works a man is justified, and not by faith only"]],
 tests:[["What is the 'single formal cause' of justification for Trent?","The justice of God by which he makes us just."],["Do Catholics believe in imputation at all?","Yes, that Christ's merits are credited to us; but not as the formal cause in place of inner renewal."]],
 read:["ST I-II q.113","Trent, Session 6","CCC 1987–1995","<a href=\"#apologetics\">Apologetics: Romans 3:28</a>"]},

{id:"grace-merit",tract:"grace",level:2,note:"Merit of the justified: de fide (Trent Session 6, can. 32)",st:"ST I-II q.114",
 q:"Can we merit anything from God?",
 one:"Not in strict justice by our own power, since we owe God everything; but God freely ordained that acts done in grace by the Holy Spirit should merit eternal life 'condignly', while the first grace and final perseverance can never be merited.",
 plain:`<p>Merit means a right to a reward. Between equals there is strict justice; between God and man there is infinite inequality, so 'there is no absolute… justice' (I-II q.114 a.1), only a justice based on God's own ordination. God freely decided to reward our works done in grace.</p>
<p>Thomas distinguishes: considered as coming from our free will, a good work merits only 'congruously' (it is fitting that God reward one who does what he can); considered as coming from 'the grace of the Holy Ghost moving us to life everlasting, it is meritorious of life everlasting condignly' (I-II q.114 a.3). So the Council of Trent can say that God's gifts are also our merits (Session 6, ch. 16, quoting Augustine).</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table class="t"><thead><tr><th>Can I merit…</th><th>Answer (q.114)</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Eternal life, in grace</td><td>Yes, condignly (a.3)</td></tr>
<tr><td>The first grace</td><td>No: 'if by grace, it is not now by works' (a.5)</td></tr>
<tr><td>The first grace for another</td><td>Only congruously (a.6), e.g. by prayer, as Monica for Augustine</td></tr>
<tr><td>Restoration after a future fall</td><td>No, neither condignly nor congruously (a.7)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Increase of grace and charity</td><td>Yes, condignly (a.8)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Final perseverance</td><td>No; we must pray for it (a.9)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>`,
 analogy:["The father's allowance","A father gives his child money, then rewards the child for buying him a gift with it. The child truly gave a gift and truly earns praise, yet everything came from the father. 'Crowning your merits, you crown your own gifts' (Augustine, Preface for Saints)."],
 deeper:`<p><strong>Why perseverance cannot be merited.</strong> Merit depends on the motion of grace; perseverance is the continuation of that motion to the end, so it is its principle, not its term. It is therefore a 'great gift' (Trent, Session 6, can. 16) to be begged daily, especially in the Our Father and the Hail Mary's 'at the hour of our death'.</p>
<p><strong>Can Protestants agree?</strong> Many fear 'merit' means earning salvation. Thomas's answer shows it does not: no one merits the first grace, and all merit is founded on God's free promise and the Spirit's action. The difference with the Reformation is whether God's gift really becomes ours so that our works, as his gifts, are rewarded.</p>`,
 authors:[["Christian B. Wagner","Annotated Thomist on all ten articles of I-II q.114 ('Whether a Man may Merit Anything from God', '…Merit for Himself the First Grace', '…Merit the First Grace for Another', '…Merit Restoration after a Fall', '…in Grace can Merit Eternal Life Condignly'); thomism.com: 'On the Justice of God and Our Merit'","A detailed commentary on the whole question on merit."],["Garrigou-Lagrange","Grace, on q.114","The classic Thomist exposition of condign and congruous merit."]],
 errors:[["Merit means earning salvation by my own effort.","Merit presupposes grace and God's free ordination."],["Good works add nothing (Reformation).","Trent, Session 6, can. 32."]],
 tests:[["Can I merit the first grace?","No."],["Can I merit perseverance?","No; I must pray for it."]],
 read:["ST I-II q.114","Trent, Session 6, ch. 16, can. 32","CCC 2006–2011"]},

{id:"grace-certainty",tract:"grace",level:1,note:"De fide (Trent Session 6, ch. 9, can. 13–14)",st:"ST I-II q.112 a.5",
 q:"Can I know for certain that I am in a state of grace?",
 one:"Not with the certainty of faith, unless God reveals it; but we can know it conjecturally from signs (delight in God, contempt of worldly things, no awareness of mortal sin), which is enough for peace and confidence.",
 plain:`<p>Thomas gives three ways of knowing. By special revelation: God sometimes reveals it, 'as when it was said to Paul… \"My grace is sufficient for thee\"' (I-II q.112 a.5). By certain knowledge from principles: no, since the principle of grace is God himself, who is hidden. By signs, conjecturally: yes, 'when he is conscious of delighting in God, and of despising worldly things, and inasmuch as a man is not conscious of any mortal sin' (I-II q.112 a.5).</p>
<p>Trent teaches that 'no one can know with the certainty of faith, which cannot be subject to error, that he has obtained the grace of God' (Session 6, ch. 9), against the Reformers' requirement of certainty of one's own justification. This is not a counsel of anxiety: we can be confident in God's mercy, faithful to the sacraments, and at peace. Paul: 'I am not conscious to myself of any thing, yet am I not hereby justified; but he that judgeth me, is the Lord' (1 Cor 4:4).</p>`,
 analogy:["A good marriage","A spouse does not have mathematical proof of being loved, but has many signs and lives in confident trust. That trust is not doubt; it is the right kind of certainty for a relationship."],
 deeper:`<p><strong>Pastoral use.</strong> For the scrupulous, Thomas's 'signs' are a relief: if I am not conscious of mortal sin, have confessed what I knew, and desire God, I may receive Communion with confidence (see the psychology track on scrupulosity, P1 lesson 5).</p>`,
 authors:[["Christian B. Wagner","Annotated Thomist: 'Whether Man can Know that He has Grace'; thomism.com: 'Is My Confession Invalid?'; 'On the Contrition Necessary for a…'","Helps readers avoid both presumption and scrupulosity by Thomas's three ways of knowing."]],
 errors:[["Assurance of salvation by faith alone.","Trent, Session 6, can. 13–15."],["Constant anxiety is a sign of holiness.","Scrupulosity is a disorder of conscience, not humility."]],
 tests:[["What three ways of knowing grace does Thomas list?","Revelation; certain knowledge from principles (impossible); conjecture from signs."]],
 read:["ST I-II q.112 a.5","Trent, Session 6, ch. 9, 12"]}
);

// Disputed questions: Christ and redemption (ST III qq.1–59)
DQ.push(
{id:"christ-why",tract:"christ",level:2,note:"Free theological opinion (Thomists vs Scotists)",st:"ST III q.1 a.3",
 q:"Would God have become man if Adam had never sinned?",
 one:"Thomas thinks it 'more in accordance' with Scripture to say the Incarnation was ordained as a remedy for sin, while granting that God could have become incarnate anyway; the Scotists hold Christ was willed first, as the summit of creation, regardless of sin; the Church leaves both open.",
 plain:`<p>Thomas reasons from revelation: things that depend purely on God's will 'can be made known to us only through being revealed in the Sacred Scripture', and 'everywhere in the Sacred Scripture the sin of the first man is assigned as the reason of Incarnation'. So 'it is more in accordance with this to say that the work of Incarnation was ordained by God as a remedy for sin; so that, had sin not existed, Incarnation would not have been. And yet the power of God is not limited to this; even had sin not existed, God could have become incarnate' (III q.1 a.3).</p>
<p>The Creed agrees: 'for us men and for our salvation he came down from heaven'. The Exsultet sings 'O happy fault (felix culpa), that merited so great a Redeemer!'</p>`,
 analogy:["The doctor and the sick","Scripture always presents the Son as coming like a doctor to the sick (Mt 9:12). Thomas reads that literally: the doctor came because we were ill. The Scotist answers that the King was always going to visit his kingdom, and the illness determined only that he would come as a physician who suffers."],
 deeper:`<p><strong>The Scotist thesis.</strong> Duns Scotus argued that God, who wills in an orderly way, must first will the greatest good, the glory of Christ, before foreseeing sin; so Christ is the first-willed (primacy of Christ) and the Incarnation was not occasioned by sin. Col 1:15–17 ('firstborn of every creature… all things were created by him and in him') is their key text. Francis de Sales and many modern theologians favour it.</p>
<p><strong>The Thomist reply.</strong> Thomists distinguish: in the order of execution, Christ is indeed first and the end of creation, and everything is for him; but the actual decree of the Incarnation, as revealed, is redemptive. Some Thomists (e.g. Cajetan, Garrigou-Lagrange) developed a synthesis: God willed the whole order 'Christ redeeming' in one decree, permitting sin for the greater good of the Redeemer. Wagner has written on 'Thomistic primacy of Christ' and Thomas's 'Cur Deus Homo'.</p>`,
 authors:[["Christian B. Wagner","christianbwagner.com: 'Thomistic Primacy of Christ'; 'St. Thomas Aquinas: Cur Deus Homo'","Shows how Thomists affirm Christ's primacy in the order of ends without adopting the Scotist thesis that the Incarnation would have happened without sin."],["Garrigou-Lagrange","Christ the Saviour (1945); Our Saviour and His Love for Us","Defends Thomas's view with the 'single decree' synthesis."]],
 tests:[["What is Thomas's main reason?","Scripture everywhere gives sin as the reason for the Incarnation; God's will is known only by revelation."]],
 read:["ST III q.1 aa.1–3","Col 1:15–20"]},

{id:"christ-esse",tract:"christ",level:3,note:"One person: de fide (Ephesus, Chalcedon); one esse: Thomist thesis, debated",st:"ST III q.17 a.2",
 q:"Does Christ have one act of existence or two?",
 one:"In the Summa Thomas teaches that Christ has one being (esse) because he is one person, the Word, whose eternal existence is communicated to the human nature; a passage in De unione Verbi seems to allow a secondary human esse, and Thomists still debate how to read it.",
 plain:`<p>Two natures, one person: that is Chalcedon (451). Thomas draws a consequence. 'Being pertains both to the nature and to the hypostasis; to the hypostasis as to that which has being—and to the nature as to that whereby it has being.' Things that belong to the nature are two in Christ (two wills, two intellects, two operations). But 'the being which belongs to the very hypostasis or person in itself cannot possibly be multiplied in one hypostasis or person, since it is impossible that there should not be one being for one thing' (III q.17 a.2). The human nature does not have its own personal existence; it exists by the eternal existence of the Word.</p>
<p>Why does this matter? If Christ's humanity had its own existence as a complete subsisting thing, it would be a human person, and we would have two persons, which is Nestorianism.</p>`,
 analogy:["The graft","A branch grafted onto a tree no longer lives by its own root but by the tree's life, yet remains truly a branch of its kind. Christ's human nature is real and complete as nature, but it 'lives' by the existence of the Word, not by its own. (All analogies fail: the Word is not a tree, and the union is not physical.)"],
 deeper:`<p><strong>The De unione problem.</strong> In the disputed question De unione Verbi incarnati a.4 (c. 1272), Thomas says there is a 'secondary esse' of the human nature, 'not principal'. Some Thomists (Cajetan, Capreolus, Garrigou-Lagrange) hold the Summa's one-esse doctrine as definitive; others (e.g. some recent scholars) read De unione as a development. Many reconcile them: the human nature has a secondary actuation that is not a personal existence.</p>
<p><strong>Subsistence.</strong> A related school dispute: what makes a nature a person ('subsistence')? Cajetan: a 'substantial mode' terminating the nature; Capreolus: the act of existence itself; Scotus: mere negation (not being assumed). Wagner's 'Thomistic Christology and Subsistence' surveys this.</p>`,
 schools:`<p>Cajetan's 'mode of subsistence', Capreolus's 'esse' view, Suárez's 'mode' view, and Scotus's 'negation' view all try to explain why Christ's human nature is not a person. All affirm one divine person. The one-esse thesis is common among Thomists but not a dogma.</p>`,
 authors:[["Christian B. Wagner","thomism.com: 'Whether Christ has One or Two Esse'; 'Thomistic Christology and Subsistence'","Examines Thomas's texts and the commentators on the esse of Christ and the theory of subsistence."],["Garrigou-Lagrange","Christ the Saviour (commentary on III qq.1–26)","Defends the one esse as the Thomist safeguard of the unity of Christ's person."]],
 errors:[["Nestorianism: two persons morally joined.","Condemned at Ephesus (431)."],["Monophysitism: one nature after the union.","Condemned at Chalcedon (451)."],["Monothelitism: one will in Christ.","Condemned at Constantinople III (681)."]],
 tests:[["What is multiplied in Christ and what is not?","What belongs to the natures (intellect, will, operation) is two; what belongs to the person (being as subsisting) is one."]],
 read:["ST III q.17; q.2","Chalcedon (451)","<a href=\"#lesson-t11\">Academy T11</a>"]},

{id:"christ-knowledge",tract:"christ",level:3,note:"Christ's human knowledge free from error and ignorance: Church teaching (CCC 473–474; Holy Office 1918; Mystici Corporis 75)",st:"ST III qq.9–12",
 q:"Did Jesus know everything? What about 'nor the Son' (Mark 13:32)?",
 one:"Christ as God knows all things; in his human mind Thomas attributes three kinds of knowledge: the beatific vision, infused knowledge, and knowledge acquired by experience; 'nor the Son' means he did not know the day in order to reveal it, not that he was ignorant.",
 plain:`<p>Christ is one person with two natures, so two minds: the divine intellect (omniscient) and a human intellect (finite). Thomas holds that Christ's human soul had:</p>
<ul><li><strong>Beatific knowledge</strong>: from conception his soul saw the divine essence, because he leads us to glory: 'men are brought to this end of beatitude by the humanity of Christ' (III q.9 a.2). In the Word he knew 'all things existing in whatever time, and the thoughts of men, of which He is the Judge' (III q.10 a.2).</li>
<li><strong>Infused knowledge</strong>: ideas given by God, like the angels' (q.11).</li>
<li><strong>Acquired knowledge</strong>: learned through experience; this grew (Lk 2:52), which Thomas came to affirm in the Summa, revising his earlier view (q.12 a.2).</li></ul>
<p>Mark 13:32: 'of that day or hour no man knoweth… nor the Son'. Thomas, with the Fathers, answers: 'He is said, therefore, not to know the day and the hour of the Judgment, for that He does not make it known, since, on being asked by the apostles (Acts 1:7), He was unwilling to reveal it' (III q.10 a.2 ad 1). He did not know it as something to be communicated. The Catechism: 'what he admitted to not knowing in this area, he elsewhere declared himself not sent to reveal' (CCC 474).</p>`,
 analogy:["The ambassador's brief","An ambassador may truly know his government's plans yet say 'that is not a matter I can disclose', meaning: it is not part of my mission to make it known. 'I do not know' can mean 'I do not know it as something to tell you'."],
 deeper:`<p><strong>Magisterium.</strong> The Holy Office (1918) said it cannot be safely taught that Christ's soul lacked the beatific vision during his earthly life. Pius XII (Mystici Corporis 75) taught that from the first moment of the Incarnation Christ enjoyed the beatific vision and knew each member of his Body. CCC 473–474 affirm that his human knowledge expressed his divine life and that he 'knew fully the eternal plans he had come to reveal'.</p>
<p><strong>Faith and suffering.</strong> If Christ saw God, did he have faith? Thomas says no: faith is of things not seen, and Christ saw (III q.7 a.3). Then how could he suffer anguish in Gethsemane? Because the vision was in the 'higher part' of his soul and did not flow over into his sensitive powers, by a special dispensation, so that he could truly suffer (III q.46 a.8).</p>`,
 schools:`<p>Since the mid-20th century some theologians (Rahner, Galot, Jean Galot's critics) proposed that Christ had an 'immediate vision' or consciousness of sonship rather than the full beatific vision, to preserve his growth and faith. Thomists (Thomas Joseph White, Garrigou-Lagrange) defend the beatific vision as necessary for Christ to reveal the Father and know his mission.</p>`,
 authors:[["Garrigou-Lagrange","Christ the Saviour; Our Saviour and His Love for Us","Defends the beatific vision in Christ as the root of his love for each person in his Passion."],["Christian B. Wagner","christianbwagner.com: 'How Christ Endured All Passions'; 'Christ had to be Tempted in the Desert'; 'The Greatness of the Pain of Christ in the Passion'","Explains how Christ's knowledge and vision coexisted with true suffering and temptation."]],
 errors:[["Kenoticism: the Son emptied himself of divine knowledge.","The divine nature cannot be emptied; Phil 2:7 speaks of taking the form of a servant."],["Christ was mistaken about the end of the world.","Contrary to CCC 474 and the Holy Office (1918)."]],
 scripture:[["Mk 13:32","nor the Son"],["Lk 2:52","Jesus advanced in wisdom and age"],["Jn 2:24–25","he knew all men"],["Jn 21:17","Lord, thou knowest all things"],["Col 2:3","in whom are hid all the treasures of wisdom"]],
 tests:[["Name Christ's three kinds of human knowledge.","Beatific, infused, acquired."],["How does Thomas read Mk 13:32?","He did not know it so as to reveal it."]],
 read:["ST III qq.9–12","CCC 471–474"]},

{id:"christ-sin",tract:"christ",level:2,note:"Christ sinless: de fide; impeccable: common teaching",st:"ST III q.15 a.1; q.41",
 q:"Could Christ have sinned? If not, how was he really tempted?",
 one:"Christ had no sin and could not sin, because his actions are the actions of a divine person and his soul saw God; but he was truly tempted from outside, by the devil and by suffering, in order to conquer for us and give us an example.",
 plain:`<p>Hebrews says Christ was 'tempted in all things like as we are, without sin' (4:15). Thomas: he took our defects 'that He might satisfy for us, that He might prove the truth of His human nature, and that He might become an example of virtue to us', but 'by reason of these three things He ought not to have assumed the defect of sin' (III q.15 a.1). Sin does not satisfy; it does not belong to human nature as God made it; it is no example.</p>
<p>Could he have sinned? Every act of Christ is the act of the divine person; a divine person cannot sin. Moreover, his soul enjoyed the vision of God, which fixes the will in good. So he was impeccable. But temptation does not require the possibility of falling inwardly; it requires a real external solicitation and real hardship. Thomas distinguishes temptation from outside (the devil, the world) from temptation from within (concupiscence). Christ had no concupiscence (the 'fomes'), so his temptations were only from without (III q.41 a.1).</p>`,
 analogy:["The fortress and the siege","A siege is real: the walls are attacked, the defenders suffer hunger and exhaustion. A fortress that cannot fall still endures a true siege. Christ's temptation in the desert was a real siege, endured to the end, by one who could not be taken."],
 deeper:`<p><strong>Why be tempted?</strong> To strengthen us against temptation, to warn that no one is exempt, to give an example of how to resist (with Scripture), and to fill us with confidence in his mercy (III q.41 a.1). Wagner's Lenten series ('Christ had to be Tempted in the Desert', 'On the Fittingness of Christ's Temptation') follows this article.</p>
<p><strong>Did he feel the pull?</strong> Christ had real human passions, but all subject to reason: 'propassions', as Jerome calls them, which begin but do not override reason (III q.15 a.4). He felt fear, sorrow, anger, tiredness, without any disorder.</p>`,
 authors:[["Christian B. Wagner","christianbwagner.com: 'Christ had to be Tempted in the Desert'; 'On the Fittingness of Christ's Temptation'; 'How Christ Endured All Passions'","Commentary on III q.41 and q.15, applied in Lenten meditations."]],
 errors:[["Christ had to be able to sin to be truly human.","Sin is not part of human nature but a wound in it; the saints in heaven are fully human and cannot sin."]],
 tests:[["What kind of temptation could Christ have?","Only external, not from concupiscence."]],
 read:["ST III q.15; q.41"]},

{id:"christ-passion",tract:"christ",level:3,note:"Christ's death as satisfaction and sacrifice: de fide (Trent Session 6, ch. 7; Session 22); 'penal substitution' as a term: debated",st:"ST III qq.46–49",
 q:"How exactly does Christ's Passion save us? Is it 'penal substitution'?",
 one:"Thomas lists five ways: merit, satisfaction (atonement), sacrifice, redemption and efficient causality; Christ freely took on himself the punishment due to our sins, offering God love greater than the offence, so that the Father did not need to vent anger on an innocent victim but accepted the perfect offering of his Son.",
 plain:`<div class="tablewrap"><table class="t"><thead><tr><th>Mode (III q.48)</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Merit (a.1)</td><td>Christ, head of the Church, merits salvation for his members, as a head acts for the body</td></tr>
<tr><td>Satisfaction (a.2)</td><td>'He properly atones for an offense who offers something which the offended one loves equally, or even more than he detested the offense'; Christ's loving obedience 'gave more to God than was required to compensate for the offense of the whole human race'</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sacrifice (a.3)</td><td>'Christ's Passion was a true sacrifice': a voluntary offering 'most acceptable to God, as coming from charity'</td></tr>
<tr><td>Redemption (a.4)</td><td>A ransom freeing us from the slavery of sin and the debt of punishment</td></tr>
<tr><td>Efficiency (a.6)</td><td>Christ's humanity is the instrument of the Godhead, so his Passion actively causes grace</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>Was Christ punished in our place? Thomas says 'one satisfies for another's sin by taking on himself the punishment due to the sin of the other' (III q.14 a.1). Yet the Father did not torment an unwilling innocent: 'It is indeed a wicked and cruel act to hand over an innocent man to torment and to death against his will. Yet God the Father did not so deliver up Christ, but inspired Him with the will to suffer for us' (III q.47 a.3 ad 1). Both God's 'severity' and 'goodness' shine: he would not remit sin without penalty, yet he himself provided the satisfaction.</p>`,
 analogy:["The friend who pays the fine","A friend who pays your fine does not become guilty of your crime, and the judge does not hate the friend. The judge honours justice, and the friend shows extraordinary love. The analogy limps: Christ's payment is not money but self-gift, and the judge and the friend are, in the Trinity, one God."],
 deeper:`<p><strong>Penal substitution, Catholic and Protestant.</strong> Christian Wagner has argued (thomism.com, October 2026) that a scholastic form of penal substitution is Catholic doctrine: Christ truly bore the penalty (poena) due to sin, as Isaiah 53 says ('the Lord hath laid on him the iniquity of us all'), as satisfaction. What Catholics reject is a version in which the Father pours out personal wrath on the Son, the Son is personally reckoned guilty, or the Son suffers the pains of the damned. Christ offered satisfaction as one beloved, never as one hated; and his merits are communicated to us not only by legal imputation but by grace (see Justification).</p>
<p><strong>Anselm and Thomas.</strong> Anselm's Cur Deus Homo argued that satisfaction was necessary. Thomas softens this: God could have forgiven without satisfaction, but the Passion was most fitting, since it shows both justice and mercy and gives us the example of love (III q.46 aa.1–3).</p>
<p><strong>The effects (q.49).</strong> Delivery from sin, from the devil's power, from the debt of punishment, reconciliation with God, the opening of heaven, and Christ's exaltation.</p>`,
 schools:`<p>Modern theologians (Aulén's 'Christus Victor', René Girard, some Catholic critics of 'penal' language such as Joseph Ratzinger's Introduction to Christianity) criticise distorted penal-substitution models. Others defend a Catholic penal-satisfaction view: Wagner; earlier, Bossuet's Good Friday sermons and many manualists. The disagreement is largely about what 'penal' and 'wrath' mean.</p>`,
 authors:[["Christian B. Wagner","thomism.com: 'Penal Substitution: A Catholic Dogma? The Scholastic Doctrine'; 'Penal Substitutionary Atonement: The Foundation of Catholicism'; 'How Penal Substitution Refutes Protestantism'; christianbwagner.com Lenten series on III q.48 ('…in the Mode of Merit', '…by Way of Satisfaction', '…by Way of Sacrifice', '…in the Mode of Redemption') and q.49","Defends the scholastic doctrine that Christ bore the punishment due to sin as satisfaction, and argues it supports Catholic teaching on purgatory and penance against Protestant views."],["Garrigou-Lagrange","Our Saviour and His Love for Us (1933); Christ the Saviour","Meditates on the Passion as the summit of Christ's love and the 'superabundant' satisfaction."]],
 errors:[["The Father hated the Son on the Cross.","Thomas: the Father inspired the Son's will to suffer; the Son was always beloved (Mt 3:17)."],["The Cross was only an example (Abelard's caricature).","Thomas includes example but also real merit, satisfaction and sacrifice."],["Christ suffered the pains of hell.","Calvin's view; Catholic theology rejects it."]],
 scripture:[["Isa 53:4–10","he hath borne our infirmities; the Lord laid on him the iniquity of us all"],["2 Cor 5:21","him that knew no sin, for us he hath made sin"],["Gal 3:13","made a curse for us"],["1 Jn 2:2","the propitiation for our sins"],["Rom 8:32","he spared not even his own Son"]],
 tests:[["Name Thomas's five ways the Passion saves.","Merit, satisfaction, sacrifice, redemption, efficiency."],["Did the Father force the Son to suffer?","No: he inspired him with the will to suffer out of charity."]],
 read:["ST III qq.46–49","CCC 599–618","Anselm, Cur Deus Homo"]},

{id:"christ-descent",tract:"christ",level:2,note:"De fide (Apostles' Creed; Lateran IV)",st:"ST III q.52",
 q:"What does 'he descended into hell' mean?",
 one:"After his death Christ's soul, united to the Word, went to the 'limbo of the fathers', the abode of the righteous dead who had died before him, to free them and bring them to glory; he did not suffer the pains of the damned.",
 plain:`<p>'Hell' (Latin infernus, Hebrew Sheol, Greek Hades) in the Creed means the realm of the dead, not only the place of the damned. Christ truly died, so his soul, separated from his body, joined the dead. Thomas distinguishes several 'hells': the hell of the damned, purgatory, the limbo of children, and the limbo of the fathers (the 'bosom of Abraham', Lk 16:22). Christ went in person to the limbo of the fathers and showed his power to the others (III q.52 a.2).</p>
<p>He freed the just who were waiting: 'he preached to those spirits that were in prison' (1 Pet 3:19). The Catechism: 'Jesus did not descend into hell to deliver the damned, nor to destroy the hell of damnation, but to free the just who had gone before him' (CCC 633). Holy Saturday's ancient homily ('Awake, O sleeper') pictures him taking Adam by the hand.</p>`,
 analogy:["The liberator entering the camp","A victorious king enters a prison camp where his loyal subjects have waited for years. He frees them and leads them out; to the enemy's strongholds elsewhere he shows only his victory."],
 deeper:`<p><strong>A modern controversy.</strong> Hans Urs von Balthasar proposed that Christ experienced the abandonment of the damned on Holy Saturday ('descent' as suffering). Thomists (Alyssa Pitstick, Light in Darkness, 2007) argued this contradicts the tradition and the Catechism, which presents the descent as the victorious Christ's proclamation. The Church has not formally judged Balthasar's view, but CCC 631–637 follows the traditional account.</p>`,
 errors:[["Christ suffered in hell to complete redemption.","Redemption was complete on the Cross ('It is consummated')."],["Christ emptied the hell of the damned.","CCC 633."]],
 scripture:[["1 Pet 3:18–20; 4:6","preached to the spirits in prison"],["Eph 4:8–10","he descended into the lower parts of the earth"],["Acts 2:24–31","his soul not left in hell"],["Lk 23:43","this day with me in paradise"]],
 tests:[["Which 'hell' did Christ descend to in person?","The limbo of the fathers."]],
 read:["ST III q.52","CCC 631–637"]}
);

// Disputed questions: Our Lady
DQ.push(
{id:"mary-ic",tract:"mary",level:3,note:"Immaculate Conception: de fide (Pius IX, Ineffabilis Deus, 1854); Thomas's own view: disputed",st:"ST III q.27 a.2",
 q:"Did St Thomas deny the Immaculate Conception?",
 one:"In the Summa Thomas says Mary was sanctified 'after animation', apparently because he thought exemption from original sin would exempt her from Christ's redemption; the 1854 dogma answered that difficulty with Scotus's 'preservative redemption'; whether Thomas's 'after' is temporal or only a priority of nature is still debated, and some Thomists argue he is closer to the dogma than usually said.",
 plain:`<p>The dogma: 'the most Blessed Virgin Mary, in the first instant of her conception, by a singular grace and privilege of almighty God, in view of the merits of Jesus Christ, the Saviour of the human race, was preserved free from all stain of original sin' (Pius IX, Ineffabilis Deus, 1854).</p>
<p>Thomas's worry was that Christ is 'the universal Saviour of all'. If Mary 'had never incurred the stain of original sin, this would be derogatory to the dignity of Christ, by reason of His being the universal Saviour of all' (III q.27 a.2 ad 2). So he concluded 'that the Blessed Virgin was sanctified after animation' (III q.27 a.2), adding that she was sanctified before birth and had the highest purity after Christ.</p>
<p>Duns Scotus solved the difficulty: Mary was redeemed in a more perfect way, by being preserved from sin rather than cleansed from it. A doctor who prevents a disease saves more perfectly than one who cures it. This is exactly what the dogma says ('in view of the merits of Jesus Christ').</p>`,
 analogy:["The fall prevented","Someone who catches you as you trip, before you hit the ground, saves you as truly as someone who picks you up after the fall, and more perfectly. Mary was 'caught' at the first instant of her existence."],
 deeper:`<p><strong>The reading of 'after'.</strong> Thomas's embryology placed the rational soul's infusion some weeks after conception. Many historians therefore read 'after animation' as a short time after. Christian Wagner, in 'St. Thomas, Doctor of the Immaculate Conception', argues the opposite: Thomas distinguishes two senses of original sin (the privation of original justice in the soul, and the 'debt' or liability inherited through the flesh) and uses 'after' of a priority of nature, not time; on this reading Mary incurred only the debitum and was sanctified at the very instant of animation, which is what the dogma teaches. Wagner draws on the Sentences commentary (III d.3 q.1 a.2) and the Compendium Theologiae (I c.224). Others point to Thomas's early statement that Mary was 'immune from all original and actual sin' (In I Sent. d.44 q.1 a.3 ad 3) and to the Exposition of the Hail Mary.</p>
<p><strong>The honest summary.</strong> The Summa's text sits uneasily with the later dogma; Thomas's principles (Christ's universal redemption; Mary's supreme purity) are both affirmed by the dogma; and Thomists after 1854 embraced it fully. Garrigou-Lagrange (The Mother of the Saviour, 1948) argues Thomas's principles led toward it.</p>`,
 authors:[["Christian B. Wagner","christianbwagner.com: 'St. Thomas, Doctor of the Immaculate Conception' (2023)","Argues that Thomas, read in his whole corpus and with the distinction between the stain of original sin and the debitum, did not deny the Immaculate Conception."],["Garrigou-Lagrange","The Mother of the Saviour and Our Interior Life (1948)","Shows how the Thomist principles of Mary's fullness of grace and Christ's universal redemption are fulfilled in the dogma."]],
 errors:[["Mary did not need a Saviour.","She was redeemed preservatively (Lk 1:47: 'my spirit hath rejoiced in God my Saviour')."],["Thomas was a heretic.","The dogma was defined after his death; before 1854 the question was open."]],
 tests:[["What was Thomas's main objection?","That exemption from original sin would seem to exempt Mary from Christ's universal redemption."],["Who solved it, and how?","Scotus: preservative redemption."]],
 read:["ST III q.27 aa.1–6","Pius IX, Ineffabilis Deus (1854)","<a href=\"#lesson-t12\">Academy T12</a>"]},

{id:"mary-fomes",tract:"mary",level:3,note:"Mary free from all actual sin: de fide (Trent Session 6, can. 23); the fomes: theological opinion",st:"ST III q.27 a.3",
 q:"Did Our Lady experience concupiscence?",
 one:"Thomas held that in her first sanctification the 'fomes' (the inclination of the senses against reason) remained in Mary but was bound so that it never moved her to sin, and was wholly removed when she conceived Christ; after the dogma most theologians hold she never had it at all.",
 plain:`<p>The 'fomes' (tinder) of sin is 'a certain inordinate, but habitual, concupiscence of the sensitive appetite' (III q.27 a.3), the leftover inclination of the passions against reason that remains even after baptism. Did Mary have it?</p>
<p>Thomas lists four opinions and favours the last: 'in her first sanctification, the fomes remained essentially, but was fettered; and that, when she conceived the Son of God, it was entirely taken away' (III q.27 a.3). It never produced a disordered movement in her. He does this partly because he thought she was sanctified after animation (previous question).</p>
<p>Since the Immaculate Conception (no original sin at all), most theologians conclude Mary never had the fomes, which is a consequence of original sin. Wagner has defended Thomas's teaching as coherent within his framework and showed how the Compendium Theologiae expresses it.</p>`,
 analogy:["The tinder never lit","Dry tinder near a spark can catch fire; tinder soaked through cannot. Thomas pictures grace so 'binding' the tinder in Mary that it never caught. The later doctrine says there was never any tinder."],
 deeper:`<p><strong>Is concupiscence sin?</strong> Trent teaches concupiscence in the baptised is 'called sin' by Paul only because it comes from sin and inclines to sin, not because it is truly sin (Session 5, can. 5). Wagner has written on 'the sinfulness of concupiscence' and 'Marriage and concupiscence', engaging the Augustinian tradition.</p>`,
 authors:[["Christian B. Wagner","christianbwagner.com: 'St. Thomas and Concupiscence in the Blessed Virgin Mary' (2025); thomism.com: 'On the Sinfulness of Concupiscence'; 'St. Thomas and the Sinfulness of Concupiscence'","Defends Thomas's teaching on the bound fomes and explains the Thomist view of concupiscence."]],
 tests:[["What is the fomes?","Habitual inordinate concupiscence of the sensitive appetite."],["What did Thomas say happened to it in Mary?","It was bound at her sanctification and removed when she conceived Christ."]],
 read:["ST III q.27 a.3","Trent, Session 5, can. 5"]},

{id:"mary-mediation",tract:"mary",level:2,note:"Mary's subordinate cooperation: Church teaching (LG 60–62); 'Co-redemptrix' title judged inappropriate (DDF, Mater Populi Fidelis, 2025)",st:"Lumen Gentium 60–62",
 q:"Is Mary 'Co-redemptrix' or 'Mediatrix of all graces'?",
 one:"Mary truly cooperated in Christ's work as the New Eve and is our mother in the order of grace, but entirely subordinate to Christ, the one Mediator; in 2025 the Dicastery for the Doctrine of the Faith judged 'Co-redemptrix' always inappropriate and urged caution with 'Mediatrix of all graces'.",
 plain:`<p>Vatican II: 'The maternal duty of Mary toward men in no wise obscures or diminishes this unique mediation of Christ, but rather shows His power' (LG 60). Her cooperation 'flows forth from the superabundance of the merits of Christ, rests on His mediation, depends entirely on it, and draws all its power from it' (LG 60). She is 'invoked by the Church under the titles of Advocate, Auxiliatrix, Adjutrix, and Mediatrix' (LG 62), always in a subordinate sense.</p>
<p>On 4 November 2025 the Dicastery for the Doctrine of the Faith published the doctrinal note Mater Populi Fidelis, approved by Pope Leo XIV. It says that 'it is always inappropriate to use the title \"Co-redemptrix\" to define Mary's cooperation', because it risks obscuring Christ's unique mediation, and that 'no human person… can act as a universal dispenser of grace'; 'Mediatrix' is acceptable only in a subordinate sense, and the note prefers titles like 'Mother of the faithful'.</p>`,
 analogy:["The mother at the son's side","A mother who stands by her son as he gives his life, offers him with love, and then cares for his friends, shares in his work in a real but entirely dependent way. She adds nothing to his gift; she shows its fruitfulness."],
 deeper:`<p><strong>Background.</strong> Before 2025 many theologians and popes used 'Co-redemptrix' in a qualified sense (e.g. Pius XI in some addresses, John Paul II on a few occasions), and a movement sought a dogmatic definition. Cardinal Ratzinger and Pope Francis opposed the title. The 2025 note settles the Church's present guidance on terminology; it does not deny Mary's real, subordinate cooperation.</p>
<p><strong>Thomistic basis.</strong> Thomas: at the Annunciation 'the Virgin's consent was besought in lieu of that of the entire human nature' (III q.30 a.1). Her consent and her offering at the Cross are the root of her spiritual motherhood (Jn 19:26–27).</p>`,
 schools:`<p>Some Mariologists and commentators (e.g. Diane Montagna's critique) have argued the note is in tension with earlier papal usage. Others welcomed it as clarifying. The note is magisterial guidance on language, not a definition; Catholics owe it religious submission (LG 25).</p>`,
 authors:[["Garrigou-Lagrange","The Mother of the Saviour and Our Interior Life (1948)","Before the 2025 note, used 'Co-redemptrix' in a strictly subordinate sense; his underlying theology of Mary's dependent cooperation matches LG 60–62."]],
 errors:[["Mary redeems alongside Christ as an equal.","Christ alone is Redeemer (1 Tim 2:5)."],["Mary is irrelevant to salvation.","LG 61–62: she is mother in the order of grace."]],
 tests:[["What did Mater Populi Fidelis say about 'Co-redemptrix'?","That it is always inappropriate to use it to define Mary's cooperation."]],
 read:["Lumen Gentium 60–65","DDF, Mater Populi Fidelis (4 Nov 2025)","ST III q.30 a.1"]}
);

// Disputed questions: the sacraments (ST III qq.60–90)
DQ.push(
{id:"sac-validity",tract:"sacraments",level:2,note:"De fide (Florence, Decree for the Armenians 1439; Trent Session 7, can. 11)",st:"ST III q.60 aa.6–8; q.64 a.8",
 q:"What makes a sacrament valid: matter, form, minister and intention?",
 one:"A sacrament is valid when a minister with the required power uses the right matter (the sign-thing) and form (the words) with the intention 'of doing what the Church does'; holiness of the minister is not required, because Christ is the principal agent.",
 plain:`<p>Florence (1439): 'All these sacraments are made up of three elements: namely, things as the matter, words as the form, and the person of the minister who confers the sacrament with the intention of doing what the Church does.'</p>
<ul><li><strong>Matter and form.</strong> Thomas applies hylomorphism: 'the words are as the form, and sensible things are as the matter' (III q.60 a.7). Water and 'I baptise you in the name of the Father…'; wheat bread and grape wine and the words of institution.</li>
<li><strong>Intention.</strong> Washing with water can mean many things, so it must be 'determined to one purpose, i.e. the sacramental effect, by the intention of him who washes' (III q.64 a.8). The minister, a living instrument, must 'intend to do that which Christ and the Church do' (ad 1).</li>
<li><strong>Holiness not required.</strong> Since Christ acts through the minister as instrument, a sinful priest validly baptises or consecrates (III q.64 a.5; Augustine against the Donatists). This is 'ex opere operato': by the work performed.</li></ul>`,
 analogy:["The pen and the author","A pen does not need to be beautiful to sign a contract; it needs to be a working pen in the hand of the one with authority to sign. The minister is Christ's pen. But unlike a pen, a human instrument must also will to be used for this purpose: that is the intention."],
 deeper:`<p><strong>How much intention?</strong> Theologians distinguish actual, virtual, habitual and interpretative intentions; at least a virtual intention is required. Thomas reports a debate on whether 'mental' intention is necessary or whether the outward words suffice (III q.64 a.8 ad 2). The common teaching after Trent: an internal intention to do what the Church does is required, but it is presumed when the rite is seriously performed. A minister's private heresy (e.g. not believing in the Real Presence) does not invalidate if he intends what the Church does.</p>
<p><strong>Anglican orders.</strong> Leo XIII's Apostolicae Curae (1896) judged Anglican ordinations 'absolutely null and utterly void' because of defects of form (the Edwardine Ordinal removed the sacrificial priesthood) and consequently of intention.</p>
<p><strong>Liturgical abuses.</strong> Changing the form invalidates (e.g. 'We baptise you' was declared invalid by the CDF in 2020). Wagner has written on intention and validity, and on whether invalid sacraments or unjust excommunications bind.</p>`,
 authors:[["Christian B. Wagner","thomism.com: 'On Intent and the Validity of Sacraments'; 'Invalid Sacraments, Excommunications…'; 'An Important Note in Sacramentology'; 'How Does Concelebration Actually…'","Explains the Thomist account of ministerial intention and applies it to recent controversies (concelebration, validity questions)."],["Garrigou-Lagrange","De Eucharistia; The Priest in Union with Christ","Treats the minister as Christ's instrument and the priest's identification with Christ."]],
 errors:[["A sinful priest's sacraments are invalid (Donatism).","Condemned; Christ is the principal agent."],["Any words with the same 'meaning' will do.","The essential form must be preserved; substantial changes invalidate."]],
 tests:[["What three elements does Florence name?","Matter, form, and the minister with the intention of doing what the Church does."],["Is the minister's holiness required for validity?","No."]],
 read:["ST III q.60; q.64","Trent, Session 7, can. 11–12","CCC 1127–1128"]},

{id:"sac-cause",tract:"sacraments",level:3,note:"Sacraments cause grace ex opere operato: de fide (Trent Session 7, can. 8); mode of causality: school dispute",st:"ST III q.62 aa.1, 4",
 q:"Do the sacraments really cause grace, or are they only occasions on which God gives it?",
 one:"For Thomas they are true instrumental causes of grace, as an axe is a true cause of the bed in the carpenter's hand; God is the principal cause, and grace is not 'in' the water, but God's power passes through the sign.",
 plain:`<p>Some medieval theologians held that the sacraments are signs on the occasion of which God gives grace, as a king pays a hundred pounds to whoever presents a lead token. Thomas rejects this: then 'the sacraments are mere signs. For the leaden coin is nothing but a sign of the king's command' (III q.62 a.1). Scripture says baptism saves (1 Pet 3:21) and that we are 'baptised into Christ' (Gal 3:27).</p>
<p>His solution: two kinds of efficient cause. 'The principal cause works by the power of its form… In this way none but God can cause grace… But the instrumental cause works not by the power of its form, but only by the motion whereby it is moved by the principal agent… for instance, the couch is not like the axe, but like the art which is in the craftsman's mind. And it is thus that the sacraments of the New Law cause grace' (III q.62 a.1).</p>`,
 analogy:["The axe and the carpenter","An axe really cuts the wood, but the bed's shape comes from the carpenter's art, not from the axe. The sacrament really acts on the soul, but the grace it causes is like God, not like water."],
 deeper:`<p><strong>The power in the sacrament.</strong> If sacraments are instruments, there must be in them 'a certain instrumental power' (III q.62 a.4), a transient power, like the motion in the axe while the carpenter swings it.</p>
<p><strong>Physical versus moral causality.</strong> Later Thomists (Cajetan, Billuart, Garrigou-Lagrange) taught 'physical' instrumental causality. Others (Lugo, Franzelin) proposed 'moral' causality: the sacraments move God by their dignity as Christ's acts, as a petition moves a king. Others again (Billot) 'intentional' causality. Trent defined only that they contain and confer grace ex opere operato.</p>
<p><strong>Sacramental grace.</strong> Each sacrament gives sanctifying grace plus a special help ordered to its purpose: in marriage, the grace to love as Christ loves the Church (III q.62 a.2). Wagner has written on how sacramental grace differs from sanctifying grace in general.</p>`,
 authors:[["Christian B. Wagner","thomism.com: 'How Does Sacramental Grace Differ…'; 'The Two-fold Necessity of the Sacraments'; 'Sacraments in the Old Covenant'","Explains the special 'sacramental grace' added to sanctifying grace and the necessity of means and precept."],["Garrigou-Lagrange","Reality, on the sacraments; De Eucharistia","Defends physical instrumental causality."]],
 errors:[["Sacraments are only reminders.","Trent, Session 7, can. 8."],["Sacraments work like magic, without faith.","Ex opere operato does not dispense from right dispositions in adults (obstacles block fruit)."]],
 tests:[["Why is the bed not like the axe?","Because the instrument acts by the power of the principal agent; the effect resembles the principal agent."]],
 read:["ST III q.62","Trent, Session 7"]},

{id:"sac-character",tract:"sacraments",level:2,note:"De fide (Trent Session 7, can. 9)",st:"ST III q.63",
 q:"What is a sacramental character, and why can't baptism, confirmation and orders be repeated?",
 one:"Baptism, confirmation and holy orders imprint an indelible 'character', a spiritual power that configures the soul to Christ's priesthood for worship; because it shares in Christ's eternal priesthood it can never be erased, so these sacraments are never repeated.",
 plain:`<p>Trent: these three sacraments 'imprint on the soul a character, that is, a certain spiritual and indelible mark, on account of which they cannot be repeated' (Session 7, can. 9). Thomas explains what it is: a share in Christ's priesthood: 'Christ's faithful have a share in His Priesthood… sharing a certain spiritual power with regard to the sacraments and to things pertaining to the Divine worship' (III q.63 a.5). Baptism gives the power to receive the sacraments and offer worship with Christ; confirmation the power to profess the faith publicly as a soldier of Christ; orders the active power to confect sacraments.</p>
<p>Why indelible? 'Christ's Priesthood is eternal… Consequently, every sanctification wrought by His Priesthood, is perpetual' (III q.63 a.5). Even mortal sin, apostasy or laicisation does not remove it; a laicised priest who says the words of consecration validly consecrates (though illicitly).</p>`,
 analogy:["The soldier's commission","A commission in an army is not removed by bad conduct; the soldier can be disciplined, demoted or court-martialled, but remains one commissioned. The character is like a permanent commission in Christ's priestly people."],
 deeper:`<p><strong>Character and grace.</strong> Character is distinct from grace: a person can have the character without grace (a baptised person in mortal sin), and so the character is a permanent title to the grace when obstacles are removed ('revival' of sacraments).</p>
<p><strong>Conditional rebaptism.</strong> When validity of a previous baptism is genuinely doubtful, the Church baptises conditionally ('If you are not baptised…'), so as not to repeat a sacrament that imprints a character.</p>`,
 tests:[["Which sacraments imprint a character?","Baptism, confirmation, holy orders."],["In what does the character consist, for Thomas?","A share in Christ's priesthood: a spiritual power for divine worship."]],
 read:["ST III q.63","CCC 1121, 1272–1274, 1304, 1581–1584"]},

{id:"sac-accidents",tract:"sacraments",level:3,note:"Transubstantiation: de fide (Lateran IV; Trent Session 13); accidents without subject: theologically certain (Constance, Wyclif condemned)",st:"ST III q.77 a.1",
 q:"After consecration, what holds up the appearances of bread if the bread is gone?",
 one:"The accidents (size, colour, taste) remain without any subject, sustained directly by God's power; this is not a contradiction, because an accident's essence is to be apt to exist in a subject, not to be actually in one.",
 plain:`<p>Transubstantiation: the whole substance of bread becomes the substance of Christ's body; the 'species' (accidents) of bread remain. But accidents normally inhere in a substance. In what do they inhere now? Not in bread (gone), not in Christ's body (his glorious body cannot be affected by these qualities), not in the air (accidents cannot pass from subject to subject). So they remain without a subject (III q.77 a.1).</p>
<p>Is this impossible? Thomas answers that the definition of an accident is not 'a being in a subject' but 'to have existence in a subject' as its natural aptitude: 'in this sacrament it is not in virtue of their essence that accidents are not in a subject, but through the Divine power sustaining them; and consequently they do not cease to be accidents' (III q.77 a.1 ad 2). And God, as first cause, can produce without the secondary cause what he normally produces through it. Thomas adds that dimensive quantity serves as a quasi-subject for the other accidents (q.77 a.2).</p>`,
 analogy:["The hand that holds the cup","A cup normally rests on a table. If the table is removed and someone holds the cup in the air, the cup still has its shape, colour and weight; it is simply held up another way. God 'holds up' the accidents himself when the substance of bread is no longer there."],
 deeper:`<p><strong>Protestant scholastic objections.</strong> Reformed scholastics (e.g. Turretin) argued that accidents without a subject are a metaphysical impossibility. Wagner has published responses ('Protestant Scholastic Argument Against Transubstantiation Refuted', 'Defending the Non-Inherence of Accidents in Transubstantiation', 'In Defense of the Metaphysics of the Eucharist: A Response to Matt Hedges'), arguing that the objection confuses an accident's essence (aptitudinal inherence) with its actual inherence.</p>
<p><strong>What breaks and what does not.</strong> When the host is broken, the species are broken; Christ is not divided but whole in each part (III q.76 a.3). When the species corrupt (e.g. digestion), Christ ceases to be present.</p>
<p><strong>Magisterium.</strong> The Council of Constance (1415) condemned Wyclif's propositions that the accidents of bread do not remain without a subject in the sacrament and that the substance of bread remains.</p>`,
 authors:[["Christian B. Wagner","christianbwagner.com: 'Defending the Non-Inherence of Accidents in Transubstantiation' (2023); 'Protestant Scholastic Argument Against Transubstantiation Refuted' (2025); thomism.com: 'Transubstantiation Defended'; 'In Defense of the Metaphysics of the Eucharist'","Answers the strongest metaphysical objections to transubstantiation from Reformed scholastics and contemporary critics."],["Edward Feser","Blog posts and Aristotle's Revenge (on substance and accident)","Explains the substance/accident distinction and why transubstantiation is not refuted by physics, which studies quantitative accidents."],["Garrigou-Lagrange","De Eucharistia (1943)","A detailed Thomist treatise on transubstantiation and the accidents."]],
 errors:[["Consubstantiation (Luther): bread and Christ together.","Trent, Session 13, can. 2."],["Transubstantiation means a chemical change.","Substance is not a physical property; the physical properties do not change."]],
 tests:[["Why can accidents exist without a subject?","Because their essence is an aptitude to inhere, and God as first cause can sustain them directly."],["Which council condemned Wyclif on this?","Constance (1415)."]],
 read:["ST III qq.75–77","Trent, Session 13","<a href=\"#lesson-t13\">Academy T13</a>"]},

{id:"sac-presence",tract:"sacraments",level:3,note:"Real presence of the whole Christ: de fide (Trent Session 13, can. 1, 3); manner: theological conclusions",st:"ST III q.76",
 q:"Is Christ in the tabernacle 'in a place'? Does he move, see or hear there?",
 one:"Christ's body is present 'after the manner of substance', not as in a place; so he is not moved when the host is moved, and he does not see or hear through the species as through organs; but in his glorified humanity in heaven he knows, by his knowledge as head, those who adore and receive him.",
 plain:`<p>Christ is present by transubstantiation, so his body is present the way the substance of bread was: 'Christ's body is not in this sacrament as in a place, but after the manner of substance, that is to say, in that way in which substance is contained by dimensions' (III q.76 a.5). Substance as such is whole in the whole and whole in each part, which is why Christ is whole in every host and every fragment.</p>
<p>Is he moved when the monstrance is carried? 'Christ is not moved locally of Himself, but only accidentally' (III q.76 a.6): his being under the sacrament is a relation to the species, which move. He is not 'in' the tabernacle as a prisoner in a cell.</p>
<p>By concomitance the whole Christ (body, blood, soul and divinity) is present under each species, because the living Christ cannot be divided (III q.76 aa.1–2). Hence Communion under one kind gives the whole Christ (Trent, Session 21).</p>`,
 analogy:["The voice and the many radios","One speaker's voice is fully heard on every radio tuned to the station; the voice is not divided among them, nor confined in any one. The analogy is weak (sound is physical), but it suggests how one Christ is wholly present in many places without being multiplied or enclosed."],
 deeper:`<p><strong>Can Christ hear us 'in' the Eucharist?</strong> Thomas holds that Christ cannot be seen by bodily eye as he is in the sacrament, even by angels' natural knowledge; he does not exercise sense through the species. Wagner's 'Can Christ Hear in the Eucharist?' explains that Christ, glorified in heaven, knows all who adore him by his beatific and infused knowledge, so adoration before the tabernacle truly reaches him, but not through 'ears' in the host.</p>
<p><strong>Miracles of the host.</strong> When a host is seen as flesh or a child appears, Thomas says the appearance is a change in the species, or in the beholder's sight; Christ's body is not truly visible there in its own form (III q.76 a.8).</p>`,
 authors:[["Christian B. Wagner","thomism.com: 'Can Christ Hear in the Eucharist?'; 'On Frequent Communion'","Applies Thomas's account of sacramental presence to devotional questions."]],
 errors:[["Christ is confined in the tabernacle.","He is present sacramentally, not locally."],["Christ is only present at the moment of Communion.","Trent, Session 13, can. 4: the presence remains in the reserved hosts."]],
 tests:[["How is Christ's body in the sacrament?","After the manner of substance, not as in a place."],["What is concomitance?","The whole living Christ is present under each species because his body, blood, soul and divinity cannot be separated."]],
 read:["ST III q.76","Trent, Session 13 and 21"]},

{id:"sac-sacrifice",tract:"sacraments",level:2,note:"The Mass a true and proper sacrifice: de fide (Trent Session 22)",st:"ST III q.83 a.1",
 q:"How is the Mass a sacrifice if Christ died 'once for all'?",
 one:"The Mass does not repeat or add to Calvary: it makes the one sacrifice of the Cross sacramentally present, with the same priest and victim offering himself in an unbloody manner, and applies its fruits to us.",
 plain:`<p>Hebrews insists Christ offered himself 'once' (9:28; 10:10–14). Trent teaches the Mass is 'a true and proper sacrifice' (Session 22, can. 1) because 'the victim is one and the same: the same now offering through the ministry of priests, who then offered himself on the Cross; only the manner of offering is different' (ch. 2).</p>
<p>Thomas gives two reasons the celebration is called a sacrifice. First, it is 'an image representing Christ's Passion, which is His true sacrifice', and images take the names of what they represent. Second, by it 'we are made partakers of the fruit of our Lord's Passion' (III q.83 a.1). The separate consecration of bread and wine sacramentally signifies the separation of body and blood in death.</p>
<p>So the Mass adds nothing to Calvary; it makes Calvary present 'in a mystery' and gives us access to it. The Roman Canon prays in Malachi's words: offering 'a pure victim, a holy victim, a spotless victim'.</p>`,
 analogy:["The one event made present","A concert recorded once can be played many times; but the Mass is not a recording. It is more like a door that opens onto the one event, so that we stand at the Cross. The sacrifice is the same, the 'door' is new each time."],
 deeper:`<p><strong>Theories of the essence of the sacrifice.</strong> Post-Tridentine theologians debated what makes the Mass a sacrifice: the double consecration (Thomists, Billot: 'mystical immolation'), a real change in the victim (Lugo), the offering (de la Taille, Mysterium Fidei 1921). All agree it is one with the Cross.</p>
<p><strong>Malachi 1:11.</strong> The Didache (ch. 14) and Trent see the Mass prophesied in the 'clean oblation' offered everywhere. Wagner's 'Defending the Sacrifice of the Mass' and 'The Two Altars and the Passion of…' answer Protestant objections from Hebrews.</p>`,
 authors:[["Christian B. Wagner","thomism.com: 'Defending the Sacrifice of the Mass'; 'The Two Altars and the Passion of…'; 'The Epistle to the Hebrews and Catholicism'","Argues that Hebrews, rightly read, supports the Catholic doctrine of one sacrifice made present."],["Garrigou-Lagrange","De Eucharistia; The Priest in Union with Christ","The Mass as the same sacrifice, Christ being the principal priest."]],
 errors:[["The Mass re-crucifies Christ.","It is the same sacrifice, unbloody, not a new death."],["The Mass is only a memorial meal.","Trent, Session 22, can. 1–3."]],
 scripture:[["Mal 1:11","a clean oblation in every place"],["Lk 22:19","do this for a commemoration of me"],["1 Cor 10:16–21","the table of the Lord and the table of devils"],["Heb 13:10","we have an altar"]],
 tests:[["What is the same and what differs between Calvary and the Mass, according to Trent?","Same victim and priest; different manner of offering (bloody/unbloody)."]],
 read:["ST III q.83","Trent, Session 22","CCC 1362–1372"]},

{id:"sac-contrition",tract:"sacraments",level:2,note:"Necessity of contrition and integral confession of mortal sins: de fide (Trent Session 14)",st:"ST III qq.84–90; Suppl. qq.1–10",
 q:"What sorrow is needed for a valid confession? Perfect and imperfect contrition",
 one:"Confession requires real sorrow with a purpose of amendment; imperfect contrition (attrition), sorrow out of fear of hell or the ugliness of sin, is enough with absolution, while perfect contrition, sorrow out of love of God, forgives mortal sin even before confession if it includes the resolve to confess.",
 plain:`<p>Trent defines contrition as 'sorrow of mind and a detestation for sin committed, with the resolution of not sinning in the future' (Session 14, ch. 4). Two kinds:</p>
<ul><li><strong>Perfect contrition</strong> arises from charity, love of God above all. It reconciles a person to God at once, with the intention to confess (CCC 1452).</li>
<li><strong>Imperfect contrition (attrition)</strong> arises from the consideration of sin's ugliness or fear of punishment. It is 'a gift of God' and disposes the sinner to receive grace in the sacrament (Trent, Session 14, ch. 4; CCC 1453).</li></ul>
<p>The acts of the penitent (contrition, confession, satisfaction) are the 'quasi-matter' of the sacrament; absolution is the form (III q.84 aa.2–3). All mortal sins remembered after a careful examination must be confessed in kind and number.</p>`,
 analogy:["The patient's cooperation","A surgeon cannot heal a patient who refuses the operation. The patient need not love the surgeon perfectly; he must want to be healed and accept the treatment. Attrition is that willingness; perfect contrition is love that already begins the healing."],
 deeper:`<p><strong>When is a confession invalid?</strong> If there is no sorrow at all, if a mortal sin is deliberately concealed, or if there is no purpose of amendment (e.g. intending to continue an adulterous relationship). Forgetting a sin does not invalidate; it is forgiven and should be confessed next time. Sorrow need not be felt emotionally; it is an act of the will (relevant to scrupulosity and depression).</p>
<p><strong>The contritionism/attritionism debate.</strong> After Trent, some (contritionists) demanded at least a beginning of love of God even with attrition; attritionists held fear-based attrition suffices. Alexander VII (1667) forbade the two sides to censure each other. Wagner's essays 'On the Contrition Necessary for a…' and 'Is My Confession Invalid?' treat these questions with Trent, the Roman Catechism and the theologians.</p>`,
 authors:[["Christian B. Wagner","thomism.com: 'Is My Confession Invalid? The Validity of Confession According to Trent, the Roman Catechism, and Theologians'; 'On the Contrition Necessary for a…'","Practical and theological guidance on the minimum requirements, aimed at reassuring the scrupulous and warning the lax."],["Garrigou-Lagrange","The Three Ages of the Interior Life","Places contrition within growth in charity, from servile fear to filial fear."]],
 errors:[["I must feel intense sorrow or my confession is invalid.","Sorrow is in the will; feeling is not required."],["Fear-based sorrow is worthless.","Trent calls attrition 'a gift of God'."]],
 tests:[["What is the difference between perfect and imperfect contrition?","The motive: love of God versus fear of punishment or the ugliness of sin."],["Does forgetting a mortal sin invalidate confession?","No; it is forgiven, and should be mentioned next time."]],
 read:["ST III qq.84–90","Trent, Session 14","CCC 1450–1460"]},

{id:"sac-desire",tract:"sacraments",level:2,note:"Baptism of desire and blood: Church teaching (Trent Session 6, ch. 4; CCC 1258–1260)",st:"ST III q.68 a.2",
 q:"Can someone be saved without being baptised? Baptism of desire and blood",
 one:"Baptism is necessary for salvation, but God is not bound to the sacraments: one who desires baptism with faith working through charity, or dies for Christ before baptism, receives its grace; the Church extends implicit desire to those who, without fault, do not know the Gospel but seek God sincerely.",
 plain:`<p>Jesus: 'unless a man be born again of water and the Holy Ghost, he cannot enter into the kingdom of God' (Jn 3:5). Thomas distinguishes: baptism may be lacking 'both in reality and in desire', which indicates contempt, and such persons 'cannot obtain salvation'. Or it may be lacking 'in reality but not in desire', as when a catechumen dies before baptism: 'such a man can obtain salvation without being actually baptized, on account of his desire for Baptism, which desire is the outcome of \"faith that worketh by charity,\" whereby God, Whose power is not tied to visible sacraments, sanctifies man inwardly' (III q.68 a.2). Ambrose said of the emperor Valentinian, who died a catechumen, that he was saved by his desire.</p>
<p><strong>Baptism of blood</strong>: martyrs who die for Christ before baptism (e.g. the Holy Innocents) receive its grace (III q.66 a.11).</p>`,
 analogy:["The letter in the post","A man who has signed and posted his acceptance of a contract, but dies before it arrives, is treated by a just party as having accepted. God, who reads hearts, treats the sincere desire as the reality, when the reality is impossible."],
 deeper:`<p><strong>Implicit desire.</strong> Pius XII's Holy Office letter to Archbishop Cushing (1949), against Fr Leonard Feeney, taught that the desire need not be explicit: an implicit desire, contained in the good disposition of a soul that wants to conform its will to God's, can suffice if animated by perfect charity and supernatural faith. Vatican II (LG 16) and CCC 1260 apply this to those who, through no fault, do not know Christ. This does not mean baptism or the Church are optional for those who know them (see 'No salvation outside the Church').</p>
<p><strong>Infants.</strong> Infants cannot desire; hence the Church's insistence on infant baptism and the question of limbo (see 'The last things').</p>`,
 authors:[["Christian B. Wagner","thomism.com: 'The Two-fold Necessity of the Sacraments'; 'Notes on Implicit Faith, Explicit Faith and Salvation'; 'On Implicit Faith and the Fathers'; 'On Implicit Faith and Scripture'; 'Salmanticenses on the Necessity of…'","Distinguishes necessity of means and of precept, and examines how much explicit faith is necessary for salvation according to Thomas and the Salmanticenses."]],
 errors:[["Feeneyism: no salvation at all without water baptism.","Rejected by the Holy Office (1949)."],["Baptism is unnecessary since desire is enough.","Desire presupposes the impossibility of the sacrament, not contempt or neglect."]],
 tests:[["What makes desire for baptism salvific, for Thomas?","That it flows from faith working through charity."]],
 read:["ST III q.66 a.11; q.68 a.2","CCC 1257–1261","<a href=\"#dq-church-eens\">No salvation outside the Church</a>"]}
);

// Disputed questions: moral theology
DQ.push(
{id:"moral-mortal",tract:"moral",level:2,note:"Distinction of mortal and venial sin: de fide (Trent Session 6, ch. 11; Session 14)",st:"ST I-II q.72 a.5; q.88",
 q:"How do I tell whether a sin is mortal or venial?",
 one:"A sin is mortal when its matter is grave and it is committed with full knowledge and deliberate consent; it is mortal 'by its kind' when its object is contrary to charity, and becomes venial in a given case only by slight matter or imperfect knowledge or consent.",
 plain:`<p>Thomas: 'when the will is directed to a thing that is in itself contrary to charity, whereby man is directed to his last end, the sin is mortal by reason of its object' (I-II q.88 a.2). Venial sin is a disorder about the means that does not turn the soul away from the last end, like a detour rather than a U-turn (I-II q.72 a.5).</p>
<p>The Catechism's three conditions: grave matter, full knowledge, deliberate consent (CCC 1857–1859). Grave matter is specified by the Ten Commandments.</p>
<p>Some sins are mortal 'by their kind' (ex genere suo): murder, adultery, blasphemy, theft of something serious. They can be venial in a particular act through lack of full deliberation (a half-awake thought) or, for sins admitting degrees, through slight matter (stealing a sweet). Some sins are venial by their kind (an idle word, slight impatience) but can become mortal through a grave purpose or contempt.</p>`,
 analogy:["The road and the destination","Mortal sin is turning your back on the destination and driving the other way. Venial sin is driving carelessly on the right road: dangerous and harmful, but not abandoning the journey."],
 deeper:`<p><strong>Denys the Carthusian's rules.</strong> Wagner has translated rules from Denys the Carthusian's Summa Peccati for judging mortal sin. Among them: acts against the natural law, justice, the Decalogue, divine law, the Church's precepts and charity toward God or neighbour are mortal by their kind (unless slight matter or imperfect deliberation); knowingly exposing oneself to the danger of mortal sin is itself mortal; failing to avoid a proximate occasion of mortal sin is mortal; deliberate consent to forbidden pleasure, and a firm intention to commit a forbidden act, are mortal even without the external act.</p>
<p><strong>Worked example: drunkenness.</strong> Thomas: if a man 'is well aware that the drink is immoderate and intoxicating, and yet he would rather be drunk than abstain', 'drunkenness is a mortal sin' (II-II q.150 a.2), because he willingly deprives himself of reason. Without that knowledge it may be venial or no sin.</p>
<p><strong>Can venial sin damn?</strong> Not of itself; but habitual venial sin disposes to mortal sin (I-II q.88 a.3). Wagner discusses the scholastic debate 'on the damnability of venial sin' (whether God could justly punish venial sin eternally in itself).</p>`,
 authors:[["Christian B. Wagner","thomism.com: 'Understanding Mortal and Venial Sin: Some Notes'; 'The 15 Rules of Determining Mortal Sin' (Denys the Carthusian, translated); 'On the Damnability of Venial Sin'; 'Whether Inebriation is a Mortal Sin'","Practical criteria from the scholastic tradition for distinguishing mortal and venial sin."],["Garrigou-Lagrange","The Three Ages of the Interior Life, on venial sin and imperfection","Shows how deliberate venial sins block progress in the spiritual life."]],
 errors:[["There is no mortal sin unless I intend to reject God (fundamental option).","Rejected by Veritatis Splendor 65–70: a single grave act can be mortal."],["Everything I feel guilty about is mortal.","Scrupulosity: feelings are not the measure; the three conditions are."]],
 tests:[["What makes a sin mortal 'by reason of its object'?","The will directed to something contrary to charity."],["Can a sin mortal by its kind be venial in a given act?","Yes, through slight matter or lack of full deliberation."]],
 read:["ST I-II qq.72, 88","CCC 1854–1864","Veritatis Splendor 65–70","<a href=\"#q-mortal-venial\">Questions: mortal and venial sin</a>"]},

{id:"moral-systems",tract:"moral",level:3,note:"Rigorism and laxism condemned; probabilism, equiprobabilism and probabiliorism permitted",st:"ST I-II q.19 aa.5–6; II-II q.47",
 q:"What may I do when I am unsure whether something is sinful? (Probabilism and its rivals)",
 one:"One may never act with a practical doubt about whether an act is sinful; to resolve a speculative doubt about a law, the Church has condemned both extreme strictness (tutiorism) and laxity, and permits several 'moral systems', of which probabilism and probabiliorism are the main contenders.",
 plain:`<p>First principle: never act with a <em>practical</em> doubt ('this might be a mortal sin and I'll do it anyway'): that willingly risks sin. The doubt must first be resolved into a practically certain conscience. How?</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table class="t"><thead><tr><th>System</th><th>Rule</th><th>Status</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Tutiorism (rigorism)</td><td>Always follow the safer opinion unless certain the law does not bind</td><td>Condemned (Alexander VIII, 1690, against Jansenists)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Probabiliorism</td><td>Follow the more probable opinion; the less safe may be followed only if it is more probable</td><td>Permitted (many Dominicans: Gonet, Billuart, Concina)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Equiprobabilism</td><td>When opinions are equally probable, liberty may be followed when the doubt is about whether a law exists</td><td>Permitted (St Alphonsus Liguori)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Probabilism</td><td>A solidly probable opinion favouring liberty may be followed, even if the opposite is more probable</td><td>Permitted (Bartolomé de Medina, 1577; most Jesuits; the common practice of confessors)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Laxism</td><td>Any slightly probable opinion suffices</td><td>Condemned (Innocent XI, 1679)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>Probabilism's key 'reflex principle' is 'a doubtful law does not bind' (lex dubia non obligat). Its critics answer that a merely probable principle cannot yield moral certainty. In some matters even probabilists require the safer course: the validity of sacraments, things necessary for salvation, and avoiding certain harm to others (Innocent XI condemned using probable opinions in conferring sacraments).</p>`,
 analogy:["The traffic light that may be broken","If you are unsure whether a light is red, you do not drive through hoping it is green. You find out, or follow a reasonable rule. But if a sign is so faded that no one can tell whether a speed limit applies, a reasonable driver may assume the general rule of liberty. The systems disagree about how faded the sign must be."],
 deeper:`<p><strong>The Thomist case against probabilism.</strong> Wagner's essay sets out the probabiliorist argument of Gonet, Billuart and Contenson: no Father or early scholastic allowed following a less safe and less probable opinion; prudence requires firm adherence to truth; and a reflex principle that is itself only probable cannot generate certainty, since the effect cannot exceed its cause. He admits he had once found probabilism persuasive. His related essay 'Against the New Laxism' criticises the misuse of probabilism to justify dissent from clear magisterial teaching.</p>
<p><strong>Alphonsus.</strong> St Alphonsus Liguori, patron of moralists and confessors, developed equiprobabilism as a middle way; the Church has declared his moral works safe to follow, without declaring his system the only correct one.</p>`,
 authors:[["Christian B. Wagner","thomism.com: 'The Case Against Probabilism'; 'Against the New Laxism'; christianbwagner.com: 'Between Laxism and Rigorism'","Presents the Thomist probabiliorist critique of probabilism while explaining all the permitted systems."]],
 errors:[["If any theologian allows it, I may do it.","Laxism, condemned in 1679."],["I may only act when I am absolutely certain.","Tutiorism, condemned in 1690."]],
 tests:[["Which two extremes are condemned?","Laxism (1679) and tutiorism/rigorism (1690)."],["What is the probabilist reflex principle?","A doubtful law does not bind."]],
 read:["Innocent XI, condemned propositions (1679)","St Alphonsus, Theologia Moralis","CCC 1776–1802 (conscience)"]},

{id:"moral-perverted",tract:"moral",level:3,note:"The teaching on contraception and sexual acts: Church teaching (Humanae Vitae 11–14; CCC 2357, 2370); the argument's formulation: philosophical",st:"Summa Contra Gentiles III.122; ST II-II q.154 a.11",
 q:"What is the 'perverted faculty argument', and is it any good?",
 one:"It argues that it is wrong to use a faculty in an act while deliberately frustrating the end that the act, by its nature, is ordered to; correctly stated it concerns the act's intrinsic end (finis operis), not the agent's purpose, and so it does not condemn chewing gum, celibacy, or marital acts in infertile periods.",
 plain:`<p>Thomas's natural law reasoning in the Summa Contra Gentiles (III.122) argues that the emission of seed is ordered to generation and so must not be used in a way that excludes it. Critics say this proves too much: is it wrong to use your ears to hold up spectacles, or to chew gum without swallowing?</p>
<p>Wagner's 'Six Misconceptions' clarifies the argument as the tradition holds it: (1) it concerns <em>acts</em>, not faculties: celibacy and periodic continence involve no act, so frustrate nothing; (2) it concerns the end of the act itself, not the agent's intention; (3) it is not the only moral rule: it judges the species of an act, not every circumstance; (4) accidental infertility (age, pregnancy) does not change the act's intrinsic order; (5) using a faculty <em>beyond</em> its end (praeter naturam: chewing gum) is not the same as acting <em>against</em> it (contra naturam), and is at most lightly disordered; (6) generation serves the good of the species, which is why sexual sins against it are grave, unlike, say, an amputation for the body's sake.</p>`,
 analogy:["Eating and purging","Thomas's critics' examples are trivial; a better one is the ancient Roman practice of feasting and then making oneself vomit to eat more. The act of eating is performed while deliberately defeating its nutritive end. Most people sense something wrong with this, even though no one else is harmed: that intuition is what the argument articulates."],
 deeper:`<p><strong>Feser's version.</strong> Edward Feser has defended the argument in several essays (e.g. 'In defense of the perverted faculty argument', in Neo-Scholastic Essays, 2015), grounding it in Aristotelian final causality: the good for a thing is to realise the ends its nature sets; so it can never be good to act in a way that contradicts those ends. He answers the 'chewing gum' and 'ears holding spectacles' objections with the distinction between using a faculty contrary to its end and merely using a body part for something else.</p>
<p><strong>Magisterium.</strong> Humanae Vitae (1968) teaches 'the inseparable connection… between the unitive significance and the procreative significance' of the marriage act (HV 12) and that 'each and every marital act must of necessity retain its intrinsic relationship to the procreation of human life' (HV 11). Natural family planning is permitted because it uses infertile periods without acting against the act's order (HV 16).</p>`,
 authors:[["Christian B. Wagner","thomism.com: '6 Misconceptions About the Perverted Faculty Argument' (2026); 'Sexual Morality and the Unmarried'; 'Marriage, Abstinence and Lent'","Restates the argument with the scholastic distinctions that answer most popular objections."],["Edward Feser","Neo-Scholastic Essays (2015): 'In Defense of the Perverted Faculty Argument'; blog posts on natural law and sexual ethics","A modern defence grounded in final causality."]],
 errors:[["The argument condemns all non-procreative uses of the body.","Only acts contrary to an act's intrinsic end, not uses beyond it."],["NFP is 'Catholic contraception'.","NFP performs no act contrary to the act's nature (HV 16)."]],
 tests:[["What is the difference between finis operis and finis operantis?","The end of the act itself versus the agent's purpose."],["Why does NFP not violate the argument?","No act is performed against its natural order; infertile periods are used."]],
 read:["Summa Contra Gentiles III.122","ST II-II q.154 aa.1, 11","Humanae Vitae 11–16","<a href=\"#q-contraception-nfp\">Questions: contraception and NFP</a>"]},

{id:"moral-double-effect",tract:"moral",level:2,note:"Principle of double effect: common teaching (rooted in ST II-II q.64 a.7)",st:"ST II-II q.64 a.7",
 q:"When may I do something that has a bad side-effect? The principle of double effect",
 one:"An act with a good and a bad effect may be done if the act itself is not evil, the bad effect is not intended (as end or as means), it is not the cause of the good effect, and there is a proportionately grave reason; Thomas first stated the core of it in discussing self-defence.",
 plain:`<p>Thomas on self-defence: 'Nothing hinders one act from having two effects, only one of which is intended, while the other is beside the intention. Now moral acts take their species according to what is intended' (II-II q.64 a.7). Defending oneself has two effects: saving one's life (intended) and the aggressor's death (beside the intention). But 'an act may be rendered unlawful, if it be out of proportion to the end'.</p>
<p>Later moralists formalised four conditions:</p>
<ol><li>The act in itself is good or indifferent.</li><li>The bad effect is not intended, only foreseen and tolerated.</li><li>The good effect does not come about by means of the bad effect.</li><li>There is a proportionately serious reason.</li></ol>`,
 analogy:["The surgeon","Removing a cancerous uterus from a pregnant woman to save her life (hysterectomy) foreseeably ends the pregnancy, but the act is the removal of a diseased organ; the death is a tolerated side-effect. Directly killing the child to save the mother would make the death the means, which is never permitted. This is the classic test case."],
 deeper:`<p><strong>Clinical relevance.</strong> Pain relief at the end of life that may shorten life (when death is not intended), treating ectopic pregnancy (debated: salpingectomy accepted; methotrexate disputed), and military actions with civilian casualties are standard applications. For a future clinical psychologist: confidentiality breaches to prevent harm to others are reasoned similarly.</p>
<p><strong>Proportionalism.</strong> Some moral theologians (1970s) reduced moral judgement to weighing goods and evils, making condition 4 everything. Veritatis Splendor (1993, nn. 74–83) rejected this: some acts are intrinsically evil regardless of consequences.</p>`,
 authors:[["Edward Feser","By Man Shall His Blood Be Shed (with Bessette, 2017); blog posts on double effect","Uses double effect to distinguish lawful self-defence and punishment from murder."]],
 errors:[["A good end justifies any means.","Rom 3:8; Veritatis Splendor 80."],["If I foresee a bad effect I must have intended it.","Foreseeing is not intending."]],
 tests:[["Name the four conditions.","Act good in itself; bad effect not intended; good not achieved by means of the bad; proportionate reason."]],
 read:["ST II-II q.64 a.7","Veritatis Splendor 71–83","CCC 2263–2265"]},

{id:"moral-death-penalty",tract:"moral",level:3,note:"Contested: CCC 2267 (revised 2018) calls the death penalty 'inadmissible'; its doctrinal status is debated",st:"ST II-II q.64 a.2",
 q:"Can the death penalty ever be legitimate? Thomas, the tradition and the 2018 revision",
 one:"Thomas taught that public authority may execute grave criminals for the common good; the Catechism was revised in 2018 to call the death penalty 'inadmissible' in light of human dignity; whether this is a development, a prudential judgement or a reversal is debated, and Feser and Bessette argue the traditional teaching remains binding.",
 plain:`<p>Thomas: every person is a part of the community as a part to a whole; 'if a man be dangerous and infectious to the community, on account of some sin, it is praiseworthy and advantageous that he be killed in order to safeguard the common good' (II-II q.64 a.2), but only by public authority (a.3). He grounds it in Scripture (Gen 9:6; Rom 13:4) and the Fathers, and the Roman Catechism (1566) and the 1992 Catechism taught it.</p>
<p>In 2018 Pope Francis revised CCC 2267: 'the death penalty is inadmissible because it is an attack on the inviolability and dignity of the person', and the Church 'works with determination for its abolition worldwide'. The accompanying letter of the CDF presented this as 'an authentic development of doctrine that is not in contradiction with the prior teachings'. The 2024 declaration Dignitas Infinita repeated the opposition.</p>`,
 analogy:["The amputation","Thomas uses the image of amputating a diseased limb to save the body. Critics reply that a person is not merely a limb: he has a dignity and an eternal destiny the state cannot override. Defenders reply that Thomas never meant the person is only a part, and that punishment honours the criminal as responsible."],
 deeper:`<p><strong>The debate.</strong> Feser and Bessette (By Man Shall His Blood Be Shed, 2017, written before the revision) argued that the legitimacy in principle of capital punishment is taught by Scripture, the Fathers and the popes so consistently that it is irreformable, while allowing popes to judge that it should not be used today. After 2018 Feser has argued that the revision can be read only as a prudential judgement, not as declaring the death penalty intrinsically evil, since that would contradict Scripture and the constant tradition. Others (e.g. E. Christian Brugger) argue the tradition always allowed development and that the revision is binding as stated.</p>
<p><strong>What every Catholic must hold.</strong> The current Catechism's teaching, received with religious submission (LG 25); the principle that the state has authority to punish; and the distinction between murder and lawful punishment. How to reconcile the revision with the tradition is the open theological question.</p>`,
 authors:[["Edward Feser","By Man Shall His Blood Be Shed: A Catholic Defense of Capital Punishment (with Joseph Bessette, 2017); many blog posts since 2018","The most thorough modern defence of the traditional teaching and the most-cited critic of reading the 2018 revision as a doctrinal reversal."],["Christian B. Wagner","thomism.com: 'Do All Papal Addresses Have Authority?'; 'When the Magisterium Forgets Doctrines'","Discusses how to weigh magisterial statements of different authority, which bears directly on this question."]],
 errors:[["The Church always taught the death penalty is wrong.","It taught its legitimacy for centuries (Innocent III's profession for the Waldensians, 1208; Roman Catechism)."],["Catholics may simply ignore CCC 2267.","Religious submission is owed; the debate concerns its theological status, not whether to take it seriously."]],
 tests:[["What did CCC 2267 say before and after 2018?","Before: permissible in very rare cases when no other means suffices; after: inadmissible."]],
 read:["ST II-II q.64 aa.2–3","CCC 2267 (2018)","CDF, Letter to Bishops on the revision (2018)","<a href=\"#lesson-t10\">Academy T10: capital punishment</a>"]}
);

// Disputed questions: the Church, what it is and how it works
DQ.push(
{id:"church-what",tract:"church",level:1,note:"De fide (Creed; Vatican I; LG 8)",st:"ST III q.8; LG 1–8",
 q:"What is the Church?",
 one:"The Church is at once the Mystical Body of Christ, animated by the Holy Spirit, and a visible, hierarchically ordered society founded by Christ; these are 'one complex reality', and the one Church of Christ 'subsists in' the Catholic Church governed by Peter's successor and the bishops in communion with him.",
 plain:`<p>St Robert Bellarmine's classic definition: the Church is 'the assembly of men bound together by the profession of the same Christian faith and the communion of the same sacraments, under the rule of legitimate pastors, and especially of the one Vicar of Christ on earth, the Roman Pontiff' (De Ecclesia militante III.2). Three bonds: faith, sacraments, government.</p>
<p>That is the visible side. Thomas stresses the inner side: Christ is head of the Church as the source of grace for all its members (III q.8 a.1), and the Holy Spirit is like its heart, giving life invisibly. Pius XII's Mystici Corporis (1943) united both: the Mystical Body of Christ is the Roman Catholic Church. Vatican II: 'the society structured with hierarchical organs and the Mystical Body of Christ… form one complex reality which coalesces from a divine and a human element… This Church… subsists in the Catholic Church' (LG 8).</p>`,
 analogy:["Body and soul","A human being is not a soul inhabiting a body as a pilot in a ship, but one living thing. The Church is not an invisible spiritual club with an optional institution attached; the visible body and its inner life of grace are one reality, as Christ is one person in two natures (LG 8 uses exactly this analogy)."],
 deeper:`<p><strong>Four causes of the Church.</strong> Efficient: Christ and the Spirit. Material: the members. Formal: the bonds of faith, sacraments and government (visible) and grace (invisible). Final: the glory of God and the salvation of souls.</p>
<p><strong>Members.</strong> Pius XII taught that only the baptised who profess the true faith and are not separated by schism, heresy or excommunication are 'really' members (Mystici Corporis 22). Vatican II speaks of being 'fully incorporated' (LG 14) and of other Christians being in 'imperfect communion' (UR 3). The two vocabularies are compatible.</p>
<p><strong>The four marks.</strong> One, holy, catholic, apostolic: signs by which the true Church can be recognised (see Academy T14).</p>`,
 authors:[["Christian B. Wagner","thomism.com: 'The Church as Necessary for the Act…'; 'The Church and the Formal Object of…'","Argues that the Church's infallible proposal is necessary for the act of divine faith, so the Church is not optional to faith."],["Garrigou-Lagrange","De Revelatione (On Divine Revelation, 1918/1950; Eng. 2022)","The classic Thomist treatise on revelation and the credibility of the Church as its guardian."]],
 errors:[["The Church is only invisible (the true believers known to God).","Rejected by Mystici Corporis and LG 8."],["The Church is only an institution.","It is the Body of Christ, animated by the Spirit."]],
 tests:[["Name Bellarmine's three bonds.","Profession of faith, communion of sacraments, rule of legitimate pastors under the Pope."]],
 read:["LG 1–17","Pius XII, Mystici Corporis (1943)","<a href=\"#lesson-t14\">Academy T14</a>"]},

{id:"church-structure",tract:"church",level:1,note:"Divine constitution (pope, bishops): de fide; organisation: ecclesiastical law",st:"LG 18–29; CIC; Praedicate Evangelium (2022)",
 q:"How does the Church actually work? Pope, bishops, Curia, dioceses, rites and orders",
 one:"By divine institution the Church is governed by the college of bishops with and under the Pope; around this core the Church has built, by its own law, the Roman Curia, dioceses and parishes, episcopal conferences, 24 self-governing ritual Churches, religious institutes and the laity's apostolate.",
 plain:`<div class="tablewrap"><table class="t"><thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>What it is</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>The Pope</td><td>Bishop of Rome, successor of Peter, with 'full, supreme and universal power' over the whole Church, ordinary and immediate (Vatican I, Pastor Aeternus ch. 3; LG 22)</td><td>Divine law</td></tr>
<tr><td>College of bishops</td><td>Successors of the apostles, with the Pope as head; supreme power exercised solemnly in an ecumenical council (LG 22)</td><td>Divine law</td></tr>
<tr><td>Diocese (eparchy)</td><td>A portion of God's people entrusted to a bishop; dioceses grouped into ecclesiastical provinces under a metropolitan archbishop</td><td>Ecclesiastical law (CIC cc. 368–572)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Parish</td><td>A community entrusted to a pastor (parish priest) under the bishop (cc. 515–552)</td><td>Ecclesiastical law</td></tr>
<tr><td>Roman Curia</td><td>The Pope's offices: since Praedicate Evangelium (2022), the Secretariat of State and 16 dicasteries (e.g. Evangelisation; Doctrine of the Faith; Causes of Saints; Bishops; Divine Worship), tribunals (Apostolic Penitentiary, Signatura, Rota) and economic bodies</td><td>Papal law</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cardinals</td><td>Advisers to the Pope; those under 80 elect him in conclave (Universi Dominici Gregis, 1996): a two-thirds majority is required</td><td>Ecclesiastical law</td></tr>
<tr><td>Episcopal conferences</td><td>Bishops of a nation or region acting together; in South Africa the Southern African Catholic Bishops' Conference (SACBC) covers South Africa, Botswana and Eswatini</td><td>Ecclesiastical law (cc. 447–459)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Churches sui iuris</td><td>The Latin Church and 23 Eastern Catholic Churches (e.g. Ukrainian, Maronite, Syro-Malabar), each with its own rite, law (CCEO 1990) and hierarchy, in full communion with Rome</td><td>Ecclesiastical law, apostolic traditions</td></tr>
<tr><td>Consecrated life</td><td>Religious orders (Dominicans, Jesuits, Franciscans…), secular institutes, societies of apostolic life; some 'exempt' and directly under the Pope</td><td>CIC cc. 573–746</td></tr>
<tr><td>Laity</td><td>Share in Christ's priestly, prophetic and kingly office; their proper task is to sanctify the world (LG 31)</td><td>Divine law (baptism)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p><strong>Law.</strong> The Latin Church is governed by the Code of Canon Law (1983); Eastern Catholic Churches by the Code of Canons of the Eastern Churches (1990). Both serve the 'supreme law': the salvation of souls (c. 1752). See Academy T16.</p>`,
 analogy:["A family, not a franchise","The Church is not a multinational with branch managers: each bishop is a true shepherd, not the Pope's delegate (LG 27). But the family has a father of the whole household, the Pope, without whom the bishops do not act as one body."],
 deeper:`<p><strong>How bishops are chosen.</strong> In the Latin Church, the apostolic nuncio consults widely and sends three names (a terna) to the Dicastery for Bishops, which advises the Pope; the Pope freely appoints (c. 377). Eastern Churches elect bishops in synod, with papal assent.</p>
<p><strong>How a pope is chosen.</strong> On the death or resignation of a pope (sede vacante), the cardinal electors meet in the Sistine Chapel; voting continues until one receives two-thirds. Pope Leo XIV (Robert Francis Prevost) was elected on 8 May 2025.</p>
<p><strong>Councils and synods.</strong> Twenty-one ecumenical councils are counted (Nicaea 325 to Vatican II 1962–65). The Synod of Bishops (since 1965) advises the Pope; its 2021–2024 session on synodality concluded with a final document the Pope adopted.</p>`,
 authors:[["Christian B. Wagner","christianbwagner.com: 'Spiritually Preparing for the Conclave' (2025); thomism.com: 'Do All Papal Addresses Have Authority?'","Explains how to understand papal acts and the vacancy of the See."]],
 errors:[["Bishops are the Pope's branch managers.","They govern by their own ordinary power (LG 27)."],["Eastern Catholics are 'less Catholic'.","They are fully Catholic, in their own Churches sui iuris."]],
 tests:[["How many Churches sui iuris are there?","24: the Latin Church and 23 Eastern Catholic Churches."],["What majority elects a pope?","Two-thirds of the cardinal electors present."]],
 read:["LG 18–29","Code of Canon Law (1983)","Praedicate Evangelium (2022)","<a href=\"#lesson-t16\">Academy T16: canon law</a>"]},

{id:"church-magisterium",tract:"church",level:2,note:"Church teaching (LG 25; Ad Tuendam Fidem 1998; CDF Doctrinal Commentary 1998; Donum Veritatis 1990)",st:"LG 25; II-II q.1 a.10",
 q:"Is everything the Pope or a council says equally binding? The levels of Church teaching",
 one:"No: the Church distinguishes truths divinely revealed (to be believed with divine and catholic faith), truths definitively proposed though not revealed (to be firmly held), and authentic but non-definitive teaching (owed 'religious submission of mind and will'), with prudential and disciplinary acts weighed differently again.",
 plain:`<div class="tablewrap"><table class="t"><thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Assent owed</th><th>Examples</th><th>Denial is</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>1. Divinely revealed, infallibly proposed (solemn definition or ordinary universal magisterium)</td><td>Divine and catholic faith (credenda)</td><td>Trinity, Real Presence, Immaculate Conception, papal infallibility</td><td>Heresy</td></tr>
<tr><td>2. Definitively proposed truths connected to revelation (secondary objects)</td><td>Firm acceptance (tenenda)</td><td>Legitimacy of a pope's election; canonizations (commonly held); reserved priestly ordination to men (Ordinatio Sacerdotalis); illicitness of euthanasia</td><td>Rejecting a truth of Catholic doctrine</td></tr>
<tr><td>3. Authentic, non-definitive teaching</td><td>Religious submission of intellect and will (LG 25)</td><td>Much of encyclicals, catechetical teaching</td><td>Not heresy, but a failure of due submission</td></tr>
<tr><td>4. Prudential judgements, disciplinary decisions, applications</td><td>Respect and obedience as appropriate</td><td>Liturgical regulations, pastoral strategies, judgements of fact in changing circumstances</td><td>Disobedience if binding law; otherwise legitimate disagreement possible</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>The categories come from the Profession of Faith added by Ad Tuendam Fidem (1998) and explained in the CDF's Doctrinal Commentary (1998). Donum Veritatis (1990) explains how a theologian may, after study, have difficulties with non-definitive teaching, raise them through proper channels, and not engage in public dissent.</p>`,
 analogy:["The doctor's instructions","A doctor's diagnosis of a broken bone shown on an X-ray (category 1), her established medical principles (2), her considered advice (3), and her suggestion of which pharmacy to use (4) all deserve respect, but not the same kind."],
 deeper:`<p><strong>The ordinary universal magisterium.</strong> Doctrines can be infallibly taught without a solemn definition, when the bishops dispersed throughout the world, in communion with the Pope, teach a doctrine as definitively to be held (LG 25). Many moral teachings belong here.</p>
<p><strong>Papal addresses.</strong> A Wednesday audience, an interview or a homily is not of the same weight as an encyclical; the intention, manner and frequency of teaching determine its weight (LG 25). Wagner's 'Do All Papal Addresses Have Authority?' and 'Against Popesplaining' warn against both treating every papal remark as dogma and explaining away clear teaching.</p>
<p><strong>Thomas.</strong> 'It belongs to the authority of the Sovereign Pontiff' to publish a new edition of the creed, because a matter of faith must be decided by him who presides over the whole Church (II-II q.1 a.10).</p>`,
 authors:[["Christian B. Wagner","thomism.com: 'Do All Papal Addresses Have Authority?'; 'Against Popesplaining: A Diagnosis and Refutation'; 'When the Magisterium Forgets Doctrines'; 'Scheeben's Explanation of the Magisterium'; 'Scheeben's Justification of the Magisterium'","Uses Scheeben and the scholastics to explain degrees of magisterial authority and the right response to each."],["Edward Feser","Blog posts on papal authority, the death penalty revision and Amoris Laetitia","Argues that not all papal statements are equally binding and that the magisterium cannot contradict previous definitive teaching."]],
 errors:[["Only ex cathedra statements bind.","Religious submission is owed to authentic teaching (LG 25)."],["Every word of a pope is infallible.","Infallibility is limited to definitions and definitive teaching."]],
 tests:[["What assent is owed to non-definitive authentic teaching?","Religious submission of intellect and will."],["Which document introduced the three paragraphs of the Profession of Faith?","Ad Tuendam Fidem (1998), with the CDF's Doctrinal Commentary."]],
 read:["LG 25","CDF, Doctrinal Commentary on the Professio Fidei (1998)","Donum Veritatis (1990)"]},

{id:"church-infallibility",tract:"church",level:2,note:"De fide (Vatican I, Pastor Aeternus ch. 4, 1870; LG 25)",st:"Pastor Aeternus ch. 4; II-II q.1 a.10; q.11 a.2 ad 3",
 q:"What exactly is papal infallibility, and how often has it been used?",
 one:"Infallibility is the gift by which the Pope, when he defines a doctrine of faith or morals to be held by the whole Church, speaking ex cathedra, cannot err; it is not impeccability or inspiration, and solemn papal definitions have been rare.",
 plain:`<p>Vatican I (1870) defined that 'when the Roman Pontiff speaks ex cathedra, that is, when, in the discharge of the office of pastor and teacher of all Christians, in virtue of his supreme apostolic authority, he defines a doctrine concerning faith or morals to be held by the whole Church, he possesses… that infallibility which the divine Redeemer willed his Church to enjoy'. Such definitions are 'of themselves, and not by the consent of the Church, irreformable'.</p>
<p>Conditions: (1) the Pope as universal teacher, (2) using his supreme authority, (3) defining (a final decision), (4) on faith or morals, (5) for the whole Church. It does not mean popes are sinless, wise in all decisions, or receive new revelation: 'the Holy Spirit was not promised to the successors of Peter that by his revelation they might make known new doctrine, but that by his assistance they might religiously guard and faithfully expound the revelation… the deposit of faith' (Pastor Aeternus ch. 4).</p>
<p>Clear examples of ex cathedra definitions: the Immaculate Conception (1854) and the Assumption (1950); many theologians add Benedictus Deus (1336) and some earlier acts. Councils with the Pope also define infallibly (e.g. Nicaea, Chalcedon, Trent).</p>`,
 analogy:["The referee's final whistle","A referee can make mistakes during the game, have bad days and even be unfair. But the rules give him the final word on certain decisions so the game can go on. Infallibility is greater than this (it is a guarantee of truth, not only of finality), but it is similarly limited to a specific act."],
 deeper:`<p><strong>Thomas's anticipation.</strong> Thomas taught that a new creed may be published only by the authority of the Pope, 'to whom the more important and more difficult questions that arise in the Church are referred', and he cites Christ's prayer that Peter's faith fail not (Lk 22:32) (II-II q.1 a.10). He also held that 'the universal Church cannot err, since she is governed by the Holy Ghost' (II-II q.1 a.9).</p>
<p><strong>Infallibility of the Church.</strong> The Pope's infallibility is that of the Church. The whole body of the faithful 'cannot err in matters of belief' when they show universal agreement (sensus fidei, LG 12). The bishops teaching together with the Pope are infallible in the ordinary universal magisterium (LG 25).</p>`,
 authors:[["Christian B. Wagner","thomism.com: 'Why Most Biblical Arguments for the Papacy Suck (and How to Fix Them)'; 'Against Popesplaining'","Criticises weak biblical arguments for the papacy and offers stronger ones; distinguishes infallible from non-infallible papal acts."],["Garrigou-Lagrange","De Revelatione","Treats the Church's infallibility as part of the credibility of revelation."]],
 errors:[["The Pope is infallible about everything.","Only in definitions of faith and morals."],["Infallibility was invented in 1870.","The doctrine was defined in 1870 but long believed (Lyons II 1274; Florence 1439; Thomas)."]],
 scripture:[["Mt 16:18–19","upon this rock; the keys"],["Lk 22:31–32","that thy faith fail not; confirm thy brethren"],["Jn 21:15–17","feed my sheep"]],
 tests:[["Name the conditions of an ex cathedra definition.","As universal pastor, with supreme authority, defining, on faith or morals, for the whole Church."]],
 read:["Vatican I, Pastor Aeternus ch. 4","LG 25","CCC 888–892"]},

{id:"church-secondary",tract:"church",level:3,note:"Infallibility in secondary objects: theologically certain; canonizations: common teaching; disciplinary laws: 'negative' infallibility, common teaching",st:"Quodlibet IX q.8; II-II q.10 a.12",
 q:"Are canonizations and universal Church laws infallible?",
 one:"Most theologians, following Thomas, hold that a solemn canonization is infallible, since the Church cannot propose a damned person for universal veneration; universal disciplinary laws are commonly held 'negatively' infallible, meaning they cannot command anything contrary to faith or morals, though they may be imprudent and can be changed.",
 plain:`<p><strong>Secondary objects.</strong> Besides revealed truths, the Church can teach infallibly truths so connected with revelation that without them revelation could not be guarded: dogmatic facts (that a council was legitimate, that a pope was validly elected), canonizations, approval of religious rules, and universal disciplinary laws.</p>
<p><strong>Canonizations.</strong> Thomas asked whether all canonized saints are in glory, and answered that since the Pope is guided by the Spirit and the Church's judgement on such matters concerns the whole Church's worship, 'it is to be piously believed that even here the judgment of the Church is not liable to error' (Quodlibet IX q.8). Later theologians raised the note to theologically certain. Wagner's 'The Infallibility of Canonizations' gathers these authorities.</p>
<p><strong>Disciplinary laws.</strong> A universal law of the Church (e.g. liturgical law) cannot command something sinful or harmful to faith; Pius VI condemned the Synod of Pistoia's claim that the Church could establish discipline 'dangerous and harmful' (Auctorem Fidei 78, 1794). This does not make every law wise or permanent.</p>`,
 analogy:["The guardrail","Infallibility in secondary matters is like a guardrail: it does not steer the car or guarantee the best route, but it stops the car going over the cliff into error."],
 deeper:`<p><strong>Liturgy and custom.</strong> Thomas: 'the custom of the Church has very great authority and ought to be jealously observed in all things' (II-II q.10 a.12), using the example of infant baptism. This underlies the principle lex orandi, lex credendi.</p>
<p><strong>Debates.</strong> Since the rapid canonizations after 1983 and changes in procedure (e.g. waiving miracles), some have questioned whether infallibility applies. Others answer that infallibility attaches to the final papal act, not to the thoroughness of the process.</p>`,
 authors:[["Christian B. Wagner","thomism.com: 'The Infallibility of Canonizations'; 'The Infallibility of Disciplinary…' (two essays)","Collects scholastic and post-Tridentine authorities on infallibility in canonizations and universal discipline."]],
 errors:[["If a law is infallible it can never change.","Negative infallibility means it is not harmful, not that it is irreformable."]],
 tests:[["What did Thomas say about canonizations?","It is to be piously believed that the Church's judgement here is not liable to error."]],
 read:["Thomas, Quodlibet IX q.8","ST II-II q.10 a.12","Pius VI, Auctorem Fidei (1794)"]},

{id:"church-papal-error",tract:"church",level:3,note:"Indefectibility of the Roman See in faith: de fide in its defined sense; 'heretic pope' scenarios: open theological questions",st:"Bellarmine, De Romano Pontifice II.30",
 q:"Can a pope err, or even become a heretic? What then?",
 one:"A pope can err in non-infallible teaching and can sin gravely; whether a pope could become a formal heretic as a private person is debated among theologians (Bellarmine thought it unlikely in fact), and the procedures discussed for such a case are theological opinions, not Church law, which states that 'the First See is judged by no one' (c. 1404).",
 plain:`<p>Historical cases: Pope Honorius I (d. 638) was condemned by the Third Council of Constantinople (681) for favouring Monothelitism in his letters, not in an ex cathedra definition. Pope John XXII (1331–34) preached that souls do not see God until the Last Judgment, retracted before his death; his successor defined the opposite (Benedictus Deus 1336). These show popes can err when not defining.</p>
<p>Could a pope become a formal heretic? Bellarmine listed five opinions: (1) a pope cannot become a heretic (the pious view he thought probable); (2) a heretic pope loses office automatically even for occult heresy; (3) he does not lose office even for manifest heresy; (4) he loses office only when declared by the Church; (5) he loses office automatically when his heresy is manifest. Bellarmine favoured the fifth as a hypothesis; Cajetan and John of St Thomas held versions of the fourth.</p>
<p>The Church's law: 'The First See is judged by no one' (CIC c. 1404). No canonical procedure exists for deposing a pope. Sedevacantists claim recent popes lost office; the Church rejects this, and most theologians hold that the peaceful universal acceptance of a pope is an infallible sign (dogmatic fact) of his legitimacy.</p>`,
 analogy:["Weather and climate","A pope's personal errors are weather: storms come and go. The Church's indefectibility is the climate: guaranteed by Christ's promise. Mistaking a storm for a change of climate is the error of panic; denying there is ever any bad weather is the error of 'popesplaining'."],
 deeper:`<p><strong>Universal peaceful acceptance.</strong> John of St Thomas and others argued that when the whole Church accepts a man as pope, this is an infallible sign that he is pope. This is why claims that recent popes are not popes are rejected as contrary to a dogmatic fact.</p>
<p><strong>Responding to papal error.</strong> Thomas: 'if the faith were endangered, a subject ought to rebuke his prelate even publicly', as Paul rebuked Peter (II-II q.33 a.4 ad 2), but with respect and without denying his office.</p>`,
 schools:`<p>Live debates since 2016 (the 'dubia' on Amoris Laetitia, the 2019 open letter accusing Francis of heresy, sedevacantist arguments) revived Bellarmine's scenarios. Wagner has defended Bellarmine against misreadings and has been criticised by sedevacantist writers on 'loss of office and heresy'. Feser has written that a pope can teach error in non-infallible acts and that the faithful may respectfully resist, while rejecting sedevacantism.</p>`,
 authors:[["Christian B. Wagner","thomism.com: 'Some Defenses of St. Robert Bellarmine's…'; 'Against Popesplaining'; 'The Crisis in the Church is a Judgement…'","Engages the theology of papal error and the heretic-pope debates using Bellarmine and the scholastics."],["Edward Feser","Blog posts 2016–2025 on papal fallibility and the limits of papal authority","Distinguishes infallible from fallible papal teaching and rejects both sedevacantism and uncritical 'papolatry'."]],
 errors:[["Sedevacantism.","Contrary to the dogmatic fact of the popes' universal acceptance and to Christ's promise to Peter's See."],["Ultramontane 'papolatry': whatever a pope says is right.","Contrary to Honorius, John XXII and Pastor Aeternus's own limits."]],
 tests:[["Which pope did an ecumenical council condemn?","Honorius I (Constantinople III, 681)."],["What does c. 1404 say?","The First See is judged by no one."]],
 read:["Bellarmine, De Romano Pontifice II.30","CIC c. 1404","Pastor Aeternus ch. 4"]},

{id:"church-eens",tract:"church",level:2,note:"'Outside the Church no salvation': de fide (Lateran IV; Florence); its interpretation: Church teaching (Pius IX; Holy Office 1949; LG 14–16)",st:"II-II q.2 aa.5–8; III q.68 a.2",
 q:"'No salvation outside the Church': what does it mean today?",
 one:"It is a dogma that all salvation comes through Christ and his Church, so that anyone who knows the Church is necessary and refuses to enter or remain cannot be saved; but those who without fault do not know the Church can be saved by grace if they seek God and follow conscience, being related to the Church by implicit desire.",
 plain:`<p>Strong statements: Lateran IV (1215): 'There is one universal Church of the faithful, outside of which no one at all is saved.' Florence (1442) applied it to 'pagans, Jews, heretics and schismatics' who do not join the Church before death. Boniface VIII (Unam Sanctam, 1302): 'it is altogether necessary to salvation for every human creature to be subject to the Roman Pontiff.'</p>
<p>Equally clear: Pius IX (Quanto Conficiamur, 1863): those 'who labour in invincible ignorance of our most holy religion' and keep the natural law can attain eternal life 'by the operating power of divine light and grace'. The Holy Office (1949, against Fr Feeney) taught that one may be united to the Church by an implicit desire, if animated by supernatural faith and perfect charity. Vatican II: 'whosoever, knowing that the Catholic Church was made necessary by Christ, would refuse to enter or to remain in it, could not be saved' (LG 14); those who 'through no fault of their own do not know the Gospel… but who nevertheless seek God with a sincere heart… may achieve eternal salvation' (LG 16).</p>
<p>Both sets of statements are true: there is no salvation <em>apart from</em> the Church, but there can be salvation for some not visibly <em>in</em> it.</p>`,
 analogy:["The ark and the swimmer","Noah's ark is the only vessel of salvation. One who sees it and refuses to board perishes by his own choice. One who never saw it, but is swimming toward the light with all his strength, may be drawn aboard by the Captain in ways the passengers do not see. Either way, salvation is only by the ark."],
 deeper:`<p><strong>How much faith is needed?</strong> Thomas taught that after Christ, explicit faith in Christ and the Trinity is necessary for salvation (II-II q.2 aa.7–8), while before Christ implicit faith in a redeemer sufficed. To reconcile this with invincible ignorance, he says that if someone brought up in the wilderness follows reason and seeks good, God will reveal what is necessary 'either by an inner inspiration or by sending a preacher' (De Veritate q.14 a.11 ad 1). Post-Tridentine theologians debated whether explicit faith in God as rewarder (Heb 11:6) suffices in invincible ignorance; Wagner's essays on implicit and explicit faith trace this debate.</p>
<p><strong>Not indifferentism.</strong> Pius IX and Vatican II both reject the idea that all religions are equally ways of salvation (Dominus Iesus, 2000). Grace outside the Church is Christ's grace, ordered to the Church, and the Church's mission remains urgent (Ad Gentes 7). Wagner also gathers the tradition's sober teaching that the saved may be few (Mt 7:13–14), as a motive for mission, not despair.</p>`,
 authors:[["Christian B. Wagner","thomism.com and blog: 'Outside the Church There is No Salvation, Vatican I, and Ven. Pius XII'; 'The Key to Understanding Extra Ecclesiam Nulla Salus and Vatican II'; 'Notes on Implicit Faith, Explicit Faith and Salvation'; 'On Implicit Faith and the Fathers'; 'Confused vs Implicit Knowledge of…'; 'Few Catholics Will be Saved'","Shows the continuity between the strong medieval formulas and Pius IX, Pius XII and Vatican II, and examines the Thomist debate on explicit faith."],["Garrigou-Lagrange","De Revelatione; Predestination","Defends the necessity of supernatural faith for salvation while allowing for God's provision for the invincibly ignorant."]],
 errors:[["Feeneyism: only the visibly baptised Catholics can be saved.","Rejected by the Holy Office (1949)."],["Indifferentism: all religions save equally.","Rejected by Pius IX, Vatican II, Dominus Iesus."]],
 scripture:[["Mk 16:16","he that believeth not shall be condemned"],["Acts 4:12","no other name under heaven"],["1 Tim 2:4","God will have all men to be saved"],["Rom 2:14–16","Gentiles who do by nature the things of the law"]],
 tests:[["Who, according to LG 14, cannot be saved?","Those who know the Church is necessary and refuse to enter or remain."],["What did the 1949 Holy Office letter teach?","Union with the Church can be by implicit desire animated by faith and charity."]],
 read:["LG 14–16","Holy Office, Letter to Cushing (1949)","CCC 846–848","<a href=\"#dq-sac-desire\">Baptism of desire</a>"]},

{id:"church-development",tract:"church",level:3,note:"Dogma does not change in meaning: de fide (Vatican I, Dei Filius ch. 4 and can. 4.3); theories of development: theological",st:"Dei Filius ch. 4; II-II q.1 a.7",
 q:"How can doctrine develop without changing?",
 one:"Doctrine develops when the Church understands more explicitly what was always contained in revelation, 'in the same sense and the same meaning'; a new teaching that contradicts an earlier definitive one is corruption, not development.",
 plain:`<p>Vincent of Lérins (434): the Church's doctrine must grow 'in the same doctrine, the same sense and the same meaning' (Commonitorium 23), as a child grows into a man without becoming something else. Vatican I adopted these words and condemned the view that dogmas can be given a different sense as science progresses (Dei Filius ch. 4, can. 3).</p>
<p>Thomas: 'as regards the substance of the articles of faith, they have not received any increase as time went on: since whatever those who lived later have believed, was contained, albeit implicitly, in the faith of those Fathers who preceded them' (II-II q.1 a.7); the increase is in explicit profession. What the Fathers believed implicitly, later ages state explicitly.</p>
<p>Newman's Essay on the Development of Christian Doctrine (1845) gave seven 'notes' of genuine development: preservation of type, continuity of principles, power of assimilation, logical sequence, anticipation of its future, conservative action on its past, chronic vigour.</p>`,
 analogy:["The oak and the acorn","An oak looks nothing like an acorn, but it is the same tree grown, not a different tree. If an acorn grew into a pine, that would not be development but replacement."],
 deeper:`<p><strong>Formal and virtual revelation.</strong> Thomists debated what can be defined as dogma. Marín-Sola (The Homogeneous Evolution of Catholic Dogma, 1924) argued that truths 'virtually revealed' (deduced from revealed truths by a conclusion using a natural premise) can be defined as dogma, explaining the Immaculate Conception. Others (Schultes, Garrigou-Lagrange in part) held only what is formally revealed, explicitly or implicitly, can be dogma. Wagner's essays on formal and virtual revelation, the 'affective mode' of development, and the Vincentian canon engage this debate.</p>
<p><strong>Modernism and nouvelle théologie.</strong> Pius X (Pascendi, 1907) condemned the view that dogma evolves with religious experience. Garrigou-Lagrange's 1946 article 'La nouvelle théologie où va-t-elle?' accused some theologians of relativising dogmatic formulas; Pius XII's Humani Generis (1950) warned against such tendencies.</p>`,
 authors:[["Christian B. Wagner","thomism.com: 'Formal and Virtual Revelation'; 'The Affective Mode of Doctrinal Development'; 'Does the Vincentian Canon Destroy…'; 'When the Magisterium Forgets Doctrines'; 'The Consensus of the Fathers and…'; 'Is the Canon Implicitly Contained…'","Explores how revelation is contained and unfolded, using Marín-Sola, Scheeben and the scholastics."],["Garrigou-Lagrange","'La nouvelle théologie où va-t-elle?' (1946); Le sens commun (Thomistic Common Sense); De Revelatione","Defends the permanence of dogmatic formulas and the realism of the intellect against relativism."]],
 errors:[["Dogma evolves with experience (modernism).","Pascendi; Dei Filius can. 4.3."],["Nothing can ever be newly defined.","Development of explicit understanding is real."]],
 tests:[["What is Vincent's formula?","The same doctrine, the same sense, the same meaning."],["How did the articles of faith increase, according to Thomas?","In explicit profession, not in substance."]],
 read:["Vincent of Lérins, Commonitorium","Newman, Essay on Development (1845)","ST II-II q.1 a.7","<a href=\"#lesson-t1\">Academy T1</a>"]},

{id:"church-notes",tract:"church",level:2,note:"Theological method (manualist tradition)",st:"Ott, Fundamentals of Catholic Dogma; Salaverri",
 q:"How certain is it? Theological notes and censures explained",
 one:"Theologians grade every proposition by how certain it is (from 'de fide' down to 'tolerated opinion') and every error by how serious it is (from 'heresy' to 'offensive to pious ears'), so you can tell what you must believe, what is safe to hold, and what is free.",
 plain:`<div class="tablewrap"><table class="t"><thead><tr><th>Note (certainty)</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Opposite censure</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>De fide divina et catholica (dogma)</td><td>Revealed and defined or universally taught as revealed</td><td>Heresy</td></tr>
<tr><td>De fide ecclesiastica definita</td><td>Not revealed but infallibly defined (secondary object)</td><td>Error in ecclesiastical faith</td></tr>
<tr><td>Proxima fidei (close to faith)</td><td>Commonly regarded as revealed, not yet defined</td><td>Proximate to heresy</td></tr>
<tr><td>Theologically certain (theologice certa)</td><td>A necessary conclusion from a revealed truth and a certain natural truth</td><td>Theological error</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sententia communis (common teaching)</td><td>Held generally by theologians; free in principle</td><td>Rash</td></tr>
<tr><td>Probable, more probable, well-founded, pious opinion</td><td>Held for good reasons; not binding</td><td>None</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tolerated opinion</td><td>Weakly founded but allowed</td><td>None</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>Further censures: 'savouring of heresy', 'suspect of heresy', 'scandalous', 'offensive to pious ears', 'schismatic'. The notes on each disputed question in this section use this scale.</p>`,
 analogy:["A map with confidence ratings","A geologist's map marks some boundaries as surveyed, some as inferred, some as conjectural. Theological notes are such ratings: they help you know where the ground is firm."],
 deeper:`<p><strong>Use with care.</strong> Notes in the manuals (Ott's Fundamentals of Catholic Dogma, 1952; Salaverri; Tanquerey) are theologians' judgements, not themselves magisterial, though the higher notes reflect magisterial acts. Since Vatican II the Church uses the 1998 three-paragraph scheme (see 'levels of Church teaching'), which maps onto the higher notes.</p>`,
 authors:[["Christian B. Wagner","christianbwagner.com: 'Recommended Reading List of Manuals in Philosophy and Theology'; 'A Little Catechism on Logic'","Recommends the classic manuals that use theological notes and teaches the logic needed to read them."]],
 tests:[["What is the opposite of a dogma?","Heresy."],["What is 'theologically certain'?","A necessary conclusion from a revealed premise and a certain natural premise."]],
 read:["Ludwig Ott, Fundamentals of Catholic Dogma, Introduction §8","<a href=\"#lesson-t17\">Academy T17: theological notes</a>"]},

{id:"church-schism",tract:"church",level:2,note:"Definitions: canon law (cc. 751, 1364); current facts as of July 2026",st:"CIC cc. 751, 1364, 1382; II-II q.39",
 q:"What are heresy, apostasy and schism, and how does excommunication work?",
 one:"Heresy is the obstinate denial of a truth of divine and catholic faith; apostasy the total repudiation of the Christian faith; schism the refusal of submission to the Pope or of communion with the Church; each incurs automatic excommunication for those who commit them knowingly, which bars from the sacraments until reconciliation.",
 plain:`<p>Canon 751 defines: heresy, 'the obstinate denial or obstinate doubt after the reception of baptism of some truth which is to be believed by divine and catholic faith'; apostasy, 'the total repudiation of the Christian faith'; schism, 'the refusal of submission to the Supreme Pontiff or of communion with the members of the Church subject to him'. Canon 1364 imposes latae sententiae (automatic) excommunication on apostates, heretics and schismatics.</p>
<p>Thomas: schism is opposed to the unity of charity, which unites the members under one head (II-II q.39 a.1). Unlike heresy, it need not involve a doctrinal error, though it tends to produce one.</p>
<p>Excommunication is a medicinal penalty: it forbids receiving the sacraments and exercising ecclesiastical functions until the person repents (c. 1331). It does not 'cut someone off' from God's mercy, and it can be lifted.</p>`,
 analogy:["The tourniquet","Excommunication is like a tourniquet: painful, temporary, meant to save life, and removed when healing begins."],
 deeper:`<p><strong>Material and formal.</strong> Someone raised in a non-Catholic community who does not know the Catholic faith's claims is not a formal heretic or schismatic; the Church regards such Christians as brothers in imperfect communion (UR 3). Only knowing, obstinate rejection makes formal heresy or schism.</p>
<p><strong>A current case.</strong> The Society of St Pius X's 1988 episcopal consecrations without papal mandate led to excommunications (c. 1382), lifted for the four bishops in 2009; Rome later granted faculties for confession (2015/16) and marriage (2017). On 1 July 2026 the Society consecrated four new bishops despite a last-minute admonition from Pope Leo XIV; on 2 July 2026 Rome declared the six bishops excommunicated as schismatics and said those who formally adhere to the schism place themselves in the same situation. Wagner has written several essays on the SSPX question; this page reports facts only.</p>`,
 authors:[["Christian B. Wagner","thomism.com: 'On Invalid and Unjust Excommunications'; 'The SSPX Discourse Needs to Change'; 'Cardinal Müller's SSPX Error'; 'So About the SSPX Episcopal Consecrations'; YouTube: 'Fr. Gregory Pine on the SSPX'","Treats excommunication, its validity and limits, and the theological issues around the SSPX."]],
 errors:[["All non-Catholic Christians are formal heretics.","Formal heresy requires knowing, obstinate denial (UR 3)."],["Excommunication means damnation.","It is a medicinal penalty."]],
 tests:[["Define schism.","Refusal of submission to the Pope or of communion with the Church's members subject to him (c. 751)."]],
 read:["CIC cc. 751, 1331, 1364, 1382","ST II-II qq.11, 39"]},

{id:"church-state",tract:"church",level:3,note:"Duty of societies toward the true religion: traditional teaching (Immortale Dei; DH 1); religious liberty: Church teaching (DH 2); integralism: disputed",st:"Dignitatis Humanae 1–2; Immortale Dei (1885)",
 q:"How should the Church relate to the state? Religious liberty and integralism",
 one:"The Church teaches both that societies have a moral duty toward the true religion and that every person has a right not to be coerced in religious matters within just limits; how these fit together, and whether a confessional Catholic state is the ideal (integralism), is debated.",
 plain:`<p>Leo XIII (Immortale Dei, 1885; Libertas, 1888) taught that the state, as a creature of God, should honour God and favour the true religion, while tolerating other cults for the sake of peace. Pius IX's Syllabus (1864) condemned the proposition that the Church should be separated from the state.</p>
<p>Vatican II's Dignitatis Humanae (1965) taught that 'the human person has a right to religious freedom', meaning 'immunity from coercion… within due limits' (DH 2), rooted in human dignity. It also stated that it 'leaves untouched traditional Catholic doctrine on the moral duty of men and societies toward the true religion and toward the one Church of Christ' (DH 1).</p>`,
 analogy:["Truth and the conscience","A good teacher wants every student to know the truth and arranges the classroom to favour it, but does not beat an answer into a student, because forced assent is not real knowledge. The debate is about how much the classroom (the state) may and should do."],
 deeper:`<p><strong>The debate.</strong> 'Integralists' (e.g. Thomas Pink, Edmund Waldstein, Thomas Crean and Alan Fimister's Integralism, 2020) argue that the state should be subordinate to the Church in spiritual matters and that DH concerns only coercion by the state on its own authority. Others (John Courtney Murray, many contemporary theologians) read DH as a development toward a state that is neutral among religions while respecting natural law. Wagner's essays ('The State Needs the Church', 'Catholic Political Philosophy', 'Blasphemy Laws and the American Tradition', 'On Nationalism and Catholicism', 'National Socialism is Irrational') defend a confessional view while rejecting ethno-nationalism.</p>
<p><strong>South African context.</strong> The Constitution (s. 15) protects freedom of religion; the Church in South Africa has worked within it, notably in opposing apartheid (SACBC's 1957 statement calling apartheid 'intrinsically evil') and in public debates on life and family.</p>`,
 authors:[["Christian B. Wagner","thomism.com: 'The State Needs the Church'; 'Catholic Political Philosophy'; 'Blasphemy Laws and the American Tradition'; 'On the Legitimacy of the United States'; 'On Nationalism and Catholicism'; christianbwagner.com: 'Forming a Catholic Democracy by Pius VII'","Argues for the traditional confessional teaching applied to modern democracies."],["Edward Feser","All One in Christ (2022); blog posts on political philosophy and natural law","Defends natural-law politics and the common good, and critiques racial ideologies from Thomist principles."]],
 errors:[["Religion is purely private.","DH 1 and Immortale Dei."],["The state may force conversion.","DH 2; the Church has always held faith must be free (II-II q.10 a.8)."]],
 tests:[["What does DH 1 say it leaves untouched?","Traditional doctrine on the moral duty of men and societies toward the true religion."]],
 read:["Dignitatis Humanae 1–3","Leo XIII, Immortale Dei (1885)","ST II-II q.10 aa.8, 11"]}
);

// Disputed questions: the last things
DQ.push(
{id:"last-hell",tract:"last",level:2,note:"Existence and eternity of hell: de fide (Lateran IV; Benedictus Deus; CCC 1035)",st:"ST I-II q.87 aa.3–4; Suppl. q.99",
 q:"How can a finite sin deserve eternal punishment?",
 one:"Mortal sin turns away from the infinite Good, and its 'pain of loss' is the loss of that Good; the punishment lasts because the sinner's will remains fixed in its turning away after death, so hell is the eternal confirmation of a freely chosen separation, not God's revenge.",
 plain:`<p>Thomas: 'sin comprises two things. First, there is the turning away from the immutable good, which is infinite, wherefore, in this respect, sin is infinite. Secondly, there is the inordinate turning to mutable good' (I-II q.87 a.4). The first deserves the 'pain of loss', 'infinite, because it is the loss of the infinite good, i.e. God'; the second the 'pain of sense', finite and varying with the sin.</p>
<p>Why eternal? Not because punishment must last as long as the act. Thomas notes that even human law punishes a brief crime with lifelong penalties (Suppl. q.99 a.1). Rather: 'Duration of punishment corresponds to duration of fault, not indeed as regards the act, but on the part of the stain, for as long as this remains' (I-II q.87 a.4 ad 3). A mortal sin destroys the principle of the order to God (charity), which cannot be restored except by God's grace (I-II q.87 a.3). After death the will is fixed and no longer seeks that grace (see the angels' fall). So the stain, and with it the punishment, remains.</p>`,
 analogy:["The door locked from inside","C.S. Lewis: 'the doors of hell are locked on the inside'. The damned are not straining toward God and kept out; they have chosen and keep choosing themselves over him. The image is Lewis's; the theology of the fixed will is Thomas's."],
 deeper:`<p><strong>Catechism.</strong> 'To die in mortal sin without repenting and accepting God's merciful love means remaining separated from him for ever by our own free choice. This state of definitive self-exclusion from communion with God and the blessed is called \"hell\"' (CCC 1033). 'The chief punishment of hell is eternal separation from God' (CCC 1035).</p>
<p><strong>Universalism.</strong> Recent proposals that all may be saved (David Bentley Hart's That All Shall Be Saved, 2019) or that we may 'dare to hope' hell is empty (Balthasar) are debated. Origenist universal restoration was condemned (543/553). Balthasar's 'hope' is distinct from asserting universal salvation. Feser has written extensively against universalism; Garrigou-Lagrange's Life Everlasting defends the classical doctrine.</p>`,
 authors:[["Edward Feser","Blog essays on hell, the fixity of the will and universalism (incl. reviews of D.B. Hart)","Argues from the Thomist account of the separated soul's will that eternal damnation is self-chosen and just, and that universalism contradicts Scripture and dogma."],["Garrigou-Lagrange","Life Everlasting (1947); The Three Ages of the Interior Life","Classic treatment of hell's pain of loss and pain of sense, and of the fixity of the will after death."],["Christian B. Wagner","thomism.com: 'On the Damnability of Venial Sin'; 'The Unforgiveable Sin'; 'Few Catholics Will be Saved'","Engages scholastic questions on the punishment of sin and on final impenitence."]],
 errors:[["Hell is temporary (universalism).","Condemned; Mt 25:46 uses the same 'eternal' for punishment and life."],["God sends people to hell arbitrarily.","No one is damned without a free mortal sin persisted in until death (Trent; CCC 1037)."]],
 scripture:[["Mt 25:41, 46","everlasting fire; everlasting punishment and life everlasting"],["Mk 9:43–48","the fire that is not quenched"],["2 Thess 1:9","eternal destruction from the face of the Lord"],["Rev 20:10","tormented day and night for ever"]],
 tests:[["What is the pain of loss?","The loss of God, infinite because God is the infinite good."],["Why does the punishment last for ever, according to Thomas?","Because the stain of mortal sin remains, the will being fixed."]],
 read:["ST I-II q.87","Suppl. q.99","CCC 1033–1037"]},

{id:"last-few",tract:"last",level:2,note:"Number of the saved: not defined; Thomas's opinion: few",st:"ST I q.23 a.7 ad 3",
 q:"Will most people be saved or lost?",
 one:"The Church has not defined the number; Thomas, following Christ's words about the narrow gate, held that 'those who are saved are in the minority', while stressing that this shows God's mercy toward those saved; many modern theologians are more hopeful, and all agree the question should spur conversion and mission, not curiosity or despair.",
 plain:`<p>Jesus: 'Enter ye in at the narrow gate: for wide is the gate, and broad is the way that leadeth to destruction, and many there are who go in thereat. How narrow is the gate… and few there are that find it!' (Mt 7:13–14). When asked 'Lord, are they few that are saved?', he answered, 'Strive to enter by the narrow gate' (Lk 13:23–24): he turns a speculative question into a personal summons.</p>
<p>Thomas: 'Since their eternal happiness, consisting in the vision of God, exceeds the common state of nature, and especially in so far as this is deprived of grace through the corruption of original sin, those who are saved are in the minority. In this especially, however, appears the mercy of God, that He has chosen some for that salvation' (I q.23 a.7 ad 3). Many Fathers and saints (Augustine, Chrysostom, Leonard of Port Maurice) held similar views.</p>`,
 analogy:["The lifeboat drill","When a ship is sinking, the right response to 'how many will survive?' is to get into a lifeboat and help others in. Christ answers the question that way."],
 deeper:`<p><strong>Other views.</strong> Some modern theologians (and the hope expressed in some liturgical texts) emphasise God's universal saving will (1 Tim 2:4), the possibility of salvation for the invincibly ignorant (LG 16), and Revelation's 'great multitude, which no man could number' (Rev 7:9). Benedict XVI (Spe Salvi 45–47) suggested that most people are neither wholly pure nor wholly closed to God, and will be purified. The Church has not settled the question.</p>
<p><strong>Wagner's collection.</strong> His essay 'Few Catholics Will Be Saved: On the Fewness of the Saved in Scripture, Tradition, and Theology' gathers the traditional testimony.</p>`,
 authors:[["Christian B. Wagner","thomism.com: 'Few Catholics Will be Saved: On the Fewness of the Saved in Scripture, Tradition, and Theology'","Collects the scriptural, patristic and scholastic testimony for the traditional view."],["Garrigou-Lagrange","Predestination; Life Everlasting","Holds the traditional view, balanced by the doctrine of sufficient grace for all."]],
 errors:[["Practically everyone is saved, so conversion doesn't matter.","Contradicts Christ's urgency and the Church's mission."],["Few are saved, so I am probably damned.","Despair is a sin against hope; God wills my salvation and gives grace."]],
 tests:[["How did Jesus answer 'are they few that are saved?'","'Strive to enter by the narrow gate.'"]],
 read:["ST I q.23 a.7","Benedict XVI, Spe Salvi 44–48"]},

{id:"last-limbo",tract:"last",level:3,note:"Limbo: theological hypothesis, never defined; ITC (2007): hope for unbaptised infants",st:"De Malo q.5 aa.2–3",
 q:"What happens to babies who die without baptism? Is limbo Catholic teaching?",
 one:"Limbo was the common theological opinion for centuries: unbaptised infants, having original sin but no personal sin, lack the beatific vision but suffer no pain and enjoy natural happiness; it was never defined, and in 2007 the International Theological Commission gave reasons to hope they can be saved, while the Church entrusts them to God's mercy.",
 plain:`<p>Original sin deprives of the supernatural gift of grace, and baptism is the ordinary means of receiving it. Infants cannot desire baptism. So what of those who die unbaptised? Augustine thought they suffer the mildest punishment. Thomas softened this greatly: they have no personal sin, so no pain of sense; and since they never knew of the beatific vision as something due to them, they feel no grief at its lack: they enjoy a natural happiness, knowing and loving God naturally (De Malo q.5 aa.2–3; In II Sent. d.33 q.2 a.2).</p>
<p>This 'limbo of children' became the common view, defended by Pius VI against the Synod of Pistoia (Auctorem Fidei 26, 1794: rejecting the claim that limbo is a Pelagian fable), but never defined. The Catechism (1992) does not mention limbo; it says that 'the Church can only entrust them to the mercy of God… allow us to hope that there is a way of salvation for children who have died without Baptism' (CCC 1261). The ITC's The Hope of Salvation for Infants Who Die Without Being Baptised (2007) concluded there are 'serious theological and liturgical grounds for hope', while stating these are 'reasons for prayerful hope, rather than grounds for sure knowledge'.</p>`,
 analogy:["The child who never knew the palace","A child raised in a beautiful countryside, never told of a royal palace, lives happily; he is not tormented by missing what he never knew was meant for him. Thomas's limbo is like that. The ITC's hope is that the King himself came to fetch the child."],
 deeper:`<p><strong>Sufficient grace and limbo.</strong> Wagner's essay 'On Limbo and Sufficient Grace' explores a classic difficulty: if God wills all to be saved and gives sufficient grace to all, how does this apply to infants who never reach reason? Thomists answered that God's universal will is satisfied by instituting baptism and the order of secondary causes, though in particular cases its means are impeded.</p>
<p><strong>Pastoral note.</strong> For grieving parents (including after miscarriage), the Church's funeral rites for children who died before baptism and CCC 1261 allow real hope. Parents should still baptise infants promptly (c. 867).</p>`,
 authors:[["Christian B. Wagner","thomism.com: 'On Limbo and Sufficient Grace'","Examines how the Thomist doctrine of sufficient grace relates to infants who die unbaptised."],["Garrigou-Lagrange","Life Everlasting","Defends the classic limbo of Thomas as natural happiness without suffering."]],
 errors:[["Limbo was defined dogma.","It was never defined (ITC 2007)."],["Unbaptised infants certainly go to heaven, so baptism can wait.","The hope is not certainty; baptism should not be delayed (c. 867)."]],
 tests:[["What did Thomas think unbaptised infants experience?","No pain, no grief, a natural happiness without the beatific vision."],["What did the ITC (2007) conclude?","There are serious grounds for hope, but not certainty."]],
 read:["Thomas, De Malo q.5","CCC 1261","ITC, The Hope of Salvation for Infants (2007)"]},

{id:"last-purgatory",tract:"last",level:2,note:"Purgatory's existence and the help of the living: de fide (Lyons II, Florence, Trent Session 25); nature of its pains: not defined",st:"Suppl. q.71; Appendix II",
 q:"What is purgatory really like? Is it a place, and is there fire?",
 one:"Purgatory is the purification after death of those who die in grace but still need cleansing from venial sins and the temporal punishment due to forgiven sins; the Church defines its existence and that our prayers and Masses help the souls there, but not its location, duration or the nature of its 'fire'.",
 plain:`<p>Florence (1439) and Trent (Session 25, 1563) taught that 'there is a purgatory, and that the souls there detained are helped by the suffrages of the faithful, and especially by the acceptable sacrifice of the altar'. Trent warned preachers to avoid 'more difficult and subtle questions' and curiosity and superstition.</p>
<p>Why? A forgiven sin can leave a debt of temporal punishment and disordered attachments. Nothing unclean enters heaven (Rev 21:27). Purgatory is God's loving completion of the work of sanctification. Scripture: 'he himself shall be saved, yet so as by fire' (1 Cor 3:15); Judas Maccabeus's sacrifice 'that they might be loosed from sins' (2 Macc 12:46); Christ's word about sin not forgiven 'in the world to come' (Mt 12:32).</p>
<p>Thomas, following the theology of his day, thought the pain of sense in purgatory was by a real fire and that the least pain there exceeds the greatest of this life (In IV Sent. d.21; Suppl. Appendix). The souls also rejoice, being certain of salvation. The Greeks at Florence objected to 'fire', so the council defined only 'purifying pains'.</p>`,
 analogy:["The eyes adjusting to the sun","Someone coming out of a dark cave into blazing sunlight must wait while his eyes adjust: it hurts, but it is the pain of approaching light he longs for. Benedict XVI (Spe Salvi 47) suggests the encounter with Christ himself may be the purifying 'fire'."],
 deeper:`<p><strong>Indulgences.</strong> The Church, administering the treasury of Christ's and the saints' merits, can remit temporal punishment (indulgences), applicable to the dead by way of suffrage (CCC 1471–1479; Paul VI, Indulgentiarum Doctrina, 1967).</p>
<p><strong>Wagner and penal substitution.</strong> Wagner argues that the Catholic doctrine of satisfaction, in which Christ's satisfaction is applied while temporal punishment may remain to be satisfied by the forgiven, explains purgatory and penance; he contends that Protestant penal substitution without this distinction has trouble with them.</p>`,
 authors:[["Christian B. Wagner","thomism.com: 'How Penal Substitution Refutes Protestantism'; 'Penal Substitutionary Atonement: The Foundation of Catholicism'","Links satisfaction, temporal punishment, penance and purgatory."],["Garrigou-Lagrange","Life Everlasting (1947)","Treats purgatory as the purification of love, with both suffering and peace."]],
 errors:[["Purgatory is a second chance for the unrepentant.","Only those who die in grace are there."],["Purgatory denies Christ's sufficiency.","It applies Christ's merits; Trent, Session 6, can. 30."]],
 scripture:[["1 Cor 3:12–15","saved, yet so as by fire"],["2 Macc 12:38–46","prayer for the dead"],["Mt 12:32","nor in the world to come"],["Rev 21:27","nothing defiled shall enter"]],
 tests:[["What has the Church defined about purgatory?","Its existence and that suffrages, especially the Mass, help the souls."],["Did Florence define a material fire?","No; it spoke of purifying pains."]],
 read:["Trent, Session 25, Decree on Purgatory","CCC 1030–1032","<a href=\"#apologetics\">Apologetics: 1 Cor 3:15</a>"]},

{id:"last-resurrection",tract:"last",level:2,note:"Resurrection of the same body: de fide (Lateran IV); qualities of the glorified body: common teaching",st:"Suppl. qq.79–85",
 q:"What will our risen bodies be like?",
 one:"Each person will rise in his own body, restored to the perfection of mature youth; the bodies of the blessed will share the soul's glory through four gifts (impassibility, subtlety, agility and clarity), modelled on the risen Christ.",
 plain:`<p>Lateran IV (1215): all 'will rise with their own bodies, which they now bear'. Paul: 'It is sown a natural body, it shall rise a spiritual body' (1 Cor 15:44), 'spiritual' meaning wholly governed by the spirit, not made of spirit.</p>
<p>Thomas: 'Man will rise again without any defect of human nature'; children and the old will be 'brought by the resurrection to the state of its ultimate perfection which is in the youthful age' (Suppl. q.81 a.1); tradition puts it at about Christ's age, 33. The four gifts (dotes) of the glorified body, drawn from 1 Cor 15:42–44:</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table class="t"><thead><tr><th>Gift</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Seen in the risen Christ</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Impassibility</td><td>Cannot suffer or die</td><td>'Death shall no more have dominion over him' (Rom 6:9)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Subtlety</td><td>Perfectly subject to the soul, so 'spiritual'</td><td>Enters where the doors were shut (Jn 20:19)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Agility</td><td>Moves at the soul's command without effort</td><td>Appears and vanishes (Lk 24:31)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Clarity</td><td>Radiance overflowing from the soul's glory</td><td>The Transfiguration (Mt 17:2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>`,
 analogy:["The seed and the plant","Paul's own image (1 Cor 15:36–38): what is sown is not the body that shall be, but bare grain; God gives it a body as he wills. The plant is the same living thing as the seed, but transformed."],
 deeper:`<p><strong>Identity.</strong> How can the body be 'the same' when its matter changes during life and is dispersed after death? Thomas answers that identity comes from the same soul as form; God will restore matter sufficient for the integrity of the body (Suppl. q.79; q.80). Feser's Immortal Souls discusses this answer in modern terms.</p>
<p><strong>The damned</strong> also rise, but without the gifts of glory; their bodies are incorruptible but passible (Suppl. q.86).</p>`,
 authors:[["Edward Feser","Immortal Souls, final chapters","Explains bodily identity at the resurrection through the soul as form."],["Garrigou-Lagrange","Life Everlasting","Describes the four gifts and their source in the beatific vision."]],
 errors:[["The resurrection is only spiritual or symbolic.","Lateran IV: our own bodies."],["We will be angels.","We will be like angels in not marrying (Mt 22:30), but remain embodied humans."]],
 scripture:[["1 Cor 15:35–55","sown corruptible, raised incorruptible"],["Phil 3:21","reform the body of our lowness, made like to the body of his glory"],["Jn 5:28–29","all that are in the graves shall hear his voice"],["Dan 12:2","many that sleep shall awake"]],
 tests:[["Name the four gifts of the glorified body.","Impassibility, subtlety, agility, clarity."],["At what 'age' will we rise, for Thomas?","The perfection of youth."]],
 read:["Suppl. qq.79–86","CCC 988–1004"]}
);

// Disputed questions: hard texts of Scripture
DQ.push(
{id:"scr-gen6",tract:"scripture",level:2,note:"Interpretation free within the analogy of faith; angels cannot generate: theological conclusion",st:"ST I q.51 a.3 ad 6",
 q:"Genesis 6:1–4: Who were the 'sons of God' and the giants (Nephilim)?",
 one:"Thomas, with Augustine, reads the 'sons of God' as the godly descendants of Seth and the 'daughters of men' as the line of Cain; the older Jewish view that they were angels conflicts with the truth that spirits cannot generate bodies; the 'giants' were mighty, violent men.",
 plain:`<p>The text: 'the sons of God seeing the daughters of men, that they were fair, took to themselves wives of all which they chose… Now giants were upon the earth in those days' (Gen 6:2, 4). Some ancient Jewish writings (1 Enoch, Jubilees) and early Christian writers (Justin, Tertullian) took 'sons of God' to be angels who mated with women and fathered giants. Jude 6 and 2 Peter 2:4 speak of angels who sinned and were imprisoned.</p>
<p>Thomas reports Augustine: some claim incubi have intercourse with women, 'But God's holy angels could not fall in such fashion before the deluge. Hence by the sons of God are to be understood the sons of Seth, who were good; while by the daughters of men the Scripture designates those who sprang from the race of Cain' (I q.51 a.3 ad 6). The reason is metaphysical: generation is a vital act of a living body; an angel's assumed body does not live (see 'How can an angel appear in a body?'). So angels cannot father children.</p>
<p>The point of the passage in its context: the mingling of the faithful line with the violent line led to universal corruption, so that 'the wickedness of men was great on the earth' (6:5), preparing the Flood.</p>`,
 analogy:["Two family lines","Genesis 4–5 traces two genealogies: Cain's line, ending in Lamech's boast of murder, and Seth's line, in which 'men began to call upon the name of the Lord' (4:26). Genesis 6 tells of their merging. Read this way, the 'sons of God' are not a sudden intrusion of mythology but the climax of a two-family story."],
 deeper:`<p><strong>Other readings.</strong> Many modern scholars hold the 'angel' reading as the original sense and see a polemic against pagan myths of divine kings. A third reading takes 'sons of God' as kings or nobles (ancient Near Eastern royal titles). The Catholic exegete may weigh these, but the Thomist conclusion that spirits cannot generate stands on its own ground. Jude's language about fallen angels does not require the Genesis reading.</p>
<p><strong>Nephilim.</strong> The Hebrew may mean 'fallen ones'; the Septuagint and Vulgate render 'giants'. Numbers 13:33 uses the word for the tall inhabitants of Canaan.</p>`,
 authors:[["Christian B. Wagner","christianbwagner.com: 'Can Demons Work True Miracles ad mentem Divi Thomae?'; thomism.com: 'On the Number of Angels'","The principles governing what angels and demons can do with bodies apply directly to the Genesis 6 question."]],
 tests:[["Why can't angels father children, for Thomas?","Generation is a function of life, and the bodies angels assume are not alive."]],
 read:["Gen 4–6","ST I q.51 a.3 ad 6","Augustine, City of God XV.23","<a href=\"#dq-angels-bodies\">Angels and bodies</a>"]},

{id:"scr-gen1-26",tract:"scripture",level:1,note:"Common patristic interpretation",st:"ST I q.93 a.5",
 q:"Genesis 1:26: Why does God say 'Let us make man'?",
 one:"The Fathers and Thomas read the plural 'us… our image' as a hidden hint of the Trinity, the one God who is three persons, while the singular 'he created' and 'his image' (1:27) show his oneness; the literal sense may also be a plural of deliberation or of the heavenly court, but the Trinitarian reading is the Church's fuller sense.",
 plain:`<p>Thomas cites the verse to show that man is made to the image of the whole Trinity, not of one person only: 'if man were made only to the image of the Son, the Father would not have said, \"Let Us make man to Our own image and likeness\"; but \"to Thy image\"' (I q.93 a.5). The Fathers (Basil, Augustine) saw the plurality of persons and the unity of essence in the shift from 'let us make… our image' to 'God created man to his own image' (1:27).</p>
<p>Jewish interpreters proposed a plural of majesty, God addressing the angels, or deliberating with himself. Catholic exegesis accepts that the human author need not have grasped the Trinity, while the fuller sense (sensus plenior) intended by God is revealed in the New Testament.</p>`,
 analogy:["The first whisper","A melody can be sounded softly in the opening bars of a symphony and only later be heard in full. Genesis 1:26 is the first whisper of a theme the New Testament plays fortissimo: 'Father, Son and Holy Spirit'."],
 tests:[["What does Thomas conclude from 'Let us make'?","Man is made to the image of the whole Trinity."]],
 read:["ST I q.93 a.5","CCC 237 (the Trinity is a mystery hidden in the OT)"]},

{id:"scr-ex33",tract:"scripture",level:2,note:"No one sees God's essence in this life except possibly by miraculous rapture: common teaching (I q.12 a.11; II-II q.175)",st:"ST I q.12 a.11",
 q:"Exodus 33: Did Moses see God 'face to face', or can no man see God and live?",
 one:"'Face to face' (Ex 33:11) describes Moses' unusual intimacy and clear speech with God, not a vision of God's essence, which 'no man shall see… and live' (33:20) in this mortal state; Thomas allows that Moses and Paul may have seen God's essence by a passing miraculous rapture.",
 plain:`<p>Exodus 33:11: 'the Lord spoke to Moses face to face, as a man is wont to speak to his friend'. Nine verses later God says: 'Thou canst not see my face: for man shall not see me and live' (33:20), and lets Moses see only his 'back parts' as his glory passes.</p>
<p>Thomas: 'God cannot be seen in His essence by a mere human being, except he be separated from this mortal life' (I q.12 a.11), because in this life the soul knows through the body and sense images. 'Face to face' in 33:11 means familiar, direct communication. Yet he thinks Moses and Paul ('caught up to the third heaven', 2 Cor 12:2) may have been granted a transient vision of God's essence in a miraculous rapture that suspended their bodily senses (II-II q.175 aa.3–4).</p>`,
 analogy:["The voice through the door","Two friends talk through a closed door every day: real, direct, intimate speech; but they do not see each other's faces. Moses' conversation with God was like that, and in the mountain cleft he was given a glimpse of the passing glory."],
 deeper:`<p><strong>The 'back parts'.</strong> Gregory of Nyssa (Life of Moses) reads seeing God's back as following him: the soul never comprehends God but endlessly follows. Thomas reads it as knowing God through his effects. Both point toward the beatific vision promised in Christ: 'we shall see him as he is' (1 Jn 3:2).</p>
<p><strong>Jesus as the visible Image.</strong> 'He that seeth me seeth the Father also' (Jn 14:9): in Christ's humanity the invisible God is seen, though his divine essence is not seen by bodily eyes.</p>`,
 tests:[["What does 'face to face' in Ex 33:11 mean?","Familiar, clear communication, not the vision of God's essence."]],
 read:["ST I q.12 aa.11–13; II-II q.175","<a href=\"#dq-god-vision\">The beatific vision</a>"]},

{id:"scr-heb6",tract:"scripture",level:2,note:"Sins after baptism can be forgiven by penance: de fide (Trent Session 14)",st:"ST III q.84 a.10 ad 1",
 q:"Hebrews 6:4–6: Is it 'impossible' for those who fall away to repent?",
 one:"Thomas, following the Fathers, reads the passage as teaching that baptism cannot be repeated: those once 'illuminated' cannot be renewed again by a second baptism; it does not deny that sinners can repent and be forgiven through penance, which the Church has always offered.",
 plain:`<p>The text: 'it is impossible for those who were once illuminated… and are fallen away, to be renewed again to penance, crucifying again to themselves the Son of God'. The Novatians (3rd century) used it to deny forgiveness to the lapsed. The Church condemned them.</p>
<p>Thomas: some Jews thought baptism could be repeated like ritual washings, so 'the Apostle wrote to the Hebrews that \"it is impossible for those who were once illuminated,\" viz. through Baptism, \"to be renewed again to penance,\" viz. through Baptism, which is \"the laver of regeneration\"' (III q.84 a.10 ad 1). Baptism configures us to Christ's death once; to repeat it would be to crucify him again. But the sacrament of penance can be repeated as often as we fall (III q.84 a.10).</p>`,
 analogy:["Birth and healing","You are born only once, but you can be healed many times. Baptism is spiritual birth (not repeatable); penance is healing (repeatable)."],
 deeper:`<p><strong>Other readings.</strong> Some interpreters read 'impossible' as describing apostates who persist in contempt, for whom repentance is impossible while they continue to reject Christ (cf. the sin against the Holy Spirit). Both readings deny the Novatian conclusion.</p>`,
 errors:[["Some sins are too great to be forgiven if repented.","The Church can forgive every sin (CCC 982); Trent, Session 14."]],
 tests:[["How does Thomas read 'impossible… to be renewed again'?","As excluding a second baptism, not penance."]],
 read:["ST III q.84 a.10","CCC 979–983"]},

{id:"scr-mt12",tract:"scripture",level:2,note:"Common teaching (II-II q.14; CCC 1864)",st:"ST II-II q.14 aa.1–3",
 q:"Matthew 12:31–32: What is the 'unforgivable sin' against the Holy Spirit?",
 one:"It is not a particular word that God refuses to forgive; it is sin committed out of deliberate malice that rejects the very means of forgiveness (final impenitence, despair, presumption, resisting known truth, envy of another's grace, obstinacy), which is 'unforgivable' because it shuts out the remedy, not because God's mercy is limited.",
 plain:`<p>Thomas gives three interpretations from the Fathers: (1) blasphemy against the Holy Spirit as speaking evil of the Spirit's works (the Pharisees calling Christ's exorcisms Satan's work); (2) final impenitence, persisting in mortal sin until death (Augustine); (3) sin from 'certain malice', against the good gifts by which the Spirit draws us back from sin (the scholastic view). Under the third he lists six species: despair, presumption, resisting the known truth, envy of a brother's spiritual good, impenitence, obstinacy (II-II q.14 a.2).</p>
<p>Why 'unforgivable'? If it means final impenitence, 'it is said to be unpardonable, since in no way is it pardoned' (II-II q.14 a.3). In the other senses it is unpardonable 'not as though it is nowise forgiven, but because, considered in itself, it deserves not to be pardoned', since it rejects the remedies. God can still heal it, 'as when a disease is said to be incurable' and yet God sometimes cures it (same article).</p>
<p>The Catechism: 'There are no limits to the mercy of God, but anyone who deliberately refuses to accept his mercy by repenting, rejects the forgiveness of his sins and the salvation offered by the Holy Spirit' (CCC 1864).</p>`,
 analogy:["Refusing the medicine","A doctor can cure any illness, but not a patient who pours every medicine down the drain. The illness is 'incurable' not because of the doctor's limits but because of the refusal."],
 deeper:`<p><strong>For anxious consciences.</strong> People with scrupulosity or intrusive thoughts often fear they have committed this sin. If you are worried you have, that worry is itself a sign you have not: the sin is the refusal of repentance, and you want to repent. (See the psychology track on scrupulosity.)</p>`,
 authors:[["Christian B. Wagner","thomism.com: 'The Unforgiveable Sin'","Explains Thomas's three interpretations and six species, and reassures the scrupulous."]],
 tests:[["Name the six species of sin against the Holy Spirit.","Despair, presumption, resisting known truth, envy of another's grace, impenitence, obstinacy."],["Why is it called unforgivable?","Because it rejects the means of forgiveness, not because God's mercy is limited."]],
 read:["ST II-II q.14","CCC 1864"]},

{id:"scr-1cor15",tract:"scripture",level:2,note:"Interpretation open; the practice described is not Catholic teaching",st:"Suppl. q.71 a.9 ad 4",
 q:"1 Corinthians 15:29: What is 'baptism for the dead'?",
 one:"Paul may refer to a practice some engaged in (proxy baptism for the dead) without approving it, simply arguing that it makes no sense if the dead do not rise; or 'for the dead' may mean 'for sins' or 'in view of the resurrection'; Thomas gives both readings, and the Church has never practised proxy baptism.",
 plain:`<p>'Otherwise what shall they do that are baptized for the dead, if the dead rise not again at all? why are they then baptized for them?' (1 Cor 15:29).</p>
<p>Thomas offers two expositions from the Gloss: either 'for the dead' means 'for sins' (dead works), which would be pointless if Christ is not risen, since his resurrection is the cause of our spiritual resurrection; or 'there have been some misguided persons who were baptized for those who had departed this life without baptism, thinking that this would profit them: and according to this explanation the Apostle is speaking… merely according to the opinion of certain persons' (Suppl. q.71 a.9 ad 4), an argument ad hominem: even your own practice assumes resurrection.</p>
<p>The Latter-day Saints practise proxy baptism from this text. The Catholic Church does not: baptism is for the living (c. 864), and the Church helps the dead by prayer and the Mass (2 Macc 12:46).</p>`,
 analogy:["Arguing from your opponent's habits","If someone who says 'there is no tomorrow' still sets an alarm, you can say: 'Why set an alarm, if there is no tomorrow?' You are not endorsing the alarm; you are pointing out what it implies."],
 tests:[["Does the Church practise baptism for the dead?","No; Paul's argument is ad hominem or 'for the dead' means something else."]],
 read:["Suppl. q.71 a.9","1 Cor 15:12–34"]},

{id:"scr-mt19",tract:"scripture",level:2,note:"Indissolubility of consummated sacramental marriage: de fide (Trent Session 24, can. 7)",st:"Trent Session 24; CCC 1614–1615, 1640",
 q:"Matthew 19:9: Does 'except it be for fornication' allow divorce and remarriage?",
 one:"The Catholic Church reads the exception as permitting separation (not remarriage) for adultery, or as referring to unions that were never valid marriages ('porneia', unlawful unions); Mark, Luke and Paul state the prohibition without exception, and Trent defined that adultery does not dissolve a valid marriage.",
 plain:`<p>'Whosoever shall put away his wife, except it be for fornication, and shall marry another, committeth adultery' (Mt 19:9; cf. 5:32). Mark 10:11–12, Luke 16:18 and 1 Corinthians 7:10–11 give the rule with no exception: 'Whosoever shall put away his wife and marry another, committeth adultery against her.'</p>
<p>Catholic readings of the exception:</p>
<ol><li><strong>Separation only.</strong> Jerome and Augustine: adultery justifies separating, but neither may remarry while the other lives (1 Cor 7:11: 'let her remain unmarried, or be reconciled').</li>
<li><strong>Invalid unions.</strong> Many modern exegetes: porneia means unlawful unions within forbidden degrees (cf. Acts 15:20, 29; Lev 18), which were never true marriages and should be ended.</li></ol>
<p>Trent anathematised anyone who says the Church errs in teaching that 'the bond of matrimony cannot be dissolved on account of the adultery of one of the married parties' (Session 24, can. 7), wording it so as not to condemn the Eastern churches' different practice.</p>`,
 analogy:["The vow that outlasts betrayal","When a soldier deserts, the oath he swore is not dissolved; he is a deserter, not a former soldier. The marriage bond, made before God, likewise outlasts betrayal; the betrayed spouse may separate for protection, but the bond remains."],
 deeper:`<p><strong>Annulments.</strong> A declaration of nullity is not 'Catholic divorce': it is a judgement that a valid marriage never existed (e.g. lack of consent, impediment). Pauline and Petrine privileges concern non-sacramental marriages (c. 1143–1150).</p>`,
 tests:[["What did Trent define about adultery and the marriage bond?","That the Church does not err in teaching adultery does not dissolve it."]],
 read:["Mt 19:3–12; Mk 10:2–12","Trent, Session 24","CCC 1614–1617, 1640, 1650"]},

{id:"scr-mt16-28",tract:"scripture",level:2,note:"Common patristic interpretation",st:"ST III q.45",
 q:"Matthew 16:28: Did Jesus wrongly predict the end of the world in his disciples' lifetime?",
 one:"No: 'some standing here shall not taste death till they see the Son of man coming in his kingdom' is fulfilled, according to the Fathers, in the Transfiguration six days later (17:1), and more fully in the Resurrection, Pentecost and the fall of Jerusalem, the first manifestations of his kingdom.",
 plain:`<p>All three Synoptic Gospels place this saying immediately before the Transfiguration (Mt 16:28–17:9; Mk 9:1–8; Lk 9:27–36). The Fathers (Chrysostom, Jerome, Gregory) read the Transfiguration as the promised preview: Peter, James and John 'saw' the Son of Man in his glory. Peter himself connects them: 'we were eyewitnesses of his greatness… in the holy mount' (2 Pet 1:16–18).</p>
<p>Others add the Resurrection and Pentecost (the kingdom coming 'with power', Mk 9:1) and the destruction of Jerusalem in AD 70 as a 'coming' in judgement (Mt 24:34). Jesus elsewhere says no one knows the day of the final coming (Mt 24:36).</p>`,
 analogy:["The trailer and the film","A film's trailer shows real scenes from the film before its release. The Transfiguration was a real 'scene' of the coming glory shown in advance."],
 tests:[["What follows Mt 16:28 immediately in all three Synoptics?","The Transfiguration."]],
 read:["Mt 16:28–17:9","ST III q.45 (the Transfiguration)","<a href=\"#dq-christ-knowledge\">Christ's knowledge</a>"]},

{id:"scr-gen3-15",tract:"scripture",level:2,note:"Protoevangelium: Church teaching (CCC 410–411); Marian reading: traditional",st:"CCC 410–411; Ineffabilis Deus",
 q:"Genesis 3:15: Does 'she shall crush thy head' refer to Mary?",
 one:"The Hebrew most likely says 'he' or 'it' (the woman's offspring) shall crush the serpent's head, which points to Christ; the Vulgate's 'she' (ipsa) gave rise to a strong Marian reading; the Church reads the verse as the first Gospel, in which the Woman and her offspring together defeat the serpent: Mary as the New Eve, inseparable from her Son.",
 plain:`<p>The Douay-Rheims follows the Vulgate: 'I will put enmities between thee and the woman, and thy seed and her seed: she shall crush thy head' (Gen 3:15). The Hebrew pronoun is masculine (hu'), referring to the 'seed'; the Greek has 'he' (autos); some Latin manuscripts had ipse/ipsum, but the Vulgate tradition settled on ipsa.</p>
<p>The Church's reading combines both: the verse is the 'Protoevangelium, the first announcement of the Messiah and Redeemer, of a battle between the serpent and the Woman, and of the final victory of a descendant of hers' (CCC 410). Many Fathers see in it 'the New Eve', Mary (CCC 411). Pius IX in Ineffabilis Deus (1854) cites the Fathers' reading of the enmity between the Woman and the serpent as foreshadowing the Immaculate Conception: total enmity means no moment under the serpent's power.</p>`,
 analogy:["The mother and the son at the gate","A mother stands at the gate with her son as he fights off an invader. Whether you say 'she drove him off' or 'her son drove him off', both are true in different ways, though the victory's power is the son's. Catholic art shows Mary's foot on the serpent because her victory is entirely by her Son."],
 deeper:`<p><strong>Revelation 12.</strong> The 'woman clothed with the sun' at war with 'that old serpent' (Rev 12:9) and her child forms an inclusio with Genesis 3:15: the Woman and her offspring triumph.</p>`,
 tests:[["What pronoun does the Hebrew use?","Masculine, referring to the woman's seed."],["Why does Catholic art show Mary crushing the serpent?","Because she shares, entirely through her Son, in his victory as the New Eve."]],
 read:["CCC 410–411","Pius IX, Ineffabilis Deus","<a href=\"#dq-mary-ic\">The Immaculate Conception</a>"]},

{id:"scr-isa45",tract:"scripture",level:2,note:"Theological conclusion (I q.49 a.2; I-II q.79 a.1)",st:"ST I q.49 a.2; I-II q.79 a.1",
 q:"Isaiah 45:7: Does God 'create evil'?",
 one:"'I make peace, and create evil' refers to the evil of penalty and calamity (war, disaster as judgement), which God causes as a just punishment or permits for a good end; it does not mean God causes the evil of sin.",
 plain:`<p>'I form the light, and create darkness, I make peace, and create evil: I the Lord that do all these things' (Isa 45:7). The contrast is with 'peace' (shalom): the evil meant is calamity, disaster, war, not moral wickedness. In context God speaks to Cyrus and to Israel in exile: he is Lord of history, of prosperity and adversity alike, against Persian dualism of a good god and an evil god.</p>
<p>Thomas cites this verse in an objection arguing God causes sin (I-II q.79 a.1 obj.), and answers with the distinction he draws in the Prima Pars: God is the author of the evil which is penalty, but not of the evil which is fault (I q.49 a.2). Amos 3:6 ('Shall there be evil in a city, which the Lord hath not done?') is read the same way.</p>`,
 analogy:["The judge and the crime","A judge 'creates' a penalty for a crime; he does not create the crime. God's 'creating evil' is like the judge's sentence, and like the physician's painful treatment, never like the criminal's act."],
 tests:[["Which kind of evil does Isaiah 45:7 mean?","The evil of penalty or calamity, not of sin."]],
 read:["ST I q.49 a.2","<a href=\"#dq-god-evil\">Does God will evil?</a>"]},

{id:"scr-prov8",tract:"scripture",level:3,note:"Patristic consensus against Arianism",st:"Athanasius, Orations against the Arians II; ST I q.27 a.2",
 q:"Proverbs 8:22: Was Wisdom (the Son) 'created'?",
 one:"The Arians used 'the Lord created (possessed) me in the beginning of his ways' to argue the Son is a creature; Athanasius and the Fathers answered that the verse refers either to the eternal generation (the Vulgate's 'possessed me') or to the Word's created human nature in the Incarnation, 'the beginning of his ways' for our salvation.",
 plain:`<p>The Greek Septuagint reads 'The Lord created (ektisen) me as the beginning of his ways'; the Vulgate and Douay-Rheims read 'The Lord possessed me in the beginning of his ways'. Arius argued: Wisdom = the Son; Wisdom was created; so the Son is a creature.</p>
<p>Athanasius (Orations against the Arians II) answered: (1) the verse speaks of the Incarnation: Wisdom 'created' as man, as the beginning of God's ways of salvation; the next verses (8:24–25: 'before the hills I was brought forth') speak of eternal generation. (2) Wisdom is God's own wisdom, and God was never without his wisdom, so it cannot be a creature. Nicaea's 'begotten, not made' settles the doctrine.</p>`,
 analogy:["The two birthdays","Christ has, so to speak, two 'births': eternal from the Father (no beginning) and temporal from Mary. Proverbs 8 is read as speaking of both: the eternal 'brought forth' and the temporal 'created' as man."],
 deeper:`<p><strong>Thomas</strong> uses the next verse, 'before the hills, I was brought forth' (Prov 8:24), to show that the Word's procession is a true generation (I q.27 a.2), and answers Arian readings of subordination texts in I q.42 (the equality of the persons). Hebrew qanani can mean 'acquired', 'possessed' or 'begot' (cf. Gen 4:1, Eve: 'I have gotten a man').</p>`,
 tests:[["How did Athanasius read Prov 8:22?","Of the Incarnation (created as man), with 8:24–25 speaking of the eternal generation."]],
 read:["Athanasius, Orations against the Arians II","Nicene Creed","<a href=\"#apologetics\">Apologetics: John 1:1</a>"]}
);

// Disputed questions: controversies with the Orthodox and Protestants
DQ.push(
{id:"controversy-energies",tract:"controversy",level:3,note:"Divine simplicity: de fide (Lateran IV; Vatican I). Blessed see the divine essence: de fide (Benedictus Deus, 1336). Whether Palamism as such contradicts these: disputed among theologians; not formally condemned",st:"ST I q.3 a.7; I q.12 a.1; I-II q.110 a.2",
 q:"Essence and energies: Does the Eastern Orthodox (Palamite) distinction contradict Catholic teaching on God's simplicity, grace and the beatific vision?",
 one:"St Gregory Palamas (d. 1359) taught that God's essence is utterly unknowable and unparticipable, while his uncreated 'energies' (grace, light, glory) are really God and are what the saints share; Thomists answer that God is absolutely simple, so there is no real distinction in him between essence and operations, that grace in us is a created gift uniting us to the uncreated God, and that the blessed see the divine essence itself; whether a charitable reading of Palamas can be reconciled with this is a live dispute.",
 plain:`<p><strong>The Palamite view.</strong> In the 14th-century Hesychast controversy, monks claimed that through prayer they saw the uncreated light of Tabor. Barlaam of Calabria objected that any light seen must be created. Palamas answered: God's <em>essence</em> (ousia) is beyond all knowledge and participation; but God is truly present and shared in his <em>energies</em> (energeiai), which are uncreated, distinct from the essence, yet not a 'part' of God. Councils in Constantinople (1341, 1347, 1351) endorsed him; he is a saint of the Orthodox Church.</p>
<p><strong>The Thomist concerns.</strong></p>
<ol><li><strong>Simplicity.</strong> 'The absolute simplicity of God may be shown in many ways' (I q.3 a.7): no composition of essence and existence, substance and accident, or anything else. If the energies are really distinct from the essence and both are uncreated God, God seems to be composed. Lateran IV (1215) calls God 'one absolutely simple essence, substance or nature'.</li>
<li><strong>Grace.</strong> For Thomas, grace in the soul is a created quality: 'a habitual gift is infused by God into the soul' (I-II q.110 a.2), by which the soul shares in the divine nature (2 Pet 1:4). It is created not because it is less than God, but because it is God's work in us; through it the soul possesses the uncreated God himself, the indwelling Trinity (I q.43 a.3).</li>
<li><strong>The beatific vision.</strong> 'God, Who is pure act without any admixture of potentiality, is in Himself supremely knowable' (I q.12 a.1). Pope Benedict XII defined (Benedictus Deus, 1336) that the blessed 'see the divine essence with an intuitive vision, and even face to face'. If only the energies, never the essence, can be known, the Palamite seems to deny this.</li></ol>`,
 analogy:["The sun and its rays","Palamites often say: we touch the sun's rays, not the sun's core. The Thomist replies that in God there is no core-and-rays, because God's 'shining' is identical with what he is; what differs is the effects he produces in us. The sunburn (created grace) is in us; the sun itself (God) is what we are united to, and in heaven, seen."],
 deeper:`<p><strong>The Thomist alternative: a distinction of reason.</strong> We name God's wisdom, power and goodness by many concepts because we know him through many effects; the concepts differ, the reality is one (I q.13 a.4). The distinction among attributes is a 'distinction of reason with a foundation in reality' (virtual distinction), not a real one.</p>
<p><strong>Created and uncreated grace.</strong> Thomists distinguish <em>uncreated grace</em> (God himself, the indwelling Holy Spirit) from <em>created grace</em> (the habitual gift in us that makes us fit for that indwelling). The Orthodox criticism that 'created grace' makes salvation a mere thing misses this: the created gift is the means; union with the uncreated God is the end.</p>
<p><strong>The vision and comprehension.</strong> Thomas agrees with the East that no creature comprehends God (I q.12 a.7): the blessed see the whole essence, but not wholly, not as perfectly as it is knowable. Many Catholic and Orthodox theologians think much of the dispute lies here.</p>
<p><strong>Where scholars stand.</strong> Older Catholic scholarship (Martin Jugie, 1930s) judged Palamism heretical. Since the mid-20th century some Catholic and Orthodox theologians (e.g. A. N. Williams, <em>The Ground of Union</em>, 1999) have argued that Aquinas and Palamas are closer than they look. Some Eastern Catholic Churches commemorate St Gregory Palamas. The Catholic Church has not issued a formal judgement on Palamas's theology, but simplicity and the vision of the essence are binding.</p>`,
 schools:`<p><strong>Strict Thomists (Wagner, Feser, Garrigou-Lagrange):</strong> a real distinction between essence and uncreated energies is incompatible with simplicity as defined. <strong>Reconcilers:</strong> Palamas's 'distinction' is not a real distinction between things but between God as he is in himself and as he gives himself, close to the Scotist formal distinction or the Thomist virtual distinction.</p>`,
 authors:[["Christian B. Wagner","thomism.com: 'Does Created Grace Prove Eastern Orthodoxy?'; 'The Eastern Orthodox \"Forgeries\" Lie'; debates with Jay Dyer","Argues that Palamism breaks divine simplicity, that the Orthodox charge against 'created grace' misreads Thomas, and rebuts the claim that the papacy rests on forged documents."],["Edward Feser","Five Proofs of the Existence of God (2017), ch. 6–7; blog posts on simplicity","Defends simplicity as following from God being pure act and subsistent existence; answers objections that simplicity makes God's attributes meaningless."],["R. Garrigou-Lagrange","God: His Existence and His Nature (1914; Eng. 1934–36)","Classic Thomist treatment of the divine attributes and their virtual distinction."]],
 errors:[["The blessed never see God's essence, only his glory.","Contradicts Benedictus Deus (1336)."],["Created grace means we are united only to a created thing.","Created grace is the disposition for union with the uncreated, indwelling Trinity."]],
 scripture:[["2 Pet 1:4","'partakers of the divine nature': both sides cite it."],["1 Jn 3:2","'we shall see him as he is'."],["1 Tim 6:16","'light inaccessible, whom no man hath seen, nor can see': read by Thomas of comprehension and of this life."]],
 tests:[["What did Benedictus Deus define?","The blessed see the divine essence intuitively, face to face."],["What is the difference between created and uncreated grace?","Uncreated grace is God himself dwelling in us; created grace is the habitual gift that makes the soul fit for that indwelling."]],
 read:["ST I q.3; q.12; q.13 a.4; I-II q.110","A. N. Williams, The Ground of Union (OUP 1999)","<a href=\"#dq-god-simple\">Divine simplicity</a>","<a href=\"#dq-god-vision\">The beatific vision</a>","<a href=\"#dq-grace-what\">What is grace?</a>"]},

{id:"controversy-imputation",tract:"controversy",level:2,note:"Justification by infused grace: de fide (Trent, Session 6, cc. 7, 16; can. 11)",st:"ST I-II q.113 a.2; Trent Session 6",
 q:"Imputed or infused righteousness: Are we justified by Christ's righteousness credited to us, or by grace that makes us righteous?",
 one:"Luther and Calvin taught that justification is a legal declaration: God imputes Christ's righteousness to the believer, who remains in himself a sinner (simul iustus et peccator); Trent taught that justification is the forgiveness of sins together with the inner renewal of the person by infused grace and charity, so that we are truly made righteous, not merely counted so; the 1999 Joint Declaration narrowed but did not close the gap.",
 plain:`<p><strong>The Reformation view.</strong> Justification is forensic (from 'forum', a law court): God declares the sinner righteous on account of Christ, received by faith alone. Sanctification (inner growth in holiness) follows but is not part of justification. The believer is 'at once righteous and a sinner'.</p>
<p><strong>The Catholic view.</strong> God's declaration is effective: what he declares, he makes. Thomas: by sin the offender loses God's love in its effect, which is grace; 'Hence we could not conceive the remission of guilt, without the infusion of grace' (I-II q.113 a.2). Trent: justification 'is not remission of sins merely, but also the sanctification and renewal of the inward man', and the formal cause is 'the justice of God, not that by which He Himself is just, but that by which He maketh us just' (Session 6, ch. 7). Canon 11 condemns the view that we are justified 'by the sole imputation of the justice of Christ… to the exclusion of the grace and the charity which is poured forth in their hearts by the Holy Ghost'.</p>`,
 analogy:["A pardon and a cure","Imagine a man dying of a disease he caught by breaking the law. The Reformers' picture: the judge pardons him and reckons him healthy while the doctor gradually treats him. The Catholic picture: the King's pardon is itself the medicine; it forgives and heals in one act, and the healing continues."],
 deeper:`<p><strong>Common ground.</strong> Both sides teach that justification is a free gift, unmerited, received by faith, on account of Christ alone (Trent Session 6, ch. 8: 'none of those things which precede justification, whether faith or works, merit the grace itself'). The <em>Joint Declaration on the Doctrine of Justification</em> (Catholic Church and Lutheran World Federation, 1999; later joined by Methodists, Anglicans and Reformed) states a 'consensus in basic truths' and that the mutual condemnations of the 16th century do not apply to the teaching as set out there. The Vatican's official Response (1998) noted remaining differences, especially on simul iustus et peccator and on the place of concupiscence.</p>
<p><strong>James 2:24.</strong> 'by works a man is justified; and not by faith only.' Catholics read Paul's 'works of the law' (Rom 3:28) as works done without grace (or the Mosaic ceremonial law), and James's 'works' as faith working through charity (Gal 5:6).</p>`,
 authors:[["Christian B. Wagner","thomism.com: series on Protestant theology, Lutheran and Reformed justification","Explains the forensic model fairly, then argues from Trent and Thomas that grace must be inherent."],["Garrigou-Lagrange","Grace: Commentary on the Summa (1952)","Thomist treatment of I-II qq.109–114."]],
 errors:[["Catholics believe we earn our justification by works.","Trent teaches the first grace of justification cannot be merited (Session 6, ch. 8)."]],
 scripture:[["Rom 5:5","'the charity of God is poured forth in our hearts, by the Holy Ghost': inner renewal."],["2 Cor 5:17","'if then any be in Christ a new creature'."],["Rom 4:3–5","Abraham's faith 'reputed to him unto justice': the Reformers' key text."]],
 tests:[["What is the formal cause of justification according to Trent?","The justice of God by which he makes us just."],["What did the 1999 Joint Declaration claim?","A consensus in basic truths, and that the 16th-century condemnations do not apply to the teaching as presented."]],
 read:["ST I-II q.113","Trent, Session 6","Joint Declaration (1999)","<a href=\"#dq-grace-justification\">Justification of the ungodly</a>"]},

{id:"controversy-sola-scriptura",tract:"controversy",level:2,note:"Scripture and Tradition as one deposit, with the Magisterium as interpreter: de fide (Trent Session 4; Vatican I; Dei Verbum 9–10)",st:"ST I q.1 a.8 ad 2; II-II q.1 a.10; Dei Verbum 9–10",
 q:"Sola scriptura: Is Scripture the only infallible rule of faith? And who decided the canon?",
 one:"Catholics hold that the Word of God comes to us in Scripture and Tradition together, interpreted authoritatively by the Magisterium; Scripture itself does not teach that it alone is the rule, it does not list its own books, and the Church recognised the canon in councils; Thomas indeed calls Scripture the 'incontrovertible proof' of theology, but he reads it within the faith of the Church.",
 plain:`<p><strong>Sola scriptura</strong> (the Reformation principle): Scripture is the only infallible rule of faith; creeds and councils are subordinate authorities to be tested by it. It is not 'Scripture without tradition' in the mainstream Reformers, but Scripture as the sole infallible norm.</p>
<p><strong>The Catholic response.</strong></p>
<ol><li><strong>Scripture points to tradition.</strong> 'Hold the traditions, which you have learned, whether by word, or by our epistle' (2 Thess 2:15). The Church is 'the pillar and ground of the truth' (1 Tim 3:15).</li>
<li><strong>The canon problem.</strong> No book of the Bible lists the books of the Bible. The canon was recognised by the Church: the Council of Rome (382, under Damasus), Hippo (393) and Carthage (397, 419) listed the 73 books; Florence (1442) and Trent (1546) defined them. Luther moved the deuterocanonical books (Tobit, Judith, 1–2 Maccabees, Wisdom, Sirach, Baruch, parts of Esther and Daniel) to an appendix.</li>
<li><strong>Interpretation.</strong> Scripture must be interpreted; disagreements among Bible-alone Christians (on baptism, the Eucharist) show the need for a living authority. 'The task of authentically interpreting the word of God… has been entrusted exclusively to the living teaching office of the Church' (Dei Verbum 10), which 'is not above the word of God, but serves it'.</li></ol>
<p><strong>Thomas's text.</strong> Protestants sometimes quote Thomas: sacred doctrine 'properly uses the authority of the canonical Scriptures as an incontrovertible proof, and the authority of the doctors of the Church as one that may properly be used, yet merely as probable' (I q.1 a.8 ad 2). But 'doctors' here are individual theologians, not councils or popes; Thomas elsewhere assigns to the Sovereign Pontiff the authority 'to decide matters of faith finally, so that they may be held by all with unshaken faith' (II-II q.1 a.10).</p>`,
 analogy:["The constitution and the court","A nation's constitution is the supreme law, but it does not interpret itself; a constitutional court does, and it is bound by the text. Scripture is like the constitution; the Magisterium like the court; Tradition like the unwritten conventions handed on from the founders."],
 deeper:`<p><strong>Material and formal sufficiency.</strong> Catholic theologians debate whether all revealed truth is at least implicitly in Scripture (material sufficiency, held by many after Vatican II, e.g. Congar, and in some sense by Thomas) or some truths come by tradition alone (the 'two-source' reading of Trent). Vatican II deliberately left this open: Scripture and Tradition 'flow from the same divine wellspring' (DV 9). Either way, Scripture is not formally sufficient: it needs the Church to interpret it.</p>
<p><strong>The deuterocanonicals.</strong> The New Testament often follows the Septuagint, which contained them; Hebrews 11:35 alludes to 2 Maccabees 7; the early Church read them liturgically. Jerome doubted them but translated them in submission to the Church.</p>`,
 authors:[["Christian B. Wagner","thomism.com: 'A Complete Refutation of Sola Scriptura'; 'Sola Scriptura's Black Box'; 'The Catholic Canon Vindicated'","Argues that sola scriptura cannot establish the canon without an extra-biblical authority (the 'black box'), and defends the 73-book canon historically."]],
 errors:[["The Catholic Church added books to the Bible at Trent.","The deuterocanonical books were in the canon lists of Rome, Hippo, Carthage and Florence centuries before Trent."]],
 scripture:[["2 Thess 2:15","Traditions by word and letter."],["1 Tim 3:15","The Church as pillar of truth."],["2 Tim 3:16","'All scripture… profitable': the key Protestant text; says profitable, not sufficient alone."],["Acts 15:28","The Council of Jerusalem decides: 'it hath seemed good to the Holy Ghost and to us'."]],
 tests:[["Which councils listed the 73 books before Trent?","Rome (382), Hippo (393), Carthage (397), Florence (1442)."],["What does Thomas mean by 'doctors' in I q.1 a.8 ad 2?","Individual theologians, whose authority is probable, not the Magisterium."]],
 read:["Dei Verbum 7–10","Trent, Session 4","ST I q.1 a.8","<a href=\"#dq-church-magisterium\">The Magisterium and levels of assent</a>","<a href=\"#dq-church-development\">Development of doctrine</a>"]},

{id:"controversy-papacy",tract:"controversy",level:2,note:"Papal primacy of jurisdiction and infallibility: de fide (Vatican I, Pastor Aeternus, 1870)",st:"ST II-II q.1 a.10; Summa contra Gentiles IV c.76",
 q:"The papacy from Scripture: Is the primacy of Peter and his successors biblical? What about Constance and the Orthodox objection?",
 one:"Catholics read Matthew 16:18–19 (the rock and the keys) against Isaiah 22:22 (the steward of David's house holding the key), Luke 22:32 and John 21:15–17 as establishing Peter as Christ's chief steward, an office passed on in the Church of Rome; Vatican I defined papal primacy and infallibility; the objections from the Council of Constance, from Pope Honorius, and from the Orthodox 'primacy of honour' each have answers.",
 plain:`<p><strong>The texts.</strong></p>
<ol><li><strong>Matthew 16:18–19.</strong> 'Thou art Peter (Petros); and upon this rock (petra) I will build my church… I will give to thee the keys of the kingdom of heaven. And whatsoever thou shalt bind upon earth, it shall be bound also in heaven.' In Aramaic both words are <em>Kepha</em> (Jn 1:42: 'Cephas').</li>
<li><strong>Isaiah 22:22: the typology of the keys.</strong> God replaces the steward Shebna with Eliakim: 'I will lay the key of the house of David upon his shoulder: and he shall open, and none shall shut'. The royal steward was an office with successors. Jesus, the Son of David, gives the keys to Peter.</li>
<li><strong>Luke 22:31–32.</strong> 'Simon… I have prayed for thee, that thy faith fail not: and thou, being once converted, confirm thy brethren.'</li>
<li><strong>John 21:15–17.</strong> 'Feed my lambs… feed my sheep.'</li></ol>
<p><strong>Thomas</strong> argues from the unity of the Church: the whole Church must be one people under one head, so that the faith may be kept one, and so Christ left Peter's successor as head (Summa contra Gentiles IV c.76). In II-II q.1 a.10 the publication of a new edition of the creed 'belongs to the authority of the Sovereign Pontiff… so that they may be held by all with unshaken faith'.</p>
<p><strong>Vatican I</strong> (Pastor Aeternus, 1870) defined that the Roman Pontiff has full, supreme, ordinary and immediate jurisdiction over the whole Church, and that when he speaks <em>ex cathedra</em> he possesses the infallibility Christ willed for his Church.</p>`,
 analogy:["The prime minister of the palace","In ancient Judah the king's steward was 'over the house', wore the key on his shoulder, and could be replaced by a successor. The king remained king. So with Christ and Peter: Christ is King and head of the Church; the Pope is the steward, the 'Vicar', who acts with delegated authority."],
 deeper:`<p><strong>Objection 1: the Council of Constance (1415).</strong> The decree <em>Haec Sancta</em> claimed that a general council has authority directly from Christ which even a pope must obey. Answer: Constance was convened to end the Western Schism (three rival claimants); <em>Haec Sancta</em> was passed before the council had a certain pope, and Martin V's later approval of the council's decrees was limited to those on faith passed 'conciliariter'; it was never ratified as dogma, and Florence (1439) and Vatican I taught papal primacy.</p>
<p><strong>Objection 2: Pope Honorius (d. 638).</strong> Condemned by the Third Council of Constantinople (681) for favouring Monothelitism in letters to Sergius. Answer: his letters were not ex cathedra definitions; Pope Leo II confirmed the condemnation for negligence in suppressing heresy. This shows a pope can err outside the conditions Vatican I set, which the Church admits (see papal error).</p>
<p><strong>Objection 3: the Orthodox.</strong> Many Orthodox grant Rome a 'primacy of honour' (first among equals) but not jurisdiction. Catholics point to early exercises of Roman authority: Clement of Rome's letter to Corinth (c. 96), Irenaeus on Rome's 'preeminent authority' (Against Heresies III.3.2, c. 180), Leo the Great's Tome accepted at Chalcedon (451: 'Peter has spoken through Leo'). John Paul II invited dialogue on how primacy is exercised (Ut Unum Sint 95, 1995).</p>
<p><strong>The 'forgeries' objection.</strong> The Pseudo-Isidorian Decretals (9th century) were forged documents that strengthened papal claims. Catholics answer that papal primacy is attested centuries earlier (above) and that Vatican I does not rest on them.</p>`,
 authors:[["Christian B. Wagner","thomism.com: 'The Eastern Orthodox \"Forgeries\" Lie'; posts on Constance and the papacy","Defends papal primacy against Orthodox historical objections."],["Edward Feser","Blog: posts on the limits of papal authority","Explains that the Pope's authority is to guard and interpret the deposit, not to change it (Pastor Aeternus: 'not… that they might make known new doctrine')."]],
 errors:[["Papal infallibility means the Pope cannot sin or is always right.","It concerns only ex cathedra definitions of faith and morals."]],
 scripture:[["Mt 16:18–19","Rock and keys."],["Isa 22:20–22","The steward's key."],["Lk 22:32","'Confirm thy brethren'."],["Jn 21:15–17","'Feed my sheep'."]],
 tests:[["What OT text explains the 'keys'?","Isaiah 22:22: the royal steward of David's house."],["Was Honorius's error ex cathedra?","No; his letters were not solemn definitions."]],
 read:["Vatican I, Pastor Aeternus","Ut Unum Sint 88–96","<a href=\"#dq-church-infallibility\">Papal infallibility</a>","<a href=\"#dq-church-papal-error\">Can a pope err?</a>","<a href=\"#dq-church-schism\">Schism</a>"]},

{id:"controversy-icons",tract:"controversy",level:2,note:"Veneration of images: de fide (Nicaea II, 787; Trent Session 25)",st:"ST III q.25 a.3",
 q:"Icons and statues: Does venerating images break the commandment against graven images?",
 one:"No: God himself commanded images in worship (the cherubim on the Ark, the bronze serpent), and since the invisible God became visible in Christ he can be depicted; the honour given to an image passes to the person it represents; Nicaea II (787) defined this against the iconoclasts.",
 plain:`<p>The commandment: 'Thou shalt not make to thyself a graven thing… Thou shalt not adore them, nor serve them' (Ex 20:4–5). Yet God commands two golden cherubim over the Ark (Ex 25:18) and the bronze serpent (Num 21:8–9; cf. Jn 3:14). Thomas: 'This commandment does not forbid the making of any graven thing or likeness, but the making thereof for the purpose of adoration' (III q.25 a.3 ad 1), that is, as gods.</p>
<p><strong>The principle of the image.</strong> Thomas, following Aristotle: there are two movements of the mind toward an image. One toward it as a thing (wood, paint), which deserves no reverence; the other toward it 'in so far as it is the image of something else', which is 'one and the same as that which is towards the thing' (III q.25 a.3). So the honour passes to the person shown. St Basil: 'the honour paid to the image passes to the prototype', quoted by Nicaea II.</p>
<p><strong>The Incarnation.</strong> St John Damascene: 'In former times God… was never depicted. Now… God has appeared in the flesh… I make an image of the God whom I see' (On the Divine Images I.16). Christ is 'the image of the invisible God' (Col 1:15).</p>`,
 analogy:["The photo of your mother","If you kiss a photograph of your late mother, you are not loving paper; the love goes to her. If someone tore it up in contempt, you would rightly feel she had been insulted."],
 deeper:`<p><strong>Latria and 'relative' latria.</strong> Nicaea II distinguished 'true latria' (adoration due to God alone) from the 'honourable veneration' (timetike proskynesis) given to images. Thomas says Christ's image is adored with latria (III q.25 a.3). These agree: Thomas means the latria directed to Christ himself passes through the image <em>as image</em> (relative), not to the wood; Nicaea II denied absolute latria to the image as a thing. Images of saints receive relative dulia; of Mary, relative hyperdulia.</p>
<p><strong>Iconoclasm</strong> (726–843) was promoted by Byzantine emperors; its final defeat in 843 is celebrated in the East as the 'Triumph of Orthodoxy'. Calvin and the Reformed revived iconoclastic arguments; Lutherans generally kept images.</p>`,
 errors:[["Catholics worship statues.","The honour passes to the person represented; adoration in the absolute sense is for God alone (CCC 2132)."]],
 scripture:[["Ex 25:18–22","Cherubim on the Ark."],["Num 21:8–9","The bronze serpent; destroyed later when it was worshipped as a god (2 Kgs 18:4)."],["Col 1:15","Christ the image of the invisible God."]],
 tests:[["What are the two movements toward an image, for Thomas?","Toward it as a thing, and toward it as an image of another; the second is one with the movement toward the person."],["What did Nicaea II define?","The legitimacy of venerating images, with honour passing to the prototype."]],
 read:["ST III q.25 aa.3–6","Nicaea II (787)","John Damascene, On the Divine Images","CCC 1159–1162, 2129–2132"]}
);

